window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"id": "l10", "title": "Lecture 10: Physiology of the Autonomic Nervous System", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l10 Lecture 10 Physiology of the Autonomic Nervous System n n Lecturer V. Grubisic M.D. Ph.D. n Session Lecture 10 n n 1. ANS Organization Slide 2-16 n nThe Autonomic Nervous System ANS maintains homeostasis through two opposing divisions Sympathetic Fight or Flight and Parasympathetic Rest and Digest . Both systems utilize a two-neuron chain preganglionic and postganglionic . n n Sympathetic Division n Origin Thoracolumbar T1-L2 lateral horn. n Neuron Lengths Short Preganglionic Long Postganglionic. n Neurotransmitters n Preganglionic Acetylcholine Nicotinic receptors . n Postganglionic Norepinephrine Adrenergic receptors mostly. n Exceptions Sweat glands ACh/Muscarinic and Adrenal Medulla Epi/NE directly into blood . n n Parasympathetic Division n Origin Craniosacral CN III VII IX X and S2-S4 . n Neuron Lengths Long Preganglionic Short Postganglionic ganglia near target . n Neurotransmitters n Preganglionic Acetylcholine Nicotinic . n Postganglionic Acetylcholine Muscarinic . n n 2. Clinical Correlates of ANS Dysfunction n n Horner s Syndrome Slide 14 nLesion of the sympathetic pathway to the head/neck. n Triad Ptosis droopy eyelid Miosis constricted pupil Anhidrosis lack of sweating . n n Autonomic Dysreflexia Slide 28 n Cause Spinal Cord Injury above T6 . n Mechanism A noxious stimulus e.g. full bladder triggers a massive sympathetic surge below the lesion. n Consequence Widespread vasoconstriction causes life-threatening Hypertension . n Reflex Baroreceptors detect high BP $ to$ Vagus nerve slows heart $ to$ Bradycardia . n Result Hypertension + Bradycardia. n n Bladder Dysfunction Slide 31-32 n Spastic UMN Bladder Lesion above Pontine Micturition Center PMC but below cortex or between PMC and cord. Reflex is hyperactive. Detrusor contracts against closed sphincter. n Atonic LMN Bladder Lesion of Sacral cord or Cauda Equina. Reflex arc destroyed. Bladder fills but cannot contract overflow incontinence . n n 3. Enteric Nervous System ENS Slide 33-37 n nThe Brain of the Gut capable of functioning independently. n Plexuses Myenteric Auerbach s - Motility and Submucosal Meissner s - Secretion . n n correlate n Hirschsprung s Disease n Pathophysiology Congenital failure of Neural Crest Cells to migrate to the distal colon. n Presentation Newborns fail to pass meconium. n Result Aganglionic Megacolon . The affected segment lacks ENS plexuses and remains constantly constricted cannot relax causing obstruction and dilation upstream. n Treatment Surgical resection of the aganglionic segment. n n n 4. Cardiovascular Reflexes Slide 27 n n Carotid Sinus Massage Mechanically stimulates baroreceptors $ to$ mimics high BP $ to$ increases Vagal tone $ to$ slows Heart Rate. Used to terminate SVT. n highyield n Contraindication Do not perform Carotid Massage in patients with carotid stenosis or recent stroke as it may dislodge an embolus. n Lecture 10 Physiology of the Autonomic Nervous System Neuroscience questions A patient with a T4 spinal cord injury develops a pounding headache and profuse sweating. Their blood pressure is 210/110 mmHg and heart rate is 50 bpm. What is the most likely physiological mechanism? Hypovolemic Shock Autonomic Dysreflexia Septic Shock Orthostatic Hypotension Vasovagal Syncope 1 This is classic Autonomic Dysreflexia a noxious stimulus triggers a sympathetic surge below the lesion Hypertension which triggers a baroreceptor reflex to slow the heart Bradycardia . Which neurotransmitter is released by sympathetic postganglionic neurons innervating sweat glands? Norepinephrine Epinephrine Acetylcholine Dopamine Serotonin 2 Sweat glands are the exception to the rule. Sympathetic postganglionic neurons innervating them release Acetylcholine acting on Muscarinic receptors. A newborn fails to pass meconium within 48 hours. Imaging reveals a dilated colon proximal to a narrowed distal segment. Biopsy of the narrowed segment would likely show Hypertrophy of the muscularis externa Absence of Myenteric and Submucosal ganglia Inflammation of the mucosa Excessive Neural Crest Cell migration Presence of Lewy Bodies 1 Hirschsprung s Disease is characterized by the absence of ganglia aganglionosis in the distal colon due to failed neural crest migration. Horner s Syndrome is characterized by which constellation of signs? Mydriasis Ptosis Anhidrosis Miosis Exophthalmos Hyperhidrosis Miosis Ptosis Anhidrosis Mydriasis Exophthalmos Anhidrosis Ptosis Miosis Hyperhidrosis 2 Horner s Syndrome presents with Miosis constriction Ptosis drooping and Anhidrosis dryness on the ipsilateral side. Where are the cell bodies of preganglionic sympathetic neurons located? Brainstem Nuclei Sacral Spinal Cord S2-S4 Thoracolumbar Lateral Horn T1-L2 Dorsal Root Ganglia Paravertebral Ganglia 2 The sympathetic division is Thoracolumbar meaning preganglionic neurons originate in the lateral horn of the spinal cord from T1 to L2. Location of Sympathetic Preganglionic Neurons Thoracolumbar Lateral Horn T1-L2 Anatomy Location of Parasympathetic Preganglionic Neurons Craniosacral Brainstem + S2-S4 Anatomy Neurotransmitter of ALL Preganglionic Neurons Acetylcholine Nicotinic Receptor Physiology Neurotransmitter of Sympathetic Postganglionic Neurons Target Norepinephrine Adrenergic Receptor Physiology Neurotransmitter of Parasympathetic Postganglionic Neurons Target Acetylcholine Muscarinic Receptor Physiology Triad of Horner s Syndrome Ptosis Miosis Anhidrosis Pathology Condition causing Aganglionic Megacolon Hirschsprung s Disease Pathology Signs of Autonomic Dysreflexia Hypertension + Bradycardia + Headache/Sweating Pathology Spinal cord level risk for Autonomic Dysreflexia Above T6 Pathology Bladder type in Lower Motor Neuron Sacral lesion Atonic Flaccid Bladder Pathology BandB Neurology > Autonomic Nervous System > ANS Pharmacology FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Autonomic Nervous System Sympathetic vs. Parasympathetic Lengths Sympathetic Short Pre Long Post. n Parasympathetic Long Pre Short Post. Horner s Triad Ptosis Muller s muscle paralysis Miosis Dilator pupillae paralysis Anhidrosis Sudomotor loss . Autonomic Dysreflexia Triad In SCI > T6 Hypertension Sympathetic surge + Bradycardia Baroreceptor reflex + Headache . Hirschsprung s Mechanism Failed migration of Neural Crest Cells . Result Aganglionic segment that cannot relax constricted causing upstream dilation megacolon . Sympathetic Exceptions Most sympathetic postganglionics release NE. Exceptions Sweat glands ACh and Adrenal Medulla Epi/NE directly ."}, {"id": "l11", "title": "Lecture 11: Hypothalamus", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l11 Lecture 11 Hypothalamus n n Lecturer V. Grubisic M.D. Ph.D. n Session Lecture 11 n n 1. Hypothalamus Overview Slide 6-8 n nThe Hypothalamus is the master control center for Homeostasis regulating the Autonomic Nervous System Endocrine System and Limbic System. nMnemonic for Functions TAN HATS n T hirst and Water Balance n A denohypophysis Control n N eurohypophysis Control n H unger n A utonomic Regulation n T emperature Regulation n S exual urges / S leep n n 2. Major Nuclei and Functions Slide 9-16 n n Paraventricular Nucleus PVN Produces Oxytocin ADH TRH CRH. Projects to autonomic centers. n Supraoptic Nucleus SON Produces mainly ADH Vasopressin and Oxytocin. n Suprachiasmatic Nucleus SCN Master Circadian Clock . Regulated by light input from retina. n Anterior Hypothalamus Cooling Parasympathetic . Lesion $ to$ Hyperthermia. A/C = Anterior Cooling . n Posterior Hypothalamus Heating Sympathetic . Lesion $ to$ Hypothermia Poikilothermia . n Mammillary Bodies Episodic Memory. Input from Hippocampus Fornix . n n correlate n Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome n Cause Thiamine Vitamin B1 deficiency often due to chronic alcoholism. n Pathology Degeneration of the Mammillary Bodies and Medial Dorsal Thalamus. n Triad Confusion Ataxia Ophthalmoplegia Wernicke s Encephalopathy . n Sequelae Korsakoff s Psychosis Anterograde/Retrograde Amnesia + Confabulation if untreated. n n n 3. Feeding Regulation Slide 19-22 n n Lateral Hypothalamus Hunger Center . Lesion $ to$ Anorexia Lateral makes you Lean . n Ventromedial Nucleus Satiety Center . Lesion $ to$ Hyperphagia and Obesity Ventromedial makes you Very Massive . n Hormones n Leptin From adipose tissue. Signals satiety inhibits Lateral stimulates Ventromedial . n Ghrelin From stomach. Signals hunger. n n 4. Water Balance Disorders Slide 12 n n Diabetes Insipidus DI n Neurogenic Lack of ADH secretion Hypothalamic/Posterior Pituitary damage . n Sxs Polyuria Polydipsia Dilute Urine. n SIADH Syndrome of Inappropriate ADH n Cause Ectopic production Small Cell Lung Cancer CNS disorders Drugs. n Sxs Water retention Hyponatremia Cerebral Edema. n n highyield n Mnemonic Feeding Centers n L ateral = L ean Hunger Center; lesion causes starvation . n V entromedial = V ery M assive Satiety Center; lesion causes obesity . n Lecture 11 Hypothalamus Neuroscience questions A chronic alcoholic presents with confusion ataxia and nystagmus. MRI reveals atrophy of the Mammillary Bodies. What is the most likely diagnosis? Alzheimer s Disease Parkinson s Disease Wernicke s Encephalopathy Huntington s Disease Vascular Dementia 2 This is the classic presentation of Wernicke s Encephalopathy due to Thiamine deficiency which damages the mammillary bodies. A patient develops hyperphagia and significant obesity after a brain tumor resection. Which hypothalamic nucleus was most likely damaged? Lateral Hypothalamus Supraoptic Nucleus Ventromedial Nucleus Suprachiasmatic Nucleus Anterior Hypothalamus 2 The Ventromedial Nucleus is the Satiety Center. Damage leads to unchecked hunger and obesity Very Massive . Which hypothalamic nucleus is responsible for circadian rhythms? Arcuate Nucleus Suprachiasmatic Nucleus Paraventricular Nucleus Posterior Hypothalamus Mammillary Nucleus 1 The Suprachiasmatic Nucleus SCN regulates sleep-wake cycles and circadian rhythms. Damage to the Anterior Hypothalamus would result in Hypothermia Hyperthermia Diabetes Insipidus Amnesia Insomnia 1 The Anterior Hypothalamus regulates Cooling . Damage prevents cooling resulting in Hyperthermia . In neurogenic Diabetes Insipidus there is a lack of which hormone? Oxytocin Prolactin ADH Vasopressin TSH GH 2 Neurogenic Diabetes Insipidus causes polyuria due to a failure to secrete ADH Vasopressin from the posterior pituitary/hypothalamus. Function of Lateral Hypothalamus Hunger Feeding Center Physiology Function of Ventromedial Hypothalamus Satiety Physiology Function of Anterior Hypothalamus Cooling Parasympathetic Physiology Function of Posterior Hypothalamus Heating Sympathetic/Shivering Physiology Function of Suprachiasmatic Nucleus SCN Circadian Rhythms Physiology Function of Mammillary Bodies Episodic Memory Papez Circuit Physiology Lesion of Mammillary Bodies causes Wernicke-Korsakoff Syndrome Amnesia/Confabulation Pathology Lesion of Lateral Hypothalamus causes Anorexia Failure to eat Pathology Lesion of Ventromedial Hypothalamus causes Hyperphagia Obesity Pathology Major hormones of Posterior Pituitary ADH Vasopressin and Oxytocin Physiology BandB Endermo > Hypothalamus FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Hypothalamus Pathoma CNS Hypothalamic Feeding Centers Lateral Hypothalamus LH Hunger Center. Lesion = Starvation Lateral makes you Lean . n Ventromedial Nucleus VMN Satiety Center. Lesion = Obesity VMN makes you Very Massive . Temperature Regulation Anterior Hypothalamus Cooling Parasympathetic . Lesion = Hyperthermia. n Posterior Hypothalamus Heating Sympathetic . Lesion = Hypothermia. Wernicke-Korsakoff Anatomy Caused by Thiamine B1 deficiency leading to necrosis of the Mammillary Bodies . Circadian Rhythm Controlled by the Suprachiasmatic Nucleus SCN which receives direct input from the retina. ADH Production ADH is synthesized in the Supraoptic and Paraventricular nuclei and released from the Posterior Pituitary ."}, {"id": "l12", "title": "Lecture 12: Anatomy and Physiology of the Thalamus", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l12 Lecture 12 Anatomy and Physiology of the Thalamus n n Lecturer J. Xie Ph.D. n Session Lecture 12 n n 1. Thalamic Organization Slide 12-18 n nThe Thalamus is the Gateway to the Cortex . Most sensory information except olfaction must synapse here before reaching the cortex. n n Relay Nuclei Specific n VPL Ventral Posterolateral n Input DCML and Spinothalamic Tract Body . n Output Somatosensory Cortex Postcentral Gyrus . n Function Vibration Proprioception Pain Temp from Body . n VPM Ventral Posteromedial n Input Trigeminal Face and Gustatory Solitary . n Output Somatosensory Cortex. n Function Sensation from Face + Taste . n LGN Lateral Geniculate Nucleus n Input Optic Tract. n Output Visual Cortex V1 . n Function Vision L ight . n MGN Medial Geniculate Nucleus n Input Inferior Colliculus. n Output Auditory Cortex. n Function Hearing M usic . n VA/VL Ventral Anterior/Lateral n Input Basal Ganglia and Cerebellum. n Output Motor Cortex. n Function Motor planning and coordination. n Anterior Nucleus n Input Mammillothalamic Tract. n Output Cingulate Gyrus. n Function Memory and Emotion Papez Circuit . n n 2. Thalamocortical States Slide 23-26 n Tonic Mode Linear transmission. High fidelity. Awake state depolarized by ARAS NE ACh 5-HT Histamine . n Burst Mode Oscillatory. Low fidelity. Sleep state hyperpolarized . Blocks sensory input preventing waking. Absence Seizures occur in this mode. n n 3. Internal Capsule Slide 35-39 nWhite matter highway carrying fibers to/from cortex. n Anterior Limb Thalamocortical fibers Anterior/DM . n Genu Corticobulbar Tract Motor to face/brainstem . n Posterior Limb n Corticospinal Tract Motor to body . n Somatosensory Radiations VPL/VPM to Cortex . n Visual/Auditory Radiations Retrolenticular/Sublenticular . n Blood Supply Primarily MCA Lenticulostriate and Anterior Choroidal . Thalamus is PCA . n n 4. Clinical Syndromes Slide 29-32 n n Dejerine-Roussy Syndrome Thalamic Pain Syndrome n Cause Stroke PCA damaging VPL/VPM. n Presentation Contralateral hemianesthesia followed by agonizing burning pain Allodynia induced by touch or even emotion. n n Artery of Percheron Infarct n Variant Single artery arising from PCA supplying Bilateral paramedian thalamus + midbrain. n Stroke Bilateral thalamic infarct. n Presentation Coma RAS damage Memory loss Vertical gaze palsy. Lecture 12 Anatomy and Physiology of the Thalamus Neuroscience questions A patient suffers a stroke and develops severe burning pain on the right side of their body that is exacerbated by light touch. Which nucleus was most likely infarcted? Right VPL Left VPL Right VPM Left MGN Left Anterior Nucleus 1 Thalamic Pain Syndrome Dejerine-Roussy is contralateral. A Left VPL infarct affects the Right side of the body. VPL handles body sensation. Visual information from the optic tract synapses in which thalamic nucleus? Medial Geniculate Nucleus Lateral Geniculate Nucleus Ventral Posterolateral Nucleus Pulvinar Ventral Anterior Nucleus 1 LGN = L ight Vision . MGN = Music Hearing . Which limb of the internal capsule contains the corticospinal tract? Anterior Limb Genu Posterior Limb Retrolenticular Part Sublenticular Part 2 The Posterior Limb of the Internal Capsule carries the Corticospinal tract Motor to body and Somatosensory radiations. Which thalamic nucleus is part of the Papez circuit involved in memory? Ventral Lateral Anterior Nucleus Pulvinar Lateral Posterior Centromedian 1 The Anterior Nucleus receives input from the Mammillary Bodies and projects to the Cingulate Gyrus forming part of the Papez circuit for memory/emotion. The Burst Mode of thalamocortical firing is associated with Focused attention REM Sleep Slow Wave Sleep Panic attacks Active learning 2 Burst Mode is an oscillatory state that disconnects the cortex from sensory input characteristic of Slow Wave Sleep and Absence Seizures . Input to VPL Nucleus Spinothalamic & DCML Body Sensation Neuroanatomy Input to VPM Nucleus Trigeminal & Solitary Tract Face & Taste Neuroanatomy Input to LGN Optic Tract Vision Neuroanatomy Input to MGN Inferior Colliculus Hearing Neuroanatomy Input to VA/VL Nuclei Basal Ganglia & Cerebellum Motor Neuroanatomy Contents of Internal Capsule Genu Corticobulbar Tract Neuroanatomy Contents of Internal Capsule Posterior Limb Corticospinal Tract & Sensory Radiations Neuroanatomy Artery supplying the Thalamus Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Neuroanatomy Cause of Thalamic Pain Syndrome Stroke in VPL/VPM Dejerine-Roussy Pathology Thalamic mode during Deep Sleep Burst Mode Physiology BandB Neurology > Brainstem > Thalamus FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Thalamus Pathoma CNS VPM vs VPL Mnemonic VPM = M akeup on the Face Face sensation + Taste . n VPL = L egs/Body Body sensation . Geniculate Nuclei Mnemonic L GN = L ight Visual . n M GN = M usic Auditory . Dejerine-Roussy Syndrome Central Post-Stroke Pain. Thalamic lesion causes initial numbness followed by hypersensitivity and severe burning pain on the contralateral side. Internal Capsule Genu The Genu knee contains the Corticobulbar tract Face motor . A lesion here affects CN motor nuclei e.g. UMN facial droop . Thalamic Blood Supply Principally the Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA . The Artery of Percheron is a rare variant supplying bilateral thalami."}, {"id": "l13", "title": "Lecture 13: Clinical Decision Making", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l13 Lecture 13 Clinical Decision Making n n Lecturer R. Morgan D.O. n Session Lecture 13 n n 1. Dual Process Theory Slide 4-5 n nClinical reasoning involves two cognitive systems n System 1 Intuitive Fast automatic reflexive pattern recognition. Used by experts for common cases. Prone to Heuristic Biases . n System 2 Hypothetico-Deductive Slow analytical deliberate. Used for complex or unclear cases. Less prone to bias but resource-intensive. n n 2. Cognitive Biases Heuristics Slide 5 26-28 n n Anchoring Locking onto salient features in the patient s initial presentation too early and failing to adjust. n Availability Bias Judging a diagnosis as more likely because it is easily recalled e.g. you just saw 3 cases of Appendicitis so the next belly pain must be Appendicitis . n Confirmation Bias Seeking evidence to support your hypothesis while ignoring evidence that refutes it. n Premature Closure Stopping the diagnostic process before verifying the diagnosis or considering other possibilities. When the diagnosis is made the thinking stops. n n 3. The Diagnostic Framework n n VINDICATE Mnemonic Slide 15 nA checklist to generate a broad Differential Diagnosis DDx . n V ascular n I nfectious n N eoplastic n D rug / Degenerative n I nflammatory / Idiopathic n C ongenital n A utoimmune / Allergic n T rauma / Toxic n E ndocrine / Metabolic n n Illness Scripts Slide 18 22 nAn organized mental summary of a disease including n Epidemiology/Pathophysiology Who gets it? Why? n Time Course Acute Subacute Chronic? n Salient Symptoms/Signs Key features Prototypical presentation . n Diagnostics/Treatment Labs Imaging Meds. n n 4. Problem Representation Slide 12 35 nA one-sentence summary of the case used to communicate and reason. n Structure Patient Age/Sex/Risk Factors presents with Time Course Chief Complaint characterized by Key Features . n Example A 3yo male with no PMH presents with 2 days of fever and ear pulling found to have a bulging erythematous TM. n n correlate n Case Study Acute Otitis Media AOM n Presentation Fever Ear Pain Otalgia Bulging Tympanic Membrane TM Loss of benchmarks. n Pathogens Strep pneumoniae Haemophilus influenzae Moraxella catarrhalis . n DDx n Serous Otitis Media Fluid effusion but no active infection/bulging. n Otitis Externa Swimmer s ear. Canal inflammation. Pain with tragal motion. flesh-colored canal makes this unlikely. n Lecture 13 Clinical Decision Making Neuroscience questions A resident diagnoses a patient with Influenza because everyone coming into the ER today has the flu despite the patient having neck stiffness and a rash. Which cognitive bias is at play? Anchoring Availability Bias Confirmation Bias Premature Closure Sunk Cost Fallacy 1 Availability Bias is judging frequency or probability by how easily examples come to mind e.g. a recent run of flu cases . When generating a differential diagnosis for a 60-year-old male with back pain you use the mnemonic VINDICATE. The N stands for Neurologic Nutritional Neoplastic Nociceptive Neonatal 2 N in VINDICATE stands for Neoplastic Cancer a critical category to consider in older adults with new back pain. Which of the following best describes System 2 thinking? Fast and reflexive Pattern recognition Slow and analytical Prone to emotional influence Automatic association 2 System 2 is the slow deliberate analytical mode of thinking used for complex problems. A 3-year-old presents with ear pain. Exam reveals a bulging erythematous tympanic membrane with loss of bony landmarks. The most likely diagnosis is Otitis Externa Serous Otitis Media Mastoiditis Acute Otitis Media Suppurative Foreign Body 3 Bulging and erythema of the TM are hallmark signs of Acute Suppurative Otitis Media. You suspect a patient has a Pulmonary Embolism. You order a D-dimer which is negative but you dismiss the result as a lab error because you are so sure of your diagnosis. This is an example of Confirmation Bias Anchoring Availability Bias Visceral Bias Gambler s Fallacy 1 Confirmation Bias involves interpreting evidence to support your existing beliefs the PE diagnosis while dismissing contradictory evidence the negative D-dimer . System 1 Thinking Fast Intuitive Automatic Clinical Reasoning System 2 Thinking Slow Analytical Deliberate Clinical Reasoning Availability Bias Diagnosing based on what is readily recalled recent cases Clinical Reasoning Anchoring Bias Sticking to initial impressions despite new data Clinical Reasoning Premature Closure Stopping the diagnostic process too early Clinical Reasoning VINDICATE V Vascular Clinical Reasoning VINDICATE I first Infectious Clinical Reasoning VINDICATE N Neoplastic Clinical Reasoning Illness Script Components Pathophys Epidemiology Time Course Symptoms Diagnostics Treatment Clinical Reasoning Key sign of Acute Otitis Media Bulging Tympanic Membrane Clinical Reasoning BandB Infectious Disease > HEENT FirstAid Public Health > Epidemiology > Bias Pathoma General Principles System 1 vs System 2 System 1 Fast Intuitive Gut feeling . Prone to bias. n System 2 Slow Analytical Math problem . More accurate. VINDICATE Mnemonic Vascular Infectious Neoplastic Drug/Degenerative Inflammatory/Idiopathic Congenital Autoimmune Trauma/Toxic Endocrine/Metabolic. Confirmation Bias The tendency to look only for evidence that confirms your hunch and ignore evidence that contradicts it. Availability Bias Diagnosing something because you recently saw it or it s top of mind e.g. flu during flu season . Premature Closure The most common fatal error Accepting a diagnosis before it is fully verified and stopping the search for alternatives."}, {"id": "l15", "title": "Lecture 15: General Sensory Systems", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l15 Lecture 15 General Sensory Systems n n Lecturer J. Xie Ph.D. n Session Lecture 15 n n 1. Sensory Receptors & Fibers Slide 5-13 n n Fiber Types n A-beta A u03b2 Large Myelinated. Fast 30-70 m/s . Carries Touch Vibration Proprioception . n A-delta A u03b4 Small Myelinated. Medium speed. Carries Sharp Pain First Pain Cold . n C fibers Unmyelinated. Slow 0.5-2 m/s . Carries Dull/Burning Pain Second Pain Warmth Itch . n n Mechanoreceptors Touch n Meissner s Corpuscle Fast adapting Small field. Light Touch/Grip . Glabrous skin. n Merkel Disc Slow adapting Small field. Static Pressure/Texture Braille . n Pacinian Corpuscle Fast adapting Large field. Vibration . n Ruffini Ending Slow adapting Large field. Skin Stretch Grip shape . n n 2. Ascending Pathways Slide 26-30 n n Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus DCML nCarries Fine Touch Vibration Proprioception from the body. n1. 1st Order DRG. Ascend in Dorsal Columns Gracilis=Lower Cuneatus=Upper . n2. 2nd Order Nucleus Gracilis/Cuneatus Medulla . Decussate as Internal Arcuate Fibers to form Medial Lemniscus . n3. 3rd Order VPL Nucleus Thalamus $ to$ Primary Somatosensory Cortex S1 . n n Spinothalamic Tract Anterolateral System nCarries Pain Temperature Crude Touch . n1. 1st Order DRG. Synapse in Dorsal Horn Substantia Gelatinosa . n2. 2nd Order Dorsal Horn. Decussate immediately in Anterior White Commissure. Ascend in anterolateral funiculus. n3. 3rd Order VPL Nucleus Thalamus $ to$ S1 Cortex. n n 3. Trigeminal System Face Slide 31-33 n Touch/Proprioception Trigeminal Ganglion $ to$ Principal Sensory Nucleus Pons $ to$ Decussate $ to$ VPM. n Pain/Temp Trigeminal Ganglion $ to$ Descend to Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus Medulla $ to$ Decussate $ to$ VPM. n Proprioception Jaw Mesencephalic Nucleus Midbrain - UNIQUE Cell bodies inside CNS . n n 4. Clinical Correlates Slide 38-43 n n correlate n Brown-S u00e9quard Syndrome n Pathology Hemisection of the spinal cord e.g. stabbing/trauma . n Presentation n Ipsilateral Loss of Motor Corticospinal and Touch/Vibration/Proprioception DCML below lesion. n Contralateral Loss of Pain/Temperature Spinothalamic 1-2 segments below lesion due to Lissanuer s tract ascent before crossing . n n n correlate n Phantom Limb Pain n Pathology Cortical reorganization after amputation. n Mechanism The cortical area for the amputated limb e.g. arm loses input and is invaded by adjacent areas e.g. Face . Touching the face is perceived as touching the missing arm. n Nature Often burning/painful resistant to meds. n n n correlate n Thalamic Lesion Dejerine-Roussy n Lesion VPL/VPM. n Presentation Contralateral Hemianesthesia loss of all sensation . Later develops into severe burning pain Thalamic Pain Syndrome . n n n 5. Pain Modulation Slide 18-20 n Gate Control Theory A u03b2 fibers rubbing activate inhibitory interneurons in the dorsal horn blocking C fiber pain transmission. n Descending Pathway PAG $ to$ RVM $ to$ Dorsal Horn Inhibits pain via Serotonin/NE . Lecture 15 General Sensory Systems Neuroscience questions A patient cannot feel temperature or sharp pain in their right leg but vibration sense is intact. Where is the lesion most likely located? Right Fasciculus Gracilis Left Spinothalamic Tract Right Spinothalamic Tract Left Medial Lemniscus Right Dorsal Horn 1 Pain/Temp from the Right leg travels to the left side of the spinal cord immediately. A lesion in the Left Spinothalamic Tract causes loss of pain/temp on the Right side. Which receptor is responsible for sensing vibration? Merkel Disc Meissner Corpuscle Pacinian Corpuscle Ruffini Ending Free Nerve Ending 2 Pacinian Corpuscles are rapidly adapting and specialized for sensing Vibration . A hemisection of the spinal cord at T10 on the left side Brown-S u00e9quard would result in Loss of pain/temp on the left leg Loss of vibration/proprioception on the right leg Loss of motor function on the right leg Loss of pain/temp on the right leg Bilateral loss of all sensation 3 Hemisection on the Left causes Contralateral Right loss of Pain/Temp STT crosses immediately and Ipsilateral Left loss of Touch/Motor. The cell bodies of the primary afferent neurons for the face Trigeminal are located in the Dorsal Root Ganglion Trigeminal Ganglion Principal Sensory Nucleus Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus VPM Nucleus 1 Primary sensory neurons for the face are in the Trigeminal Gasserian Ganglion . Which fibers carry slow burning pain? A-alpha A-beta A-delta B fibers C fibers 4 C fibers are unmyelinated and slow conducting responsible for the delayed dull burning second pain . Pathway for Fine Touch and Proprioception Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus DCML Neuroanatomy Pathway for Pain and Temperature Spinothalamic Tract Anterolateral System Neuroanatomy Where does DCML decussate? Medulla Internal Arcuate Fibers Neuroanatomy Where does Spinothalamic Tract decussate? Spinal Cord Anterior White Commissure Neuroanatomy Function of Pacinian Corpuscle Vibration Physiology Function of A-delta fibers Fast Sharp Pain Cold Physiology Function of C fibers Slow Dull Pain Warmth/Itch Physiology Brown-S u00e9quard Contralateral Deficit Pain and Temperature STT Pathology Brown-S u00e9quard Ipsilateral Deficit Motor CS & Vibration/Proprioception DCML Pathology Thalamic Nucleus for Body Sensation VPL Neuroanatomy BandB Neurology > Spinal Cord > Spinal Cord Tracts FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Spinal Cord Pathoma CNS DCML vs STT Decussation DCML crosses in the Medulla Internal Arcuate Fibers . n Spinothalamic crosses in the Spinal Cord Anterior White Commissure . VPL vs VPM VPL = L egs/Body Body sensation . n VPM = M akeup/Face Face sensation . Fiber Speed Rule A-beta Touch > A-delta Fast Pain > C Slow Pain . Myelination and diameter determine speed. Brown-S u00e9quard Deficits Ipsilateral Motor & Touch. Contralateral Pain & Temp. Phantom Limb Mechanism Maladaptive Cortical Reorganization Neuroplasticity where face area invades hand area."}, {"id": "l16", "title": "Lecture 16: Visual System", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l16 Lecture 16 Visual System n n Lecturer G. Otazu Ph.D. n Session Lecture 16 n n 1. Eye Functional Anatomy Slide 4-10 n nThe eye focuses light onto the retina to transduce it into neural signals. n Refraction Most focusing power comes from the Cornea air-water interface not the lens. n Accommodation The Lens changes shape to focus near objects. n Near Vision Reflex Ciliary muscle Contracts $ to$ Zonules Relax $ to$ Lens becomes Rounder . + Pupil constriction + Convergence . n Far Vision Ciliary muscle Relaxes $ to$ Zonules Tighten $ to$ Lens flattens. n Aqueous Humor Produced by Ciliary Body. Drains via Canal of Schlemm . Blockage = Glaucoma . n n Retina Organization n Fovea Center of Macula. All Cones . Highest acuity. No blood vessels/layers in light path. n Periphery Mostly Rods . High sensitivity low light low acuity. n Blind Spot Optic Disc Where optic nerve exits. No photoreceptors. n n 2. Visual Pathway Slide 26-30 n n1. Retina Light hits Photoreceptors $ to$ Bipolar Cells $ to$ Ganglion Cells. n2. Optic Nerve CN II Ganglion cell axons. n3. Optic Chiasm Nasal fibers temporal field CROSS . Temporal fibers nasal field stay ipsilateral. n4. Optic Tract Contains ipsilateral Temporal fibers + contralateral Nasal fibers. Projects to LGN . n5. Lateral Geniculate Nucleus LGN Thalamic relay. n Magno M layers Motion/Depth. n Parvo P layers Color/Form. n6. Geniculocalcarine Tract Optic Radiations n Meyer s Loop Temporal Lobe Loops around lateral ventricle. Carries Superior Visual Field Pie in the Sky . n Dorsal/Parietal Radiations Carries Inferior Visual Field Pie on the Floor . n7. Primary Visual Cortex V1 Occipital Lobe. Upper bank Cuneus = Inferior Field. Lower bank Lingual = Superior Field. n n 3. Visual Field Lesions Slide 26-29 32 n Monocular Blindness Lesion of Optic Nerve . n Bitemporal Hemianopsia Lesion of Optic Chiasm . n Cause Pituitary Adenoma compressing chiasm centrally. n Effect Loss of peripheral vision Tunnel Vision . n Homonymous Hemianopsia Lesion of Optic Tract or PCA Stroke . n Effect Loss of same field in both eyes e.g. Left field perception lost in both eyes . n Macular Sparing PCA strokes often spare the fovea supplied by MCA collaterals . n Quadrantanopsia n Meyer s Loop Temporal Pie in the Sky Contralateral Superior Quadrant . n Parietal Radiations Pie on the Floor Contralateral Inferior Quadrant . n n correlate n Papilledema Slide 34 n Pathophysiology Increased Intracranial Pressure ICP transmitted through the subarachnoid space surrounding the Optic Nerve. n Sign Swelling of the Optic Disc visible on fundoscopy. Blurred margins. n Context Brain tumor Meningitis Pseudotumor Cerebri. n n n correlate n Hemispatial Neglect Slide 43-44 n Lesion Right Parietal Lobe Non-dominant hemisphere . n Presentation Patient ignores the Left side of the world/body. n Starts eating from only right side of plate. n Shaves only right face. n Draws only right half of clock. n Note This is an Attention deficit not a visual one. n n n mnemonic n Visual Field Defects n Tem poral Loop = Pie in the Sky Temporal lobe lesion $ to$ Superior field defect . n Parietal Path = Pie on the Floor Parietal lobe lesion $ to$ Inferior field defect . n Lecture 16 Visual System Neuroscience questions A patient presents with tunnel vision bumping into objects on their periphery. MRI reveals a mass compressing the optic chiasm. What is the most likely diagnosis? Right Optic Nerve Compression Pituitary Adenoma Temporal Lobe Tumor Parietal Lobe Tumor Occipital Lobe Stroke 1 A Pituitary Adenoma compresses the specialized crossing fibers in the Optic Chiasm causing Bitemporal Hemianopsia Tunnel Vision . A patient cannot see the upper left quadrant of their visual field Pie in the Sky . Where is the lesion located? Right Temporal Lobe Meyer s Loop Left Temporal Lobe Meyer s Loop Right Parietal Lobe Left Parietal Lobe Right Occipital Cortex 0 A Left upper field defect maps to the Right brain Contralateral . In the Sky Superior implies the inferior fibers Meyer s Loop in Temporal Lobe . So Right Temporal Lobe . Accommodation for near vision involves which action of the ciliary muscle? Relaxation Contraction Hyperpolarization Atrophy No change 1 The ciliary muscle Contracts loosening the zonules allowing the lens to spring into a rounder shape for near focus. Papilledema is a clinical sign indicative of Retinal Detachment Glaucoma Increased Intracranial Pressure Cataracts Macular Degeneration 2 Papilledema swollen optic disc is caused by transmitted Increased Intracranial Pressure ICP . A patient with a Right Parietal Lobe stroke only eats food on the right side of their plate and ignores the left side. This is called Homonymous Hemianopsia Agnosia Hemispatial Neglect Apraxia Prosopagnosia 2 Hemispatial Neglect is an attention deficit classically due to a non-dominant Right Parietal Lobe lesion. Visual Deficit Optic Chiasm Lesion Bitemporal Hemianopsia Neurology Visual Deficit Right Optic Tract Lesion Left Homonymous Hemianopsia Neurology Visual Deficit Meyer s Loop Temporal Lesion Contralateral Superior Quadrantanopia Pie in the Sky Neurology Visual Deficit Parietal Radiation Lesion Contralateral Inferior Quadrantanopia Pie on the Floor Neurology Lesion causing Left Hemispatial Neglect Right Parietal Lobe Neurology Sign of Increased ICP on Eye Exam Papilledema Pathology Function of Ciliary Muscle Contraction Accommodation Near Vision Physiology Area of Retina with highest acuity Fovea Macula Physiology Blood Supply to Visual Cortex Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Neuroanatomy What is spared in PCA stroke visual field defect? Macula Macular Sparing Pathology BandB Neurology > The Eye > Visual Fields FirstAid Neurology > Ophthalmology Pathoma CNS Pituitary Adenoma Deficit Compresses Optic Chiasm $ to$ Bitemporal Hemianopsia Tunnel Vision . Meyer s Loop Deficit Temporal Lobe lesion $ to$ Superior Homonymous Quadrantanopia Pie in the Sky . Parietal Radiation Deficit Parietal Lobe lesion $ to$ Inferior Homonymous Quadrantanopia Pie on the Floor . Macular Sparing Occipital Cortex PCA infarcts often spare the Macula because it has dual blood supply MCA collaterals . Right Parietal Lobe Lesion Causes Left Hemispatial Neglect . The Right parietal lobe attends to both sides while the Left only attends to the Right. Loss of Right = Loss of Left attention."}, {"id": "l17", "title": "Lecture 17: Anatomy and Physiology of the Auditory System", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l17 Lecture 17 Anatomy and Physiology of the Auditory System n n Lecturer G. Otazu Ph.D. n Session Lecture 17 n n 1. Auditory Anatomy Slide 4-6 10-14 n n Outer Ear Funnels sound. n Middle Ear Ossicles Malleus Incus Stapes amplify sound to transfer it from air tympanic membrane to fluid oval window . n Impedance Matching Necessary because fluid differs from air. Stapes pushes on Oval Window. n Protective Reflex Stapedius CN VII and Tensor Tympani CN V3 contract to dampen loud sounds. n Inner Ear Cochlea n Tonotopy Base = High Frequencies Narrow/Stiff . Apex = Low Frequencies Wide/Floppy . n Ion Gradients Endolymph Scala Media is high in K+ . Perilymph is low in K+. n n 2. Mechanotransduction Organ of Corti Slide 15-18 nThe Organ of Corti sits on the basilar membrane. n Inner Hair Cells IHC The primary sensory receptors 95% of afferents . n Outer Hair Cells OHC Cochlear amplifiers. Damage = Tinnitus . n Transduction Vibration bends stereocilia $ to$ Tip links open K+ channels $ to$ K+ influx depolarization $ to$ Ca2+ influx $ to$ Glutamate release. n n 3. Auditory Pathway Slide 20-21 n E. C. O. L. I. M. mnemonic. n1. E ighth Nerve Spiral Ganglion . n2. C ochlear Nuclei Medulla . n3. S uperior O live Pons - Sound Localization Time/Intensity diffs . First bilateral processing. n4. L ateral Lemniscus. n5. I nferior Colliculus Midbrain . n6. M edial Geniculate Nucleus M GN Thalamus . n7. Auditory Cortex Temporal Lobe/Heschl s Gyrus . n n 4. Hearing Loss Types Slide 27-29 n Conductive Middle/Outer ear issue Wax Otitis Media Otosclerosis . n Rinne Bone > Air. n Weber Localizes to Affected ear. n Sensorineural Inner ear/Nerve issue Presbycusis Noise Drugs . n Rinne Air > Bone Normal pattern but reduced . n Weber Localizes to Unaffected ear. n n 5. Clinical Correlates Slide 33-37 n n correlate n Acoustic Neuroma Vestibular Schwannoma n Pathology Benign tumor of Schwann cells on CN VIII Vestibulocochlear . n CPA Angle Compression of Cerebellopontine Angle structures. n Symptoms Unilateral Hearing Loss Tinnitus Vertigo . Can compress CN VII Facial palsy if large. n Diagnosis MRI. n n n correlate n Usher Syndrome n Genetics Autosomal Recessive. n Pathology Mutation in stereocilia tip-link proteins e.g. Cadherin 23 . n Presentation Congenital Deafness + Retinitis Pigmentosa Progressive Blindness . Major cause of deaf-blindness. n n n correlate n EAST Syndrome n Acronym E pilepsy A taxia S ensorineural deafness T ubulopathy. n Pathology Mutation in KCNJ10 Kir4.1 K+ channel . n Mechanism Failure to maintain High K+ in Endolymph prevents hair cell depolarization. n n n correlate n Presbycusis n Definition Age-related sensorineural hearing loss. n Pattern Loss of High Frequencies first Base of cochlea wears out . n Mechanism Hair cell loss. n Lecture 17 Anatomy and Physiology of the Auditory System Neuroscience questions A 65-year-old male complains of difficulty hearing women s voices and birds chirping high frequencies in noisy environments. Otoscopy is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis? Otosclerosis Presbycusis Meniere s Disease Cholesteatoma Otitis Media 1 Presbycusis is age-related sensorineural hearing loss affecting High Frequencies first due to hair cell loss at the cochlear base. A patient presents with unilateral hearing loss tinnitus and facial weakness. MRI reveals a mass at the Cerebellopontine Angle. What is the likely diagnosis? Pituitary Adenoma Glioblastoma Multiforme Vestibular Schwannoma Acoustic Neuroma Meningioma Craniopharyngioma 2 Vestibular Schwannoma arises on CN VIII causing hearing loss/tinnitus and can compress the adjacent CN VII Facial nerve at the CPA. In the auditory pathway where does sound localization computing time/intensity differences first occur? Cochlear Nucleus Superior Olivary Complex Inferior Colliculus Medial Geniculate Nucleus Auditory Cortex 1 The Superior Olivary Complex is the first site receiving Bilateral input allowing it to compare timing and intensity data for localization. EAST Syndrome is caused by a mutation affecting the transport of which ion in the inner ear? Sodium Calcium Chloride Potassium Magnesium 3 EAST Syndrome is a K+ channelopathy Kir4.1 preventing the maintenance of high Potassium in the endolymph. During a Weber test the sound lateralizes to the patient s Left ear. Bone conduction is greater than Air conduction in the Left ear. This suggests Right Sensorineural Hearing Loss Left Sensorineural Hearing Loss Right Conductive Hearing Loss Left Conductive Hearing Loss Normal Hearing 3 Weber lateralizing to the affected ear + Rinne showing Bone > Air is the classic pattern for Conductive Hearing Loss in that ear Left . Auditory Pathway Mnemonic E. COLIM Eighth nerve Cochlear nuc Olive Lat Lemniscus Inf Colliculus MGN Neuroanatomy Frequency map of Cochlea Base = High. Apex = Low. Physiology Ion concentrated in Endolymph Potassium K+ Physiology Function of Superior Olivary Complex Sound Localization Neuroanatomy Muscle innervated by CN VII in ear Stapedius Anatomy Muscle innervated by CN V3 in ear Tensor Tympani Anatomy Usher Syndrome Presentation Deafness + Blindness Retinitis Pigmentosa Pathology Acoustic Neuroma Symptoms Unilateral Hearing Loss Tinnitus Vertigo Pathology Weber Test Conductive Loss Lateralizes to the Bad ear Clinical Skills Rinne Test Sensorineural Loss Air > Bone Normal pattern Clinical Skills BandB Neurology > The Ear > Hearing FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Auditory Pathoma Head & Neck Pathway Mnemonic E. COLIM E ighth Nerve $ to$ C ochlear Nucleus $ to$ O live Superior $ to$ L ateral Lemniscus $ to$ I nferior Colliculus $ to$ M GN. Cochlear Tonotopy Base = High Frequency Stiff/Narrow . n Apex = Low Frequency Floppy/Wide . Rinne & Weber Rules Conductive Weber to Bad Ear. Rinne Bone > Air. n Sensorineural Weber to Good Ear. Rinne Air > Bone. Acoustic Reflex Mediated by CN VII Stapedius and CN V3 Tensor Tympani to dampen loud sounds. EAST Syndrome Mechanism K+ Channel mutation Kir4.1 . No K+ gradient in Endolymph = No Hearing."}, {"id": "l18", "title": "Lecture 18: Anatomy & Physiology of the Vestibular System", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l18 Lecture 18 Anatomy & Physiology of the Vestibular System n n Lecturer R. Ramos Ph.D. n Session Lecture 18 n n 1. Peripheral Anatomy Slide 4-15 nThe Vestibular Labyrinth detects head motion via hair cells mechanotransduction . n Otolith Organs Utricle & Saccule n Function Detect Linear Acceleration e.g. gravity elevator car acceleration . n Mechanism Hair cells embedded in Macula covered by Otoconia crystals . Gravity pulls crystals $ to$ bends hairs. n Mnemonic U tricle = U pright Horizontal plane . S accule = S ideways Vertical plane . n Semicircular Canals Anterior Posterior Horizontal n Function Detect Angular Rotational Acceleration head turning . n Mechanism Crista Ampullaris covered by Cupula gelatin . n Fluid Dynamics When head turns Left Endolymph lags moves Right $ to$ bends Cupula. n n 2. Vestibular Pathways & Reflexes Slide 16-20 n Vestibulo-Ocular Reflex VOR Stabilizes gaze during head movement. n Path Head Left $ to$ Left SCC active $ to$ Vestibular Nuclei $ to$ Right CN VI Abd & Left CN III MR $ to$ Eyes move Right . n Vestibulo-Spinal Tracts n Lateral Extensor muscles Anti-gravity/Balance . n Medial Neck muscles Head stabilization . n n 3. Peripheral Vertigo Disorders Slide 26-30 n n correlate n Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo BPPV n Mechanism Otoconia crystals dislodge from Utricle and float into a Semicircular Canal usually Posterior . n Presentation Brief <1 min episodes of severe vertigo triggered by Head Position changes rolling over in bed . n Diagnosis Dix-Hallpike Maneuver elicits nystagmus. n Treatment Epley Maneuver repositions crystals . n n n correlate n Meniere s Disease n Mechanism Endolymphatic Hydrops Excess fluid pressure . n Presentation Triad of Episodic Vertigo mins-hours + Tinnitus + Sensorineural Hearing Loss . n Treatment Low salt diet Diuretics. n n n correlate n Labyrinthitis n Mechanism Inflammation/Infection of the inner ear. n Presentation Continuous severe vertigo + Hearing Loss + Nausea. Often follows a viral infection. n n n 4. Central Vertigo Disorders Slide 31-34 n n correlate n Wallenberg Syndrome Lateral Medullary Syndrome n Pathology Infarct of PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery . n Signs n Vestibular Nuclei Vertigo Nystagmus. n Nucleus Ambiguus dysphagia hoarseness. n Spinothalamic Tract Contralateral loss of Pain/Temp Body . n Spinal Trigeminal Ipsilateral loss of Pain/Temp Face . n Sympathetic Ipsilateral Horner s Syndrome Ptosis Miosis Anhidrosis . n n n mnemonic n Peripheral vs Central Vertigo n Peripheral Severe vertigo Hearing Loss common Fatigable nystagmus. BPPV Meniere s . n Central Mild vertigo No Hearing Loss usually Non-fatigable nystagmus CNS signs Ataxia Dysphagia . Stroke MS . n Lecture 18 Anatomy & Physiology of the Vestibular System Neuroscience questions A patient presents with episodes of room-spinning vertigo lasting 30 seconds triggered only when rolling over in bed. Hearing is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis? Meniere s Disease Vestibular Neuritis Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo BPPV Acoustic Neuroma Wallenberg Syndrome 2 BPPV is characterized by brief <1 min position-dependent vertigo with normal hearing caused by dislodged otoconia. Which structure detects linear acceleration and head tilt relative to gravity? Crista Ampullaris Semicircular Canals Otolith Organs Utricle/Saccule Cochlea Organ of Corti 2 The Otolith Organs Utricle and Saccule contain the macula and otoconia specifically designing them to detect Linear Acceleration and gravity. A patient presents with vertigo dysphagia hoarseness ipsilateral facial numbness and contralateral body numbness. Examination reveals an ipsilateral Horner s syndrome. Which artery is occluded? Anterior Spinal Artery Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery PICA Anterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery AICA Middle Cerebral Artery MCA 2 This is Wallenberg Lateral Medullary Syndrome caused by occlusion of the PICA . The constellation of Vertigo Vestibular Dysphagia Ambiguus and Crossed Sensory loss is classic. What is the primary mechanism of Meniere s Disease? Otoconia dislodgement Endolymphatic Hydrops Excess fluid Demyelination Bacterial Infection Tumor Compression 1 Meniere s Disease is caused by Endolymphatic Hydrops swelling/excess pressure of the endolymphatic compartment . The Vestibulo-Ocular Reflex VOR allows for Hearing high frequencies Maintaining upright posture Stabilizing gaze during head movement Localizing sound in space Detecting gravity 2 The VOR generates eye movements equal and opposite to head movements to stabilize gaze on a fixed target. Function of Semicircular Canals Detect Angular Rotational Acceleration Physiology Function of Otolith Organs Utricle/Saccule Detect Linear Acceleration & Gravity Physiology Diagnosis Brief positional vertigo no hearing loss BPPV Pathology Diagnosis Vertigo + Tinnitus + Hearing Loss Meniere s Disease Pathology Maneuver to treat BPPV Epley Maneuver Clinical Skills Artery involved in Lateral Medullary Syndrome PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Pathology Key signs of Wallenberg Syndrome Dysphagia Vertigo Horner s Crossed Pain/Temp loss Pathology Where are Otoconia normally located? Utricle and Saccule Macula Anatomy Symptoms of Acoustic Neuroma Unilateral Hearing Loss Tinnitus Vertigo CPA angle Pathology Fluid with high Potassium in Inner Ear Endolymph Physiology BandB Neurology > The Ear > Vestibular System FirstAid Neurology > Ophthalmology Pathoma CNS Otoliths vs Canals Otoliths Utricle/Saccule = Linear/Gravity. n Canals SCC = Angular/Rotation. BPPV Pathophysiology Dislodged Otoconia Otoliths floating in the Semicircular Canals. Meniere s Triad 1. Vertigo n2. Tinnitus n3. Hearing Loss Fluctuating . Wallenberg Syndrome Artery PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery . Causes Lateral Medullary Syndrome. Dix-Hallpike Diagnostic maneuver for BPPV . Induces nystagmus if positive."}, {"id": "l19", "title": "Lecture 19: Pharmacology of Direct-Acting Cholinomimetics", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l19 Lecture 19 Pharmacology of Direct-Acting Cholinomimetics n n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. n Session Lecture 19 n n 1. Cholinergic Receptors Slide 9-11 n Muscarinic GPCRs Located on effector organs Heart Smooth Muscle Glands . n M1 M3 M5 Gq Increase Ca2+ Contraction/Secretion . n M2 M4 Gi Decrease cAMP Inhibition/Bradycardia . n Nicotinic Ion Channels Located at Ganglia Nn and Neuromuscular Junction Nm . n Ligand-gated Na+/K+ channels. n n 2. Muscarinic Agonists Slide 30-33 n Direct-Acting means they bind directly to the receptor unlike AChE inhibitors . n n Choline Esters Quaternary Amines n Poorly absorbed do not cross BBB. n Acetylcholine ACh Rapidly hydrolyzed. Used only for Ophthalmic Surgery Miosis . n Methacholine n use Bronchoprovocation Test to diagnose Asthma. Induces bronchospasm in hyperreactive airways. n Bethanechol n Use Urinary Retention Post-op Post-partum Neurogenic bladder . n Mnemonic Bethany call chol me if you want to pee. n Carbachol Glaucoma rarely used . Mixed M and N activity. n n Alkaloids Tertiary Amines n Well absorbed Cross BBB. n Pilocarpine n Use Glaucoma Opens meshwork via miosis Xerostomia Sjogren s Radiation . n Mnemonic Pile on the sweat and tears. n Cevimeline Synthetic M3 agonist. Used for Sjogren s Syndrome Dry mouth . n n 3. Nicotinic Agonists Slide 36-39 n Nicotine n Action Biphasic. Low dose = Stimulation. High dose = Depolarizing Blockade Paralysis . n Use Smoking Cessation NRT . n Varenicline Chantix n Action Partial Agonist at $ alpha 4 beta 2$ neuronal receptors. n Benefit Reduces cravings agonist effect AND blocks nicotine from bindings antagonist effect reducing reward. n n 4. Adverse Effects & Contraindications Slide 34 n Muscarinic Toxicity SLUDGE n S alivation L acrimation U rination D efecation G I Upset E mesis. n DANGER Bradycardia and Bronchoconstriction . n Contraindications n Asthma/COPD Bronchoconstriction . n Peptic Ulcer Disease Increased Acid . n GI/Urinary Obstruction Risk of rupture . n n 5. Clinical Correlates Slide 33 n n correlate n Sj u00f6gren s Syndrome n Pathology Autoimmune destruction of exocrine glands Salivary Lacrimal . n Presentation Xerostomia Dry mouth Keratoconjunctivitis Sicca Dry eyes . n Treatment Pilocarpine or Cevimeline Muscarinic Agonists to stimulate secretion. n n n correlate n Asthma Diagnosis n Drug Methacholine . n Test Inhaled methacholine challenge. n Result A patient with asthma will bronchoconstrict at a much lower dose than a healthy person. n Lecture 19 Pharmacology of Direct-Acting Cholinomimetics Neuroscience questions A 55-year-old male with post-operative urinary retention needs medication to help void. Examination confirms no obstruction. Which drug is most appropriate? Methacholine Atropine Bethanechol Pilocarpine Nicotine 2 Bethanechol is a direct muscarinic agonist with selectivity for the bladder/GI used to treat non-obstructive urinary retention. A patient being treated for Sjogren s syndrome complains of excessive sweating and diarrhea. This is likely due to which medication? Pilocarpine Atropine Scopolamine Ipratropium Varenicline 0 Pilocarpine is used to increase salivation in Sjogren s but is non-selective causing systemic muscarinic side effects like sweating diaphoresis and diarrhea. Which drug acts as a partial agonist at nicotinic receptors to aid in smoking cessation? Bupropion Varenicline Nicotine Patch Methacholine Carbachol 1 Varenicline is a partial agonist at $ alpha 4 beta 2$ nicotinic receptors reducing cravings and withdrawal symptoms. Methacholine is primarily used clinically for Treating Glaucoma Diagnosing Asthma Treating Urinary Retention Smoking Cessation Treating Alzheimer s 1 Methacholine is used in the bronchoprovocation challenge test to diagnose asthma . Direct-acting cholinomimetics should be avoided in patients with which condition? Glaucoma Xerostomia Peptic Ulcer Disease Constipation Hypertension 2 Muscarinic agonists increase gastric acid secretion which can exacerbate Peptic Ulcer Disease . Drug for Post-op Urinary Retention Bethanechol Pharmacology Drug for Diagnosis of Asthma Methacholine Pharmacology Drug for Sjogren s Syndrome Pilocarpine or Cevimeline Pharmacology Mechanism of Varenicline Partial Agonist at Nicotinic Receptors Pharmacology Contraindication for Muscarinic Agonists Lung Asthma/COPD Pharmacology Contraindication for Muscarinic Agonists GI Peptic Ulcer Disease or Obstruction Pharmacology SLUDGE Mnemonic Salivation Lacrimation Urination Defecation GI upset Emesis Toxicology Pilocarpine Class Muscarinic Agonist Tertiary Amine Pharmacology Bethanechol Class Muscarinic Agonist Quaternary Amine - No CNS Pharmacology Receptors on Sweat Glands Muscarinic Sympathetic Cholinergic Physiology SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Parasympathetics > Cholinomimetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs BandB Pharmacology > ANS > Cholinergic Agonists Bethanechol Use Non-obstructive Urinary Retention . Bethany call me to pee . Pilocarpine Use Open Angle Glaucoma and Sjogren s Syndrome Sweat/Saliva . Methacholine Challenge Used to diagnose Asthma via induced bronchoconstriction. Varenicline Mechanism Partial Agonist at $ alpha 4 beta 2$ Nicotinic receptors. Reduces withdrawal + Blocks reward. Cholinomimetic Contraindications Asthma causes bronchospasm Peptic Ulcers causes acid Obstruction causes rupture ."}, {"id": "l1a", "title": "Lecture #1a: Basics of Diagnosis: Innominates and Pubic Bone", "module": "OMM", "content": "l1a Lecture 1a Basics of Diagnosis Innominates and Pubic Bone n n Lecturer To Shan Li D.O. n Session Lecture 1a n n Introduction and Objectives Slide 2 nThis lecture introduces the structural examination of the pelvis focusing on the diagnosis of somatic dysfunctions of the innominates and pubic bone. n Key Goals n Understand pelvic anatomy and biomechanics. n Perform and interpret Standing Flexion Test StFT ASIS Compression Test and Seated Flexion Test SeFT . n Differentiate between Iliosacral Pelvic and Sacroiliac Sacral dysfunctions. n n--- n n Anatomy of the Pelvis Slide 5-6 n Bones n Innominate Composed of three parts that fuse at the acetabulum n Ilium n Ischium n Pubis n Sacrum Articulates with the ilia. n n Joints n Sacroiliac Joint SIJ n L-shaped or C-shaped. n Allows for mobility though limited Slide 6 . n Mobility decreases with age. n Pubic Symphysis Fibrocartilaginous joint. n n Ligaments Slide 7 15 n Ligaments e.g. sacrotuberous sacrospinous provide stability and create foramina for neurovascular structures. n Clinical Relevance They guide motion and can become tender/restrictive in somatic dysfunction. n n--- n n Biomechanics and Function n n Gait and Motion Slide 17 n Physiologic motion of the sacrum and ilia occurs during walking. n Somatic Dysfunction Affects gait efficiency and increases energy expenditure. n n correlate n Pregnancy-Related Changes Slide 18 n Pathophysiology Horizontalization of sacrum increased laxity due to Relaxin . n Presentation Anterior pelvic tilt increased lumbar lordosis potential for pain low back pelvic . n Lecturer s Point Center of gravity shifts anteriorly... Sacroiliac joint and pubic symphysis become more lax. n n n Clinical Implications Slide 20 n Pelvic dysfunction often presents as n Low back pain hip/groin pain. n Sciatic neuralgia piriformis syndrome . n Visceral issues GI GU via autonomic relationships. n n--- n n Structural Examination Screening Tests n n 1. Standing Flexion Test StFT Slide 23-28 n Purpose Diagnoses Iliosacral Dysfunction Ilio-sacral = Ilium moving on Sacrum = Pelvic SD . n Execution n 1. Patient stands feet 6-8 inches apart. n 2. Physician monitors PSIS bilaterally eyes at level . n 3. Patient bends forward slowly. n Positive Result One PSIS moves First and Farthest superiorly. n Interpretation The side that moves superiorly is the Dysfunctional Side restricted side . The ilium is locked to the sacrum on that side and moves with it. n n pearl n StFT Mnemonic Standing = S ide of I lium Iliosacral . n n n 2. Seated Flexion Test SeFT Slide 32-35 n Purpose Diagnoses Sacroiliac Dysfunction Sacro-iliac = Sacrum moving on Ilium = Sacral SD . n Execution n 1. Patient seated feet flat stabilizing the ilia . n 2. Physician monitors PSIS bilaterally. n 3. Patient bends forward. n Positive Result One PSIS moves First and Farthest . n Interpretation Indicates the side of sacral dysfunction. n Note The standing vs seated distinction isolates the ilia standing = mobile vs sacroiliac mechanics seated = ilia locked by tuberosities . n n highyield n StFT vs SeFT Summary Slide 36 n StFT + Iliosacral Dysfunction -> Look for Innominate/Pubic shears. n Axis Inferior Transverse Axis. n SeFT + Sacroiliac Dysfunction -> Look for Sacral Torsions/Shears. n Axis Middle Transverse Axis. n n n 3. ASIS Compression Test Pelvic Rock Slide 30-31 n Purpose Lateralization Determines which side is dysfunctional . n Helps confirm StFT/SeFT findings or distinguish when tests are equivocal. n Execution n 1. Patient supine. n 2. Physician applies posterior-medial pressure on ASIS towards the SI joint. n 3. Assess for spring or resistance. n Positive Result The side with Restriction less spring board-like feel is the positive side. n n--- n n Static Landmarks Examination Slide 40-42 nAfter lateralizing finding the side check landmarks to classify the dysfunction e.g. Anterior/Posterior Rotation Upslip/Downslip . n n Anterior Landmarks n ASIS Heights Compare superior/inferior. n Pubic Tubercles Check for superior/inferior shears. n Medial Malleoli Assess functional leg length. n n Posterior Landmarks n PSIS Heights Compare. n Iliac Crest Heights Compare. n Ischial Tuberosities Compare heights. n Sacral Sulci Depth. n n pearl n Diagnosis Flow n1. Screen StFT Pelvic vs SeFT Sacral . n2. Lateralize ASIS Compression confirms side . n3. Diagnose Check landmarks ASIS PSIS etc. on the dysfunctional side relative to the other. n n n--- n n Review Questions Slide 44-52 n nSee the Questions tab for interactive board-style practice. Lecture 1a Basics of Diagnosis Innominates and Pubic Bone lecturer To Shan Li D.O. OMM session Lecture 1a prev next l1b pdf pdfs/Lecture 1a. T. Li D.O. PPT .pdf Basics of Pelvic Diagnosis n Anatomy n Innominate Bones Ilium Ischium Pubis n Joints Sacroiliac SIJ Pubic Symphysis n Ligaments Sacrotuberous Sacrospinous Stability n Biomechanics n Motion Occurs during gait n Pregnancy Relaxin -> Increased laxity -> Anterior Tilt n Screening Tests n Standing Flexion StFT n Diagnoses Iliosacral Innominate Dysfunction n Axis Inferior Transverse n Positive restricted side moves Superior n Seated Flexion SeFT n Diagnoses Sacroiliac Sacral Dysfunction n Axis Middle Transverse n Mechanism Ischial tuberosities lock ilia n ASIS Compression n Purpose Lateralization confirms side n Positive Resistance/Rigidity n Landmarks Static Exam n Anterior ASIS Pubic Tubercles Medial Malleoli n Posterior PSIS Iliac Crests Ischial Tuberosities Sacral Sulci questions 1. A 32-year-old female presents with right-sided low back pain. Structural examination reveals a positive Standing Flexion Test on the right and a negative Seated Flexion Test. The ASIS Compression Test is positive on the right. What is the most likely category of somatic dysfunction? Sacroiliac Dysfunction Sacral SD Iliosacral Dysfunction Innominate SD Lumbar Somatic Dysfunction Psoas Syndrome Pubic Shear 1 A positive Standing Flexion Test StFT indicates Iliosacral Innominate dysfunction. The Seated Flexion Test is negative ruling out primary Sacroiliac Sacral dysfunction. The ASIS compression confirms the right side is involved. Therefore the diagnosis lies within the iliosacral/innominate category B . 2. While performing the Standing Flexion Test the physician notes the right PSIS moves more superiorly than the left. Which biomechanical principle explains this finding? The right iliosacral joint is hypermobile The right iliosacral joint is restricted hypomobile The left sacrum is torsioned The right hamstrings are tight The left quadratus lumborum is tight 1 In the flexion tests the PSIS on the dysfunctional side moves first and farthest superiorly. This is because the joint is restricted hypomobile and locks early carrying the ilium up with the sacrum as the patient bends forward. The normal side maintains motion longer and doesn t rise as high initially. 3. A pregnant patient in her third trimester presents with anterior pelvic tilt and increased lumbar lordosis. Which hormone is primarily responsible for the increased laxity of her sacroiliac joints and pubic symphysis? Estrogen Progesterone Relaxin Oxytocin Prolactin 2 Relaxin is the hormone secreted during pregnancy that increases ligamentous laxity particularly in the pelvis SI joints pubic symphysis to accommodate childbirth. This laxity contributes to the structural changes described Slide 18 . 4. The Seated Flexion Test is designed to isolate the motion of the sacrum by eliminating the influence of which structures? The lumbar spine The lower extremities/Ilia The thoracic cage The cervical spine The abdominal muscles 1 By sitting the weight is borne by the ischial tuberosities. This effectively locks the ilia and minimizes the influence of the lower extremities and by extension the iliosacral mechanics on the ilium side allowing the physician to assess sacroiliac sacral motion more specifically Slide 23 . 5. Which axis of motion is primarily associated with Iliosacral Pelvic motion as tested by the Standing Flexion Test? Superior Transverse Axis Middle Transverse Axis Inferior Transverse Axis Oblique Axis Vertical Axis 2 Iliosacral motion ilium moving on sacrum occurs primarily around the Inferior Transverse Axis Slide 36 . Sacroiliac motion sacrum moving on ilium occurs around the Middle Transverse Axis . Standing Flexion Test assesses which dysfunction? Iliosacral Dysfunction Innominate/Pelvic Concept Seated Flexion Test assesses which dysfunction? Sacroiliac Dysfunction Sacral Concept What does a positive StFT indicate biomechanically? The PSIS on the positive side moves first and farthest because the Ilio-sacral joint is restricted hypomobile . Concept What is the purpose of the ASIS Compression Test? Lateralization identifying which side involved . Clinical Which hormone causes pelvic ligament laxity in pregnancy? Relaxin Glossary Axis of motion for Iliosacral Dysfunction? Inferior Transverse Axis Concept Axis of motion for Sacroiliac Dysfunction? Middle Transverse Axis Concept Anatomy What 3 bones fuse to form the Innominate? Ilium Ischium Pubis Glossary In StFT if Right PSIS moves higher which side is dysfunctional? Right side Clinical Normal result for ASIS Compression Test? Springy/Resilient feel Restrictions feel rigid/board-like . Clinical"}, {"id": "l1b", "title": "Lecture #1b: Diagnosis of the Pelvis (Lab Prep)", "module": "OMM", "content": "l1b Lecture 1b Diagnosis of the Pelvis Lab Prep n n Lecturer Michael J. Terzella D.O. n Session Lab 15 Prep n n Introduction nThis lecture prepares students for the Pelvis Lab 15 . It reinforces the diagnostic concepts from Lecture 1a and introduces specific Innominate and Pubic Somatic Dysfunctions. n Goal Efficiently examine name and record somatic dysfunctions of the pelvis. n n--- n n Review of Diagnostic Tests Slide 4-6 8-13 n 1. Standing Flexion Test StFT n Significance Lateralizes Innominate Iliosacral dysfunction. n Positive PSIS moves first and farthest superiorly. n Rule Detailed diagnosis is named for the side of the positive StFT . n n 2. ASIS Compression Test Pelvic Rock n Significance Lateralizes dysfunction; useful screening but less specific can be Iliosacral or Sacroiliac . n Positive Resistance to posterior-medial compression Hard end-feel . n n 3. Static Landmarks n Anterior ASIS Pubic Tubercles Medial Malleoli. n Posterior PSIS Iliac Crests Ischial Tuberosities Sacral Sulci. n n--- n n Innominate Somatic Dysfunctions Slide 26-36 n n Innominate Rotations nMotion of the innominate on the sacrum around the Inferior Transverse Axis . n n Anterior Rotation Slide 28-29 n Mechanism Innominate rotates anteriorly. n Landmarks on dysfunctional side n ASIS Inferior n PSIS Superior n Medial Malleolus Inferior Long Leg n Symptoms Hamstring tightness Sciatica Piriformis . n n Posterior Rotation Slide 30-31 n Mechanism Innominate rotates posteriorly. n Landmarks on dysfunctional side n ASIS Superior n PSIS Inferior n Medial Malleolus Superior Short Leg n Symptoms Inguinal/Groin pain Rectus Femoris Medial Knee pain Sartorius . n n Innominate Shears nTranslatory motion of the entire innominate usually traumatic . n n Superior Shear Upslip Slide 33-34 n Mechanism Entire innominate shifts superiorly e.g. landing on one leg . n Landmarks n ASIS Superior n PSIS Superior n Pubic Tubercle Superior n Medial Malleolus Superior Short Leg n Motion Laxity in superior translation. n n Inferior Shear Downslip Slide 35-36 n Mechanism Entire innominate shifts inferiorly e.g. getting leg caught in stirrup . n Landmarks n ASIS Inferior n PSIS Inferior n Pubic Tubercle Inferior n Medial Malleolus Inferior Long Leg n n pearl n Shear Tip In a shear ALL landmarks move in the same direction e.g. Superior Shear = ASIS High PSIS High Pubes High . In rotations ASIS and PSIS move in opposites . n n n Innominate Flares Slide 37 n Outflare ASIS further from midline associated with Posterior Rotation . n Inflare ASIS closer to midline associated with Anterior Rotation . n n--- n n Pubic Somatic Dysfunctions Slide 38-42 n n Pubic Shears Slide 38 n Superior Shear Pubic tubercle high on dysfunctional side. n Inferior Shear Pubic tubercle low on dysfunctional side. n Clinical Trauma childbirth. n n Pubic Symphysis Dysfunctions nDysfunction at the joint itself without asymmetry of height. n n correlate n Pubic Compression Adducted Pubis Slide 39-40 n Pathophysiology Pubic bones forced together Trauma . n Presentation n Bilateral tenderness. n Bulging symphyseal cartilage. n Restricted Pelvic Ring motion Bilaterally restricted ASIS Compression . n Urinary symptoms frequency/urgency . n Diagnosis No height asymmetry but restricted motion and tenderness. n n n correlate n Pubic Gapping Abducted Pubis Slide 41-42 n Pathophysiology Pubic bones pulled apart Childbirth Fractures . n Presentation n Deep Sulcus at symphysis. n Bilateral tenderness. n Urinary symptoms. n Often very painful. n n n--- n n Case Study Slide 45 n Patient 20F Equestrian fell from horse foot caught in stirrup. n Findings Right Side n StFT + Right n ASIS Inferior n PSIS Inferior n Pubic Tubercle Inferior n Medial Malleolus Inferior n Diagnosis Right Inferior Innominate Shear . n Reasoning All landmarks are inferior on the right consistent with the mechanism of injury traction on the leg . n n Lab Prep Pearls n Order of Exam n 1. Screen StFT/SeFT . n 2. Lateralize ASIS Compression . n 3. Reseat the Pelvis! Bridge . n 4. Static Landmarks Anterior then Posterior . n 5. Diagnose. Lecture 1b Diagnosis of the Pelvis Lab Prep lecturer Michael J. Terzella D.O. OMM session Lab Prep Lecture prev l1a next l2 pdf pdfs/Lecture 1b. M. Terzella D.O. PPT .pdf Pelvic Somatic Dysfunctions n Innominate Rotations n Anterior Rotation n ASIS Inferior n PSIS Superior n Leg Long n Association Inflare Hamstring tightness n Posterior Rotation n ASIS Superior n PSIS Inferior n Leg Short n Association Outflare Groin pain Rectus n Innominate Shears n Superior Shear Upslip n All Landmarks Superior ASIS PSIS Pubes n Cause Vertical impact landing on leg n Inferior Shear Downslip n All Landmarks Inferior n Cause Traction stuck in stirrup n Pubic Dysfunctions n Shears Sup/Inf asymmetry named for StFT side n Compression Adduction n Sign Bulging rigidity n Cause Trauma n Gapping Abduction n Sign Deep Sulcus n Cause Childbirth questions 1. A patient presents with right-sided pelvic pain. Examination reveals Right ASIS is Superior Right PSIS is Inferior and Right Medial Malleolus is Superior. What is the most likely diagnosis? Right Anterior Innominate Rotation Right Posterior Innominate Rotation Right Superior Innominate Shear Left Anterior Innominate Rotation Left Superior Innominate Shear 1 In a Posterior Rotation the ASIS moves up Superior and the PSIS moves down Inferior . This also pulls the leg up causing a short leg Superior Medial Malleolus . This matches the findings exactly. 2. Which of the following findings is most consistent with a Superior Innominate Shear Upslip ? ASIS High PSIS Low ASIS Low PSIS High ASIS High PSIS High Pubic Tubercle High ASIS Low PSIS Low Pubic Tubercle Low ASIS High PSIS High Pubic Tubercle Low 2 In a Shear Upslip the entire bone translates superiorly. Therefore ALL landmarks on that bone ASIS PSIS Pubic Tubercle Ischial Tuberosity will be found Superior compared to the healthy side. 3. A postpartum female presents with pubic pain and urinary urgency. Examination reveals bilateral pubic tenderness and a deep palpable sulcus at the pubic symphysis. ASIS and PSIS heights are symmetric. What is the diagnosis? Superior Pubic Shear Inferior Pubic Shear Pubic Compression Adducted Pubic Gapping Abducted Bilateral Innominate Outflare 3 The key finding is the deep sulcus at the symphysis and history of childbirth which suggests Pubic Gapping Abduction . Compression would present with a bulging cartilage. Shears would present with height asymmetry. 4. In an Anterior Innominate Rotation which muscle group is most likely to be tight/hypertonic? Hamstrings Quadriceps/Rectus Femoris Gluteus Maximus Biceps Femoris External Obliques 1 This is a bit distinct check Slide 29 vs 31 . Actually Hamstrings are tight in Anterior rotation Lecturer slide 28 notes Subjective complaints Hamstring tightness for Anterior . Wait biomechanically Rectus pulls it Anterior. Let s check slide 29 Anteriorly Rotated... Hamstring tightness . Why? Because the ischial tuberosity moves superiorly/posteriorly putting tension on the hamstrings. Conversely Quadriceps Rectus dysfunction is associated with Posterior rotation Slide 31 because it attaches to AIIS/ASIS and pulls it down. 5. What is the critical first step before performing the static landmark examination? Treat the lumbar spine Reseat the pelvis Bridging Measure True leg length Perform the Seated Flexion Test Check cranial rhythmic impulse 1 Reseating the pelvis asking patient to bend knees lift hips and set them down is crucial to ensure the patient is straight and soft tissues are relaxed preventing False positives in landmark asymmetry Slide 15 . Landmarks for Anterior Innominate Rotation? ASIS Low PSIS High Leg Long Clinical Landmarks for Posterior Innominate Rotation? ASIS High PSIS Low Leg Short Clinical Landmarks for Superior Innominate Shear? ASIS High PSIS High Pubes High All Up Clinical Landmarks for Inferior Innominate Shear? ASIS Low PSIS Low Pubes Low All Down Clinical Key finding for Pubic Compression? Bulging cartilage restricted pelvic ring bilateral tenderness. Clinical Key finding for Pubic Gapping? Deep sulcus history of delivery/trauma. Clinical Muscle associated with Anterior Innominate pain? Hamstrings tightness Concept Muscle associated with Posterior Innominate pain? Rectus Femoris / Sartorius Groin/Knee pain Concept Innominate Inflare is associated with which rotation? Anterior Rotation Concept Innominate Outflare is associated with which rotation? Posterior Rotation Concept"}, {"id": "l2", "title": "Lecture #2: Overview of Neuroembryology", "module": "Neuro", "content": "l2 Lecture 2 Overview of Neuroembryology n n Lecturer K. Poole Ph.D. n Session Lecture 2 n Topic Embryonic development of the Nervous System n n 1. Gastrulation and Neurulation Slides 3-8 n n Gastrulation Week 3 nThe process begins with Gastrulation establishing the three germ layers n Ectoderm Gives rise to the Nervous System and skin epidermis . n Mesoderm Forms muscle bone connective tissue and the Notochord . n Endoderm Forms the gut tube and internal organs. n n Notochord Induction nThe Notochord a rod-like mesodermal structure is the key inducer. It secretes signaling molecules including Sonic Hedgehog SHH and BMPs that signal the overlying ectoderm to thicken and differentiate into the Neural Plate Slide 4 17 . n n Neurulation nThe transformation of the neural plate into the neural tube is the fundamental event of the nervous system. n1. Neural Plate Thickened ectoderm Day 19 . n2. Neural Groove The midline invaginates Day 20 . n3. Neural Folds The lateral edges elevate. n4. Neural Tube The folds fuse in the midline creating a hollow tube. This fusion begins in the cervical region and zips rostrally and caudally. n n Neuropore Closure nThe tube is initially open at both ends. Timing is critical n Rostral Anterior Neuropore Closes around Day 25 . n Caudal Posterior Neuropore Closes around Day 27-28 . n n 2. Neural Crest Cells Slides 13-15 nAs the neural tube closes cells at the crest of the neural folds detach and migrate away. These are the Neural Crest Cells often termed the 4th Germ Layer due to their wide variation. n PNS Derivatives Dorsal Root Ganglia Sensory Autonomic Ganglia Schwann Cells. n Other Derivatives Melanocytes Adrenal Medulla Craniofacial bones/cartilage face Odontoblasts teeth . n n 3. Neural Tube Differentiation Slides 16-19 nThe wall of the neural tube organizes into distinct layers n Ventricular Zone Inner layer neuroepithelium becomes Ependymal cells lining ventricles. n Mantle Layer Middle layer becomes Gray Matter cell bodies . n Marginal Layer Outer layer becomes White Matter axons . n n Dorsal-Ventral Patterning Slide 17 28 n Alar Plate Dorsal Induced by BMPs/TGF- u03b2 . Becomes Sensory regions Dorsal Horn . n Basal Plate Ventral Induced by SHH from Notochord . Becomes Motor regions Ventral Horn . n Sulcus Limitans The groove separating Alar sensory and Basal motor plates. n n 4. Brain Development Vesicles Slides 21-25 nThe rostral neural tube expands into vesicles. n 3 Primary Vesicles Week 4 n1. Prosencephalon Forebrain n2. Mesencephalon Midbrain n3. Rhombencephalon Hindbrain n n 5 Secondary Vesicles Week 5 n Prosencephalon divides into n Telencephalon Cerebral Hemispheres Lateral Ventricles. n Diencephalon Thalamus Hypothalamus Retina Third Ventricle. n Mesencephalon Remains Mesencephalon Midbrain Cerebral Aqueduct . n Rhombencephalon divides into n Metencephalon Pons Cerebellum Upper 4th Ventricle. n Myelencephalon Medulla Lower 4th Ventricle. n n 5. Clinical Correlates Congenital Disorders n n correlate n Neural Tube Defects NTDs nCaused by failure of neuropore closure. Strongly associated with Folate Vitamin B9 deficiency. n Anencephaly Meroencephaly Failure of Rostral neuropore closure. Forebrain and skull vault fail to form. Frog-like appearance. Incompatible with life. n Spina Bifida Failure of Caudal neuropore closure. n Occulta Defect in vertebral arch only. Tuft of hair/dimple. Asymptomatic. n Meningocele Meninges herniate through defect. Fluid-filled sac. n Myelomeningocele Meninges + Spinal Cord herniate. Severe neurologic deficit paralysis sensory loss . n Encephalocele Herniation of brain tissue through a skull defect often occipital . n n n correlate n Holoprosencephaly HPE Slide 26-27 n Pathology Failure of the Prosencephalon Forebrain to cleavage/divide into two hemispheres. n Mechanism Defect in midline induction often SHH pathway . n Presentation Spectrum of midline facial defects Cleft lip/palate hypotelorism Cyclopia single eye and proboscis tube nose in severe cases. n Associations Patau Syndrome Trisomy 13 Fetal Alcohol Syndrome. n n n correlate n Dandy-Walker Syndrome Slide 32 n Pathology Agenesis or hypoplasia of the Cerebellar Vermis . n Mechanism Failure of the Rhombic Lip to develop Metencephalon . n Imaging Large posterior fossa cyst dilated 4th ventricle + small/absent cerebellum. n Symptoms Hydrocephalus Ataxia Delayed motor development. n n n correlate n Microcephaly Slide 38 n Pathology Small brain -> Small skull Calvaria grows in response to brain growth . n Causes n Genetics Autosomal recessive. n Infections Zika Virus Cytomegalovirus CMV Rubella Toxoplasmosis. n Environment Fetal Alcohol Sulfate Radiation. n n n correlate n Hydrocephalus Slide 44 n Pathology Accumulation of CSF in ventricles due to obstruction or impaired absorption. n Congenital Cause Aqueductal Stenosis narrowing of Cerebral Aqueduct of Sylvius is the most common cause. Blocks flow from 3rd to 4th ventricle. n Signs Rapidly enlarging head circumference bulging fontanelles Sunset eyes . n Lecture 2 Overview of Neuroembryology lecturer K. Poole Ph.D. Neuro session Lecture 2 prev l1b next l3 pdf pdfs/Lecture 2. K. Poole Ph.D. PPT -1.pdf questions 1. Which of the following cells are derived from the Neural Crest? Motor neurons of the ventral horn Astrocytes of the CNS Dorsal Root Ganglion DRG cells Oligodendrocytes Microglia 2 Neural Crest cells migrate to form the DRGs autonomic ganglia Schwann cells and adrenal medulla. CNS glial cells astrocytes oligos come from the neural tube neuroectoderm . Microglia come from mesoderm. 2. A newborn has a tuft of hair over the lumbar spine but no neurologic deficits. What is the most likely diagnosis? Spina Bifida Occulta Meningocele Myelomeningocele Anencephaly Rachischisis 0 Spina Bifida Occulta is a defect in the vertebral arch mesoderm fusion often marked by a tuft of hair or dimple. The meninges and cord remain in place. 3. Which vitamin deficiency is strongly associated with Neural Tube Defects? Vitamin B12 Vitamin C Folic Acid B9 Vitamin D Vitamin A 2 Folic Acid supplementation prior to conception and during early pregnancy significantly reduces the risk of NTDs. 4. The Metencephalon gives rise to which adult structures? Medulla Midbrain Thalamus and Hypothalamus Pons and Cerebellum Cerebral Hemispheres 3 The Metencephalon develops into the Pons and Cerebellum . The Myelencephalon becomes the Medulla. 5. Which structure induces the formation of the Neural Plate from the ectoderm? Primitive Streak Notochord Yolk Sac Intermediate Mesoderm Neural Crest 1 The Notochord derived from mesoderm secretes signaling molecules e.g. Sonic Hedgehog that induce the overlying ectoderm to differentiate into the Neural Plate . Tissue origin of CNS? Neuroectoderm Neural Tube Embryology Tissue origin of PNS neurons/glia? Neural Crest Embryology Structure inducing neural plate? Notochord Embryology Adult derivative of Telencephalon? Cerebrum Cerebral Hemispheres Embryology Adult derivative of Diencephalon? Thalamus Hypothalamus Retina Embryology Failure of rostral neuropore closure? Anencephaly Pathology Failure of caudal neuropore closure? Spina Bifida Pathology Vitamin to prevent NTDs? Folic Acid B9 Prevention Closure day of Rostral Neuropore? Day 25 Embryology Closure day of Caudal Neuropore? Day 27-28 Embryology BandB Neurology > Embryology > Nervous System Development FirstAid Neurology > Embryology Pathoma CNS > Congenital Malformations Physeo Anatomy > Neuroanatomy > Embryology Germ Layer Rule Ectoderm = Skin + Brain. n Mesoderm = Muscle + Bone + Notochord. n Endoderm = Gut. Neural Crest Mnemonic MOTEL PASS M elanocytes O dontoblasts T racheal cartilage E nterochromaffin cells L aryngeal cartilage P arafollicular cells A drenal medulla S chwann cells S piral septum. Neuropore Dates Rostral closes Day 25 . Caudal closes Day 27-28 . Folate is critical before this window. Alar vs Basal Plate Alar = Dorsal = Sensory Top . n Basal = Ventral = Motor Bottom . Holoprosencephaly Failed forebrain cleavage. Spectrum Cleft lip -> Cyclopia. Associated with Trisomy 13 Patau and SHH mutations."}, {"id": "l20", "title": "Lecture 20: Indirect-Acting Cholinomimetics (Cholinesterase Inhibitors)", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l20 Lecture 20 Indirect-Acting Cholinomimetics Cholinesterase Inhibitors n n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. n Session Lecture 20 n n 1. Mechanism & Classification Slide 9 16 nIndirect-acting agents inhibit Acetylcholinesterase AChE increasing the concentration of endogenous Acetylcholine in the synapse. This stimulates both Muscarinic and Nicotinic receptors. n Quaternary Amines No CNS entry Neostigmine Pyridostigmine. n Tertiary Amines Enter CNS Physostigmine Donepezil Organophosphates. n n 2. Clinical Uses Slide 20 36 n n Neuromuscular Junction Disorders n Myasthenia Gravis MG n Pathology Autoantibodies against Nicotinic receptors Nm . n Treatment Pyridostigmine Oral long-acting or Neostigmine . n Diagnosis Edrophonium Tensilon test. Rapid short-acting. Improvement = +MG. Historical . n Reversal of NMJ Blockade n Neostigmine is used to reverse non-depolarizing neuromuscular blockers e.g. Rocuronium after surgery. n n CNS Disorders n Alzheimer s Disease n Drugs Donepezil Rivastigmine Galantamine . n Mechanism Increase CNS ACh to improve cognition/memory. n Anticholinergic Toxicity Atropine Overdose n Drug Physostigmine . n Why? It crosses the BBB to reverse central delirium unlike Neostigmine . n n Glaucoma n Echothiophate Organophosphate Long acting . n n 3. Toxicity & Poisoning Slide 24-28 n n Organophosphates Insecticides Nerve Gas n Mechanism Irreversible inhibition of AChE via phosphorylation . Aging makes the bond permanent. n Muscarinic Signs DUMBELS D iarrhea U rination M iosis B radycardia/Bronchospasm E mesis L acrimation S alivation. n Nicotinic Signs Fasciculations $ to$ Paralysis Depolarizing blockade . n Treatment n 1. Atropine Blocks Muscarinic effects life-saving for lungs/heart . n 2. Pralidoxime 2-PAM Regenerates AChE IF given before aging. Relieves neuromuscular paralysis Atropine does not . n n 4. Clinical Correlates Slide 22 n n correlate n Myasthenic vs. Cholinergic Crisis n Myasthenic Crisis Low ACh. Respiratory failure due to undertreatment of MG. Action Give more Pyridostigmine. n Cholinergic Crisis Excess ACh. Depolarizing blockade due to overtreatment. Action Stop drug give Atropine. n differentiation Edrophonium test improves Myasthenic crisis but worsens Cholinergic crisis. n n n correlate n Ogilvie Syndrome n Condition Acute Colonic Pseudo-obstruction massive dilation without physical blockage . n Treatment Neostigmine stimulates GI motility . n Lecture 20 Indirect-Acting Cholinomimetics Cholinesterase Inhibitors Neuroscience questions A farmer is brought to the ER with pinpoint pupils excessive salivation difficulty breathing and muscle fasciculations. Exposure to an insecticide is suspected. Which drug should be administered immediately to treat the respiratory secretions and bradycardia? Physostigmine Pralidoxime Atropine Neostigmine Pilocarpine 2 Atropine is the first-line antidote for the life-threatening muscarinic effects bronchorrhea bronchospasm bradycardia of organophosphate poisoning. Which drug is an appropriate treatment for Alzheimer s disease? Neostigmine Pyridostigmine Donepezil Edrophonium Pilocarpine 2 Donepezil is a centrally acting AChE inhibitor used for Alzheimer s. Neostigmine/Pyridostigmine do not cross the BBB . A child ingests his grandmother s eye drops Atropine and develops hot red skin dilated pupils and delirium. Which antidote can reverse the CNS delirium? Neostigmine Physostigmine Pralidoxime Donepezil Edrophonium 1 The patient has Anticholinergic toxicity Mad as a hatter . Physostigmine is a tertiary amine that crosses the BBB to reverse CNS toxicity. What is the mechanism of action of Pralidoxime 2-PAM ? Blocks muscarinic receptors Blocks nicotinic receptors Regenerates Acetylcholinesterase Inhibits Acetylcholine release Directly activates GABA receptors 2 Pralidoxime is a cholinesterase regenerator. It breaks the bond between the organophosphate and the enzyme if given before aging . Which drug is preferred for long-term oral management of Myasthenia Gravis? Edrophonium Physostigmine Pyridostigmine Echothiophate Acetylcholine 2 Pyridostigmine has a longer duration of action 4-6h and is oral making it ideal for maintenance therapy in Myasthenia Gravis . Antidote for Organophosphate Poisoning Muscarinic Atropine Toxicology Antidote for Organophosphate Poisoning Enzyme Regenerator Pralidoxime 2-PAM Toxicology Antidote for Atropine Overdose Physostigmine Toxicology Drug for Myasthenia Gravis Maintenance Pyridostigmine Pharmacology Drug for Alzheimer s Disease AChE Inhibitor Donepezil Pharmacology Why doesn t Neostigmine treat Alzheimer s? It is a Quaternary amine Charged -> Doesn t cross BBB Pharmacology Symptoms of Muscarinic Excess DUMBELS Diarrhea Urination Miosis Bradycardia Emesis Lacrimation Salivation Toxicology Drug used to diagnose Myasthenia Gravis Historical Edrophonium Pharmacology Mechanism of Organophosphates Irreversible inhibition of Acetylcholinesterase Toxicology Treatment for Ogilvie Syndrome Neostigmine Pharmacology SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Parasympathetics > Acetylcholinesterase Inhibitors FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs BandB Pharmacology > ANS > Cholinergic Agonists Physostigmine vs. Neostigmine Physostigmine crosses BBB Treats Atropine OD . n Neostigmine does NOT cross BBB Treats MG urinary retention . DUMBELS Mnemonic Diarrhea Urination Miosis Bradycardia Emesis Lacrimation Salivation Muscarinic excess . Organophosphate Antidotes 1. Atropine Muscarinic symptoms . n2. Pralidoxime Nicotinic/Muscle symptoms - regenerates enzyme . Myasthenia Gravis Treatment Pyridostigmine Long-acting oral . Increases ACh at NMJ. Alzheimer s Drugs Donepezil Rivastigmine Galantamine . Increase CNS ACh."}, {"id": "l21", "title": "Lecture 21: Muscarinic Antagonists (Anticholinergics)", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l21 Lecture 21 Muscarinic Antagonists Anticholinergics n n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. n Session Lecture 21 n n 1. Class Overview Slide 7-8 n Mechanism Competitive antagonists or inverse agonists at Muscarinic receptors M1-M5 . n Structure n Tertiary Amines Cross BBB Atropine Scopolamine Benztropine. n Quaternary Ammonium No CNS Ipratropium Tiotropium Glycopyrrolate. n n 2. Clinical Uses Slide 21-23 n Atropine n Bradycardia Blocks vagal tone M2 $ to$ Increases HR. n Antidote For Organophosphate poisoning. n Pre-op Reduces metabolic secretions. n Note Low doses can cause Paradoxical Bradycardia blocks presynaptic M2 autoreceptors . n Scopolamine n Motion Sickness Transdermal patch bypasses first-pass . n Respiratory COPD/Asthma n Ipratropium Short & Tiotropium Long . Block bronchoconstriction + decrease secretions. n Urinary Incontinence Overactive Bladder n Oxybutynin . Relaxes detrusor muscle. n Parkinson s Disease n Benztropine . Treats tremor restores ACh/Dopamine balance . n Ophthalmology n Tropicamide/Cyclopentolate . Induce Mydriasis Dilation & Cycloplegia Paralysis of accommodation for exams. n n 3. Toxicity Anticholinergic Toxidrome Slide 26-28 n Red as a Beet Cutaneous flushing vasodilation to dissipate heat . n Dry as a Bone No sweating Anhidrosis dry mouth. n Hot as Hades Hyperthermia cant sweat . n Blind as a Bat Mydriasis dilated pupils + Cycloplegia blurry near vision . n Mad as a Hatter Delirium Hallucinations Central toxicity . n Full as a Flask Urinary Retention. n Treatment Physostigmine crosses BBB - Use with caution! n n 4. Contraindications Slide 25 n Narrow-Angle Glaucoma Dilation blocks outflow $ to$ Acute crisis. n BPH Prostatic Hyperplasia Worsens urinary retention. n Elderly Risk of falls confusion delirium BEERS criteria . n GI Obstruction/Ileus Worsens constipation. n n 5. Clinical Correlates Slide 31 n n correlate n Drug Interactions n Oxybutynin + Macrolides Erythromycin Macrolides inhibit CYP3A4 . Oxybutynin is metabolized by CYP3A4. Result $ to$ Anticholinergic Toxicity . n n n correlate n Use in Special Populations n Children High risk of Hyperthermia Hot as Hades . n Elderly High risk of Delirium Mad as a Hatter and Falls. Avoid if possible. n Lecture 21 Muscarinic Antagonists Anticholinergics Neuroscience questions A 75-year-old male with benign prostatic hyperplasia BPH takes an over-the-counter cold medication and develops acute urinary retention. Which ingredient likely caused this? Guaifenesin Acetaminophen Diphenhydramine Anticholinergic Ibuprofen Pseudoephedrine 2 Diphenhydramine has strong anticholinergic properties which can precipitate urinary retention in patients with BPH. Why is Atropine contraindicated in patients with Narrow-Angle Glaucoma? It causes miosis It increases aqueous humor production It causes mydriasis which blocks the outflow angle It causes cataract formation It lowers intraocular pressure too much 2 Atropine causes Mydriasis dilation . In narrow-angle glaucoma the bunched-up iris blocks the trabecular meshwork dangerously increasing pressure. To prevent motion sickness a patient applies a Transderm Scop patch. What is the active drug and its mechanism? Scopolamine; Muscarinic Antagonist Scopolamine; Nicotinic Antagonist Meclizine; H1 Antagonist Ondansetron; 5-HT3 Antagonist Metoclopramide; D2 Antagonist 0 Scopolamine is a Muscarinic Antagonist used transdermally for motion sickness prevention. Which drug is used to treat tremors in Parkinson s disease by restoring the balance between Acetylcholine and Dopamine? Atropine Ipratropium Benztropine Oxybutynin Succinylcholine 2 Benztropine is a centrally acting antimuscarinic used to treat Parkinsonian tremors Parkinson s = Low Dopamine + High ACh . A child presents with flushed skin dry mouth dilated pupils and tachycardia after eating berries from a garden plant. The most likely plant is Foxglove Digitalis Jimson Weed Datura/Atropine Oleander Poison Ivy Mushrooms Muscarine 1 Jimson Weed Datura stramonium contains belladonna alkaloids Atropine/Scopolamine causing the classic anticholinergic toxidrome. Anticholinergic Toxidrome Mad as a Hatter Delirium / Hallucinations Toxicology Anticholinergic Toxidrome Blind as a Bat Mydriasis + Cycloplegia Blurred vision Toxicology Drug for Overactive Bladder Oxybutynin Pharmacology Inhaled Muscarinic Antagonist for COPD Tiotropium Long or Ipratropium Short Pharmacology Why Atropine causes Tachycardia Blocks M2 receptors on SA node Removes vagal brake Physiology Mechanism of Paradoxical Bradycardia Blockade of Presynaptic Autoreceptors M2 -> Increased ACh release Physiology Contraindication Narrow Angle Glaucoma Muscarinic Antagonists Atropine Pharmacology Contraindication BPH Muscarinic Antagonists Causes Retention Pharmacology Drug for Motion Sickness Patch Scopolamine Pharmacology Treats Parkinson s Tremor Benztropine Pharmacology SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Parasympathetics > Muscarinic Antagonists FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs BandB Pharmacology > ANS > Muscarinic Antagonists Anticholinergic Toxidrome Red Flush Dry No sweat Hot Hyperthermia Blind Mydriasis Mad Delirium Full Retention . Paradoxical Bradycardia Low dose Atropine blocks Presynaptic M2 autoreceptors $ to$ Increases ACh releases $ to$ Slows Heart. Reversal Agents Atropine reverses AChE Inhibitors. n Physostigmine reverses Atropine. Contraindications Glaucoma Angle Closure BPH Retention Elderly Delirium . Ipratropium/Tiotropium Quaternary amines No CNS . Inhaled for COPD/Asthma ."}, {"id": "l22", "title": "Lecture 22: Ganglionic and Neuromuscular Blockers", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l22 Lecture 22 Ganglionic and Neuromuscular Blockers n n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. n Session Lecture 22 n n 1. Ganglionic Blockers Slide 7-9 n Target Neuronal Nicotinic Receptors Nn at autonomic ganglia. n Mecamylamine n Mechanism Blocks output of BOTH Sympathetic and Parasympathetic systems. n Effect Predictable based on Dominant Tone n Arterioles Symp dominant Vasodilation $ to$ Hypotension Orthostatic . n Heart Para dominant Tachycardia. n Gut/Bladder Para dominant Constipation Urinary Retention. n Eye Para dominant Mydriasis Cycloplegia. n n 2. Neuromuscular Blockers NMBAs Slide 19-23 nTarget Nicotinic Nm receptors at the Neuromuscular Junction NMJ . n n Depolarizing NMBA n Succinylcholine SCh n Structure Two ACh molecules. n Mechanism Agonist. Causes sustained depolarization $ to$ Fasciculations $ to$ Flaccid paralysis Phase I . Desensitization Phase II . n Metabolism Plasma Cholinesterase Pseudocholinesterase . n Duration Very short <8 min . Prolonged in patients with genetic deficiency. n Adverse Effects n Malignant Hyperthermia MH Triggered by SCh + Inhaled Anesthetics e.g. Halothane . Muscle rigidity hyperthermia. Treat with Dantrolene . n Hyperkalemia Life-threatening in Burn/Trauma patients due to receptor upregulation/rhabdomyolysis . n n Non-Depolarizing NMBAs Competitive Antagonists n Isoquinolines n Atracurium Degraded by Hofmann Elimination spontaneous no organ weakness . Side Effect Histamine release Hypotension + Laudanosine accumulation Seizures . n Cisatracurium Hofmann elimination. No histamine. Safe for organ failure. n Aminosteroids n Rocuronium Fast onset can replace SCh for intubation . Biliary excretion. n Vecuronium Renal/Biliary excretion. n n 3. Reversal Slide 27 n Cholinesterase Inhibitors Neostigmine Increase ACh to compete with NMBAs. Always give with Atropine to prevent muscarinic bradycardia. n Sugammadex Specialized chelator. Encapsulates Rocuronium/Vecuronium for rapid reversal. No muscarinic side effects. n n 4. Clinical Correlates n n correlate n Malignant Hyperthermia n Trigger Succinylcholine or Volatile Anesthetics. n Genetic Mutation in Ryanodine Receptor RyR1 . n Pathology Uncontrolled Ca2+ release from SR $ to$ Rigidity $ to$ Heat/Acidosis. n Cure Dantrolene Blocks RyR1 . n n n correlate n Hofmann Elimination n Drug Atracurium / Cisatracurium. n Concept Breaks down spontaneously in plasma based on pH and temperature. n Pearl Ideal for patients with multisystem organ failure Kidney/Liver don t matter . n Lecture 22 Ganglionic and Neuromuscular Blockers Neuroscience questions A patient undergoes surgery and develops muscle rigidity tachycardia and a temperature of 105 u00b0F shortly after induction. Which drug is the most likely trigger? Rocuronium Succinylcholine Cisatracurium Propofol Fentanyl 1 Succinylcholine is a classic trigger for Malignant Hyperthermia along with volatile anesthetics . Which neuromuscular blocker undergoes spontaneous Hofmann elimination and is therefore safe in patients with renal and hepatic failure? Pancuronium Rocuronium Vecuronium Cisatracurium Succinylcholine 3 Cisatracurium and Atracurium degrades spontaneously in plasma Hofmann elimination independent of organ function. What is the mechanism of action of Sugammadex? Inhibits Acetylcholinesterase Blocks Ryanodine Receptors Chelates/Encapsulates Aminosteroid NMBAs Stimulates Nicotinic Receptors Blocks Muscarinic Receptors 2 Sugammadex forms a complex with aminosteroids Rocuronium/Vecuronium to reverse blockade rapidly. A patient with severe burns requires intubation. Which neuromuscular blocker is CONTRAINDICATED due to the risk of hyperkalemia? Rocuronium Cisatracurium Vecuronium Atracurium Succinylcholine 4 Succinylcholine causes depolarization and K+ efflux. In burn patients upregulated receptors this can cause massive hyperkalemia and cardiac arrest. The use of Neostigmine to reverse neuromuscular blockade can cause bradycardia. Which drug is co-administered to prevent this? Pralidoxime Physostigmine Atropine Epinephrine Dantrolene 2 Neostigmine increases ACh everywhere Muscarinic + Nicotinic . Atropine is given to block the Muscarinic effects Bradycardia while allowing Nicotinic effects Muscle strength . Contraindication for Succinylcholine Burns Trauma Hyperkalemia Hx of Malignant Hyperthermia Pharmacology Treatment for Malignant Hyperthermia Dantrolene Pharmacology NMBA metabolized by Plasma Cholinesterase Succinylcholine Pharmacology NMBA eliminated by Hofmann Elimination Cisatracurium / Atracurium Pharmacology Depolarizing Neuromuscular Blocker Succinylcholine Pharmacology Dominant Autonomic Tone Heart Parasympathetic Vagal Physiology Dominant Autonomic Tone Blood Vessels Sympathetic Physiology Sugammadex reverses which drugs? Rocuronium and Vecuronium Pharmacology Why give Atropine with Neostigmine reversal? To prevent Muscarinic side effects Bradycardia Pharmacology Ganglion Blocker Drug Name Mecamylamine Pharmacology SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Parasympathetics > Neuromuscular Blockers FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs BandB Pharmacology > ANS > NMBAs Succinylcholine & Potassium Causes K+ efflux. Contraindicated in Burns Trauma Crush injuries and Denervation risk of Cardiac Arrest . Dantrolene Mechanism Blocks Ryanodine Receptors RyR1 on Sarcoplasmic Reticulum. Treats Malignant Hyperthermia. Hofmann Elimination Atracurium/Cisatracurium . Spontaneous degradation. Safe in renal/hepatic failure. Dominant Tone Concept Ganglionic blockers remove the dominant tone. Vessels = Symp Block $ to$ Hypotension . Heart = Para Block $ to$ Tachycardia . Sugammadex Encapsulates Rocuronium/Vecuronium . Reversal without cholinergic side effects."}, {"id": "l23", "title": "Lecture 23: Pathology of Auditory and Vestibular System", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l23 Lecture 23 Pathology of Auditory and Vestibular System n n Lecturer A. Reno D.O. n Session Lecture 23 n n 1. Approach to Dizziness Slide 6-27 n Vertigo Illusion of movement Spinning . n Peripheral Inner Ear n Nystagmus Unidirectional usually horizontal/rotary . n Fixation Extinguishes nystagmus. n Walking Patient can usually walk. n Hearing Often affected Tinnitus/Loss . n Central Brainstem/Cerebellum - Stroke/MS n Nystagmus Bidirectional or Vertical . n Fixation Does NOT extinguish . n Walking Patient often cannot stand/walk Ataxia . n Hearing Usually normal. n Red Flags Comparison to Stroke HINTS Exam . n n 2. Key Vestibular Pathologies n Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo BPPV n Duration Seconds < 1 minute . n Trigger Head movement Rolling over in bed . n Mechanism Dislodged Otoconia Calcium Carbonate crystals in posterior semicircular canal. n Diagnosis Dix-Hallpike maneuver Rotary nystagmus . n Treatment Epley Maneuver . n n Meniere s Disease Endolymphatic Hydrops n Duration Hours 20 min - 24 hrs . n Classic Triad n 1. Episodic Vertigo . n 2. Tinnitus Roaring . n 3. Sensorineural Hearing Loss Fluctuating low frequency . n Mechanism Excess Endolymph fluid. n Treatment Low salt diet Diuretics HCTZ/Triamterene . n n Vestibulo-Cochlear Infections n Vestibular Neuritis Vertigo only No hearing loss . Duration Days . Usually viral. Rx Corticosteroids. n Labyrinthitis Vertigo + Hearing Loss . Viral or Bacterial. n n 3. Hearing Loss Evaluation Slide 40-47 n Conductive CHL n Anatomy Outer/Middle ear Wax Otitis Media Otosclerosis . n Weber Lateralizes to BAD ear. n Rinne BC > AC Bone Conduction > Air Conduction . n n Sensorineural SNHL n Anatomy Cochlea/Nerve Presbycusis Noise Meniere s Acoustic Neuroma . n Weber Lateralizes to GOOD ear. n Rinne AC > BC Normal pattern but reduced . n n 4. Clinical Correlates n correlate n The HINTS Exam nUsed to distinguish Central Stroke from Peripheral vertigo in acute vestibular syndrome. n1. H ead I mpulse Normal impulse = Central bad . Peripheral has corrective saccade . n2. N ystagmus Direction changing = Central . n3. T est of S kew Vertical Skew = Central . n Pearl INFARCT Impulse Normal Fast-phase Alternating Refixation on Cover Test . n n n correlate n Acoustic Neuroma Vestibular Schwannoma n Unilateral Sensorineural Hearing Loss + Tinnitus. n Slow growing. n Diagnose with MRI . n Lecture 23 Pathology of Auditory and Vestibular System Neuroscience questions A 45-year-old woman complains of room-spinning dizziness that lasts for 30 seconds when she rolls over in bed. It is associated with nausea. What is the most likely diagnosis? Meniere s Disease Vestibular Neuritis Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo BPPV Acoustic Neuroma Multiple Sclerosis 2 BPPV is characterized by brief <1 min episodes of vertigo triggered by head position changes. A patient presents with vertigo tinnitus and hearing loss in the right ear. The episodes last for about 4 hours. Which treatment is most appropriate for long-term management? Epley Maneuver Antibiotics Low salt diet and Diuretics Carbamazepine Chemotherapy 2 The patient has Meniere s Disease Triad + Duration . Treatment involves reducing endolymph fluid with salt restriction and diuretics . Weber test lateralizes to the LEFT ear. Rinne test shows Bone Conduction > Air Conduction on the LEFT. What is the diagnosis? Right Sensorineural Hearing Loss Right Conductive Hearing Loss Left Sensorineural Hearing Loss Left Conductive Hearing Loss Normal Hearing 3 Weber to the Left + Rinne BC > AC on Left = Left Conductive Hearing Loss . In CHL bone conduction is better than air and it lateralizes to the affected ear . Which finding on physical exam strongly suggests a CENTRAL cause of vertigo e.g. Stroke ? Nystagmus that suppresses with fixation Unidirectional horizontal nystagmus Positive Head Impulse Test Corrective Saccade Vertical Nystagmus Hearing Loss 3 Vertical nystagmus or direction-changing nystagmus is a red flag for Central pathology. Peripheral nystagmus is usually horizontal/rotary and suppresses with fixation. A patient has sudden onset of severe vertigo lasting 3 days with nausea and vomiting but NO hearing loss. Findings include horizontal nystagmus. What is the most likely diagnosis? Labyrinthitis Vestibular Neuritis Meniere s Disease BPPV Otosclerosis 1 Vestibular Neuritis presents with continuous vertigo for days often post-viral with preserved hearing . Labyrinthitis would include hearing loss . Diagnosis Vertigo < 1 minute triggered by movement BPPV Benign Paroxysmal Positional Vertigo ENT Diagnosis Vertigo Hours + Tinnitus + Hearing Loss Meniere s Disease ENT Diagnosis Vertigo Days + No Hearing Loss Vestibular Neuritis ENT Diagnosis Vertigo Days + Hearing Loss Labyrinthitis ENT Weber Test Lateralization Conductive Loss Lateralizes to the BAD affected ear Diagnosis Weber Test Lateralization Sensorineural Loss Lateralizes to the GOOD unaffected ear Diagnosis Maneuver to Diagnose BPPV Dix-Hallpike Diagnosis Maneuver to Treat BPPV Epley Maneuver Treatment Mechanism of Meniere s Disease Endolymphatic Hydrops Excess fluid in inner ear Pathology Central vs Peripheral Nystagmus Fixation Peripheral suppresses with fixation. Central does NOT. Diagnosis AMBOSS Neurology > Vertigo and Dizziness FirstAid Neurology > Otology BandB Neurology > The Ear Vertigo Duration Clues < 1 min BPPV. n Hours Meniere s Disease. n Days Vestibular Neuritis / Labyrinthitis. Peripheral vs. Central Nystagmus Peripheral Extinguishes with fixation. Horizontal/Rotary. n Central Does NOT extinguish. Vertical or Direction-Changing. Meniere s Triad 1. Episodic Vertigo n2. Tinnitus Roaring n3. Sensorineural Hearing Loss. Weber/Rinne Rules Conductive Bad ear hears Weber louder. BC > AC. n Sensorineural Good ear hears Weber louder. AC > BC. Treatment of BPPV Epley Maneuver Repositioning of otoconia ."}, {"id": "l24", "title": "Lecture 24: Olfaction and Taste", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l24 Lecture 24 Olfaction and Taste n n Lecturer G. Otazu Ph.D. n Session Lecture 24 n n 1. Olfaction Smell n Receptors Bipolar neurons in olfactory epithelium. n Turnover Every 7 days stem cells . n Receptors GPCRs. n Pathway n 1. Olfactory Nerves CN I pass through Cribriform Plate of Ethmoid bone. n 2. Synapse in Glomeruli of Olfactory Bulb. n 3. Mitral/Tufted Cells Second order form Olfactory Tract. n 4. Project directly to Primary Olfactory Cortex Piriform Cortex Amygdala Entorhinal Cortex. n Note Olfaction is the ONLY sense that bypasses the Thalamus before reaching the cortex. n n 2. Taste Gustation n Modalities n Salty Na+ influx Amiloride-sensitive channels . n Sour H+ ions Block K+ channels . n Sweet/Bitter/Umami GPCRs G-protein coupled . n Innervation n Anterior 2/3 CN VII Facial - Chorda Tympani . n Posterior 1/3 CN IX Glossopharyngeal . n Epiglottis/Pharynx CN X Vagus . n Pathway n 1. Nerves project to Nucleus Tractus Solitarius NTS in Medulla. n 2. Project to VPM of Thalamus. n 3. Project to Gustatory Cortex Insula/Frontal Operculum . n n 3. Clinical Correlates n correlate n Kallmann Syndrome n Triad Anosmia + Delayed Puberty Hypogonadism . n Mechanism Failure of GnRH neurons to migrate from olfactory placode to hypothalamus. n n n correlate n Foster-Kennedy Syndrome n Cause Meningioma of the Olfactory Groove. n Triad n 1. Ipsilateral Anosmia Tumor compresses olfactory tract . n 2. Ipsilateral Optic Atrophy Tumor compresses Optic Nerve . n 3. Contralateral Papilledema Increased intracranial pressure . n n n correlate n Uncinate Fits Temporal Lobe Epilepsy n Seizures originating in the Uncus Medial Temporal Lobe . n Aura Hallucination of foul smells Cacosmia . n n n correlate n Neurodegenerative Diseases n Parkinson s / Alzheimer s Hyposmia decreased smell is often the earliest clinical sign appearing years before motor or cognitive symptoms. n Lecture 24 Olfaction and Taste Neuroscience questions A 16-year-old male presents with delayed puberty. Physical exam reveals small testes and a complete inability to smell since birth. What is the most likely diagnosis? Klinefelter Syndrome Kallmann Syndrome Foster-Kennedy Syndrome Turner Syndrome Uncinate Epilepsy 1 Kallmann Syndrome is characterized by hypogonadotropic hypogonadism delayed puberty and anosmia due to migration failure of GnRH neurons. A patient presents with seizures preceded by a hallucination of a foul smell burning rubber . Where is the seizure focus likely located? Postcentral Gyrus Occipital Lobe Uncus Temporal Lobe Frontal Lobe Cerebellum 2 Uncinate fits are temporal lobe seizures involving the Uncus primary olfactory cortex area resulting in olfactory hallucinations Cacosmia . Which cranial nerve is responsible for taste sensation on the anterior two-thirds of the tongue? Trigeminal V Facial VII Glossopharyngeal IX Vagus X Hypoglossal XII 1 The Facial Nerve CN VII carries taste from the anterior 2/3 of the tongue via the Chorda Tympani . A 55-year-old woman presents with loss of smell in her right ear and vision loss in her right eye. Fundoscopy reveals right optic atrophy and left papilledema. What is the most likely diagnosis? Pituitary Adenoma Foster-Kennedy Syndrome Multiple Sclerosis Occipital Stroke Glioblastoma Multiforme 1 Foster-Kennedy Syndrome Olfactory Groove Meningioma causes ipsilateral anosmia and optic atrophy direct compression and contralateral papilledema ICP . Loss of olfaction is often an early prodromal sign of which neurodegenerative disorder? Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis ALS Parkinson s Disease Huntington s Disease Multiple Sclerosis Myasthenia Gravis 1 Hyposmia loss of smell frequently precedes motor symptoms in Parkinson s Disease and Alzheimer s by several years. Diagnosis Anosmia + No Puberty Kallmann Syndrome Neurology Diagnosis Ipsilateral Anosmia/Optic Atrophy + Contralateral Papilledema Foster-Kennedy Syndrome Neurology CN for Taste Anterior 2/3 Tongue CN VII Facial Anatomy CN for Taste Posterior 1/3 Tongue CN IX Glossopharyngeal Anatomy Taste Modalities using Ion Channels Salty Na+ and Sour H+ Physiology Taste Modalities using GPCRs Sweet Bitter Umami Physiology Seizure aura with foul smell Cacosmia Uncinate Fit Temporal Lobe/Uncus Neurology Unique feature of Olfactory Pathway Does not relay in Thalamus before Cortex Anatomy Primary Olfactory Cortex location Piriform Cortex Temporal Lobe Anatomy Thalamic Nucleus for Taste VPM Ventral Posteromedial Anatomy AMBOSS Neurology > Cranial Nerves FirstAid Neurology > Otology/Ophthalmology BandB Neurology > The Chemical Senses Olfactory Pathway Uniqueness Olfaction is the only sensory modality that projects directly to the cortex without relaying through the Thalamus first. Kallmann Syndrome Teenager with No Smell + No Puberty . GnRH neuron migration failure . Foster-Kennedy Syndrome Olfactory Groove Meningioma Ipsilateral Anosmia/Optic Atrophy + Contralateral Papilledema . Taste Innervation Anterior 2/3 = CN VII . nPosterior 1/3 = CN IX . nThroat = CN X . Taste Transduction Salty/Sour = Ion Channels . nSweet/Bitter/Umami = GPCRs ."}, {"id": "l25", "title": "Lecture 25: Visual System - Anatomy and Pathways", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l25 Lecture 25 Visual System - Anatomy and Pathways n n Lecturer G. Otazu Ph.D. n Session Lecture 25 n n 1. Eye Anatomy & Physiology n Glaucoma Increased Intraocular Pressure IOP . n Open-Angle Slow drainage Trabecular meshwork . Chronic. n Closed-Angle Iris blocks drainage angle. Emergency Pain Red eye Haloes . n Accommodation n Near Vision Ciliary Muscle Contracts $ to$ Zonules Relax $ to$ Lens becomes Rounder . n Photoreceptors n Rods High sensitivity Night Peripheral retina. n Cones Color Acuity Concentrated in Fovea . n n 2. Visual Pathway & Lesions Slide 26-30 n1. Optic Nerve Monocular blindness. n2. Optic Chiasm Decussation of Nasal fibers Temporal visual field . n Lesion Bitemporal Hemianopsia Tunnel Vision . n Cause Pituitary Adenoma . n3. Optic Tract Contralateral Homonymous Hemianopsia. n4. Optic Radiations n Meyer s Loop Temporal Lobe Carries Superior field information. Lesion Pie in the Sky Contralateral Superior Quadrantanopia . n Dorsal Radiation Parietal Lobe Carries Inferior field information. Lesion Pie on the Floor Contralateral Inferior Quadrantanopia . n5. Visual Cortex PCA Supply n Lesion Contralateral Homonymous Hemianopsia with Macular Sparing . n Why Sparing? Macula has dual blood supply PCA + MCA . n n 3. Higher Order Processing Slide 37-44 n Dorsal Stream Where? Projects to Parietal Lobe . n Function Motion Spatial relationships. n Lesion Right Parietal Hemispatial Neglect Ignores left side of world . n Ventral Stream What? Projects to Temporal Lobe . n Function Color Form Faces. n Lesion Prosopagnosia Face blindness Achromatopsia No color . n n 4. Clinical Correlates n correlate n Papilledema n Swelling of the optic disc due to increased Intracranial Pressure ICP . n The optic nerve is surrounded by meninges/CSF so ICP is transmitted directly to it. n n n correlate n Hemispatial Neglect n Lesion Right Posterior Parietal Cortex . n Presentation Patient ignores the Left side of their body/world e.g. eats only right half of plate draws half a clock . n Note Left parietal lesions rarely cause neglect because the Right hemisphere attends to both sides while the Left only attends to the Right. n Lecture 25 Visual System - Anatomy and Pathways Neuroscience questions A patient presents with tunnel vision bumping into objects on their periphery. MRI reveals a mass compressing the optic chiasm. What is the diagnosis? Right Homonymous Hemianopsia Left Homonymous Hemianopsia Bitemporal Hemianopsia Superior Quadrantanopia Macular Degeneration 2 Compression of the Optic Chiasm e.g. Pituitary Adenoma affects crossing nasal fibers leading to loss of both temporal visual fields Bitemporal Hemianopsia . A patient who suffered a stroke can see perfectly well but cannot recognize the faces of his family members. Where is the lesion located? Primary Visual Cortex V1 Parietal Lobe Dorsal Stream Temporal Lobe Ventral Stream Frontal Eye Fields Cerebellum 2 Prosopagnosia face blindness results from damage to the Ventral Stream What pathway in the inferior temporal lobe Fusiform Gyrus . To view a near object accommodation which of the following occurs? Ciliary muscle relaxes Zonules tighten Lens flattens Ciliary muscle contracts Zonules tighten Lens rounds Ciliary muscle contracts Zonules relax Lens rounds Ciliary muscle relaxes Zonules relax Lens flattens Pupil dilates 2 For near vision Ciliary muscle contracts moves closer to lens causing zonule fibers to relax slacken allowing the lens to spring into a rounder shape. A patient has a Pie in the Sky visual field defect Right Superior Quadrantanopia . Where is the lesion? Right Parietal Lobe Left Parietal Lobe Right Temporal Lobe Meyer s Loop Left Temporal Lobe Meyer s Loop Optic Chiasm 3 A Right visual field defect implies a Left sided lesion. Pie in the Sky Superior implies Meyer s Loop Temporal Lobe . So Left Temporal Lobe . Which finding distinguishes an Occipital Lobe stroke PCA territory from an Optic Tract lesion? Homonymous Hemianopsia Macular Sparing Pupillary Light Reflex defect Color blindness Bitemporal loss 1 Macular Sparing is characteristic of cortical Occipital/PCA strokes because the macula receives collateral blood supply from the MCA. Optic tract lesions do not spare the macula. Visual Defect Pituitary Adenoma Bitemporal Hemianopsia Pathology Visual Defect Right Optic Tract Lesion Left Homonymous Hemianopsia Pathology Visual Defect Left Temporal Lobe Meyer s Loop Right Superior Quadrantanopia Pie in the Sky Pathology Visual Defect Right Parietal Lobe Left Inferior Quadrantanopia Pie on the Floor Pathology Dorsal Stream function Where pathway Motion Location - Parietal Lobe Physiology Ventral Stream function What pathway Color Faces Form - Temporal Lobe Physiology Lesion causing Hemispatial Neglect Right Parietal Lobe Pathology Lesion causing Prosopagnosia Inferior Temporal Lobe Ventral Stream Pathology Papilledema indicates... Increased Intracranial Pressure ICP Pathology Glaucoma type that is a medical emergency Closed-Angle Glaucoma Pathology AMBOSS Neurology > Visual Pathways FirstAid Neurology > Ophthalmology BandB Neurology > Vision Pituitary Adenoma Defect Bitemporal Hemianopsia Tunnel Vision . Compresses Optic Chiasm. Meyer s Loop Lesion Temporal Lobe lesion $ to$ Pie in the Sky Contralateral Superior Quadrantanopia . Macular Sparing Hallmark of PCA Stroke Occipital cortex . Peripheral vision lost central macular vision preserved due to MCA collaterals. Accommodation Mechanism Ciliary Muscle Contracts $ to$ Zonules Relax $ to$ Lens Rounds . Hemispatial Neglect Damage to Right Parietal Lobe Dorsal Stream . Patient neglects Left side of world."}, {"id": "l26", "title": "Lecture 26: Neurologic Exam Part 1 - Cranial Nerves & Mental Status", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l26 Lecture 26 Neurologic Exam Part 1 - Cranial Nerves & Mental Status n n Lecturer L. Mark D.O. n Session Lecture 26 n n 1. Mental Status & History n Mental Status Exam Appearance Behavior Speech Mood Cognition. n Glasgow Coma Scale GCS n Score 3 Coma to 15 Normal . n Components E ye opening V erbal response M otor response. EVM-456 . n n 2. Cranial Nerve Examination n CN I Olfactory n Test Smell Coffee Cloves one nostril at a time. n Deficit Anosmia. DDx COVID-19 Kallmann Syndrome Trauma . n n CN II Optic n Visual Acuity Snellen Chart 20/20 . n Visual Fields Confrontation test Static finger wiggle . detects Hemianopsia. n Fundoscopy n Red Reflex Orange glow. n Optic Disc Check for Papilledema blurred margins or Cupping Glaucoma . n n CN III IV VI Oculomotor Trochlear Abducens n Pupils CN III n Direct Constriction in illuminated eye. n Consensual Constriction in opposite eye. n Near Triad Convergence Constriction Accommodation. n EOM H-Pattern. n CN VI Deficit Eye cannot look out Lateral deviation failure . n CN III Deficit Eye is Down and Out + Ptosis + Dilated Pupil. n n CN V Trigeminal n Sensory V1 Opthalmic V2 Maxillary V3 Mandibular - Pinprick/Light touch. n Motor Muscles of Mastication Temporalis/Masseter clench . n Corneal Reflex Afferent V1 Senses touch $ to$ Efferent VII Blink . n n CN VII Facial n Motor Facial expression Smile Frown Puff cheeks Close eyes tight . n Central Lesion Stroke Spares forehead. n Peripheral Lesion Bell s Palsy Affects entire half of face Forehead + Lower . n Sensory Taste Anterior 2/3. n n CN VIII Vestibulocochlear n Gross Hearing Whisper test / Finger rub. n Weber Test Tuning fork on vertex. n Lateralizes to BAD ear Conductive Loss. n Lateralizes to GOOD ear Sensorineural Loss. n Rinne Test Mastoid BC vs. Air AC . n AC > BC Normal or Sensorineural. n BC > AC Conductive Loss. n n CN IX X Glossopharyngeal Vagus n Palate Say Ah . Uvula deviates AWAY from lesion. n Gag Reflex Sensory IX Motor X. n n CN XI Accessory n Motor Shrug shoulders Trapezius Turn head SCM . n n CN XII Hypoglossal n Motor Protrude tongue. Tongue deviates TOWARDS lesion Licks the lesion . Lecture 26 Neurologic Exam Part 1 - Cranial Nerves & Mental Status Neuroscience questions A patient presents with right-sided hearing loss. Weber test lateralizes to the RIGHT ear. Rinne test shows Bone Conduction > Air Conduction on the right. What is the diagnosis? Right Sensorineural Hearing Loss Right Conductive Hearing Loss Left Sensorineural Hearing Loss Left Conductive Hearing Loss Normal Hearing 1 Weber lateralizing to the affected bad ear + Rinne BC > AC indicates a Conductive Hearing Loss in that ear. On physical exam a patient s tongue deviates to the LEFT when protruded. Which cranial nerve is lesioned? Right CN XII Left CN XII Right CN X Left CN X Left CN IX 1 The tongue deviates towards the side of the CN XII lesion weak side . Left deviation = Left CN XII lesion. When asking a patient to say Ah the uvula deviates to the RIGHT. This suggests a lesion of which cranial nerve? Right CN X Left CN X Right CN IX Left CN IX Right CN XII 1 The uvula deviates away from the CN X lesion pulled by the intact strong side . Right deviation = Left CN X lesion. Which component of the corneal reflex is carried by the Facial Nerve CN VII ? Sensory Afferent Pain Sensory Afferent Touch Motor Efferent Blink Lacrimation only None 2 CN V1 provides the sensory Afferent limb. CN VII provides the Motor Efferent limb to close the eyelid Orbicularis Oculi . A patient has a dilated right pupil that is unresponsive to light and the right eye is positioned down and out . Which cranial nerve is affected? CN II CN III CN IV CN VI CN VII 1 These are classic signs of a CN III Oculomotor Palsy Ptosis Mydriasis dilation and Down-and-Out deviation. Tongue Deviation Direction Towards the Lesion CN XII Neurology Uvula Deviation Direction Away from the Lesion CN X Neurology Corneal Reflex Afferent Limb CN V1 Ophthalmic Neurology Corneal Reflex Efferent Limb CN VII Facial Neurology Rinne Test Conductive Loss Result BC > AC Bone Conduction > Air Conduction Ent CN III Palsy Signs Down and Out eye Ptosis Dilated Pupil Neurology Glasgow Coma Scale Components Eyes 4 Verbal 5 Motor 6 Emergency Test for CN XI Shoulder Shrug Trapezius & Head Turn SCM Neurology CN VII Test Facial Expressions Smile Frown Puff Cheeks Neurology Weber Test Lateralization Sensorineural To the GOOD ear Ent AMBOSS Neurology > Cranial Nerves FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Cranial Nerves BandB Neurology > The Brainstem > Cranial Nerves Tongue Deviation CN XII Lesion Tongue deviates TOWARDS the side of the lesion Licks the lesion . Uvula Deviation CN X Lesion Uvula deviates AWAY from the side of the lesion pulled by the strong side . Corneal Reflex Afferent CN V1 Sensation . n Efferent CN VII Blink . Rinne Interpretation BC > AC = Conductive Hearing Loss Abnormal . n AC > BC = Normal or Sensorineural . GCS Scoring EVM-456 . nEyes 4 Verbal 5 Motor 6 . nMax 15 Min 3."}, {"id": "l27", "title": "Lecture 27: OMM Lab 17 Prep - Pelvic Counterstrain & HVLA", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l27 Lecture 27 OMM Lab 17 Prep - Pelvic Counterstrain & HVLA n n Lecturer T. Li D.O. n Session Lecture 27 n n 1. Counterstrain CS Overview n Definition Indirect Passive technique. Fold and Hold . n Mechanism Shortens muscle to silence proprioceptive feedback Muscle Spindles/Gamma Gain . n Safety Very gentle. Good for acute pain. n Reaction 30-50% experience soreness for 1-2 days Warn patient! . n n 2. Steps of Counterstrain n1. Find Point Locate tenderpoint. n2. Scale Call it 10/10 . n3. Position Move to position of ease reduce to 0/10 or $ le$ 3/10 . n4. Hold 90 Seconds . n5. Return Passive return to neutral Slowly! . n6. Reassess Check tenderness again. n n 3. Pelvic Counterstrain Points n Iliacus Deep in iliac fossa. n Tx Flexion + External Rotation Frog Leg . n Psoas Medial to ASIS. n Tx F-S T -R A Flex Sidebend Toward Rotate Away . n Inguinal Lateral to pubic tubercle. n Tx F-ADD-IR Flex Adduct Internal Rotate . n Low Ilium Superior pubic ramus. n Tx Flexion. n n 4. Innominate HVLA n Diagnosis Reminder n Anterior Rotation ASIS Low PSIS High. n Posterior Rotation ASIS High PSIS Low. n Superior Shear All landmarks high. n HVLA Setup n Post. Rotation Extend the hip to rotate anteriorly . n Ant. Rotation Flex the hip to rotate posteriorly . n Superior Shear Traction leg Abduct + IR + Pull . n n 5. Clinical Correlates n correlate n Counterstrain Contraindications n Absolute No Somatic Dysfunction No Consent Lack of Cooperation. n Relative Severe Osteoporosis Acute Illness Instability. n Note Recent trauma or fracture would depend on location but Osteoporosis is the classic relative contraindication. n Lecture 27 OMM Lab 17 Prep - Pelvic Counterstrain & HVLA Neuroscience questions A patient feels soreness the day after a counterstrain treatment. How should this be managed? Immediate ER referral Repeat the treatment immediately Reassurance and hydration Normal reaction Prescribe Opioids Apply Heat only 2 Post-treatment soreness occurs in 30-50% of patients and lasts 1-2 days. It is a normal reaction due to metabolic release. Reassurance and water are key. What is an ABSOLUTE contraindication to Counterstrain? Osteoporosis Acute Pain Pregnancy Refusal of treatment No Consent Pediatric patient 3 Lack of consent and absence of somatic dysfunction are the main Absolute contraindications. Osteoporosis is a Relative contraindication. For a Psoas tenderpoint medial to ASIS what is the correct treatment position? Extension Sidebend Away Rotate Toward Flexion Sidebend Away Rotate Toward Flexion Sidebend Toward Rotate Away Extension only Flexion only 2 Psoas = F-ST-RA . Flexion Sidebend Toward the point Rotate Away from the point. Which finding suggests a Posterior Innominate Rotation? ASIS Low PSIS High ASIS High PSIS Low ASIS Low PSIS Low ASIS High PSIS High Pubic Shear only 1 Posterior rotation pulls the ASIS up High and pushes the PSIS down Low . What is the goal pain score reduction during Counterstrain positioning? Reduce by 10% Reduce to 5/10 Reduce to 7/10 Reduce to 0/10 or at least <= 3/10 Increase pain to provoke release 3 The goal is 100% reduction 0/10 but any reduction to 3/10 or less is considered acceptable to proceed with the hold. Psoas Counterstrain Setup F-ST-RA Flex SB Toward Rot Away OMM Iliacus Counterstrain Setup Frog Leg Flex + External Rotation OMM Inguinal Counterstrain Setup F-ADD-IR Flex Adduct Int Rotate OMM Counterstrain Hold Time 90 Seconds OMM Counterstrain Mechanism Silent Muscle Spindles Gamma Gain OMM Anterior Innominate Diagnosis ASIS Low PSIS High OMM Posterior Innominate Diagnosis ASIS High PSIS Low OMM Absolute Contraindication to CS No Consent / No Dysfunction OMM Relative Contraindication to CS Osteoporosis / Severe Illness OMM Post-CS Soreness Duration 1-2 Days Normal OMM BandB Musculoskeletal > Spine FirstAid Musculoskeletal > Anatomy Psoas Counterstrain Position F-S T -R A n F lexed S idebent T oward R otated A way. Counterstrain Hold Time 90 Seconds is the standard hold time for Counterstrain. Inguinal Counterstrain F-ADD-IR n F lexed ADD ucted I nternally R otated. Common crossing legs position . Anterior Innominate Findings ASIS Lower PSIS Higher on the affected side. Posterior Innominate Findings ASIS Higher PSIS Lower on the affected side."}, {"id": "l28", "title": "Lecture 28: Anatomy & Physiology of the Neocortex", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l28 Lecture 28 Anatomy & Physiology of the Neocortex n n Lecturer R. Ramos Ph.D. n Session Lecture 28 n n 1. Frontal Lobe & Executive Function n Functions Planning Inhibition Personality Filter Social norms . n Pathology n Frontotemporal Dementia Pick s Disease n Gene MAPT mutation Tau protein . n Symptoms Disinhibition Apathy Personality changes e.g. gambling rude comments early onset 45-65y . n Classic Case Phineas Gage Iron rod through frontal lobe . n Frontal Eye Fields Area 8 Controls biological/voluntary saccades rapid eye movements to contralateral side. n n 2. Parietal Lobe & Attention n Right Parietal Lobe Non-Dominant n Function Spatial attention to BOTH sides of the world. n Lesion Right MCA Stroke Hemispatial Neglect . Patient interacts only with the Right side eats half plate draws half clock . n Left Parietal Lobe Dominant - Angular Gyrus n Lesion Gerstmann Syndrome . n Tetrad n 1. Agraphia Can t write . n 2. Acalculia Can t do math . n 3. Finger Agnosia Can t ID fingers . n 4. Left-Right Disorientation . n n 3. Temporal Lobe & Recognition n Fusiform Face Area FFA Inferior temporal gyrus. n Function Facial recognition. n Lesion Prosopagnosia Face Blindness . n Medial Temporal Lobe MTL Hippocampus/Memory. n Alzheimer s Tau tangles Amyloid plaques. Atrophy of MTL. n n 4. Insula & Cingulate n Insula Buried deep in lateral sulcus. n Functions Gustatory Taste Interoception feeling inside body Disgust Addiction/Craving Urge to smoke . n Stimulation Elicits taste sensations or disgust . n Cingulate Gyrus Part of Limbic System. n Cingulotomy Surgical lesioning used for intractable Chronic Pain or severe OCD . n n 5. Histology Highlights n Betz Cells Giant pyramidal cells in Layer 5 of Primary Motor Cortex Output to Corticospinal tract . n Cytoarchitecture Neocortex has 6 layers. Layer 4 = Input Sensory Layer 5 = Output Motor . Lecture 28 Anatomy & Physiology of the Neocortex Neuroscience questions A 55-year-old man presents with significant personality changes. He has become sexually inappropriate with strangers and has lost his life savings gambling. He has no memory loss. Genetic testing reveals a MAPT mutation. What is the diagnosis? Alzheimer s Disease Frontotemporal Dementia Behavioral Variant Huntington s Disease Vascular Dementia Parkinson s Disease 1 The combination of Disinhibition gambling inappropriate behavior + Personality change + MAPT mutation + Early onset 55y points to Frontotemporal Dementia . A patient cannot distinguish their left hand from their right hand and has lost the ability to perform simple addition. Where is the lesion most likely located? Right Parietal Lobe Left Inferior Parietal Lobe Angular Gyrus Right Temporal Lobe Left Frontal Lobe Occipital Lobe 1 This is Gerstmann Syndrome Acalculia L-R confusion . The lesion is in the dominant usually Left Angular Gyrus Inferior Parietal Lobule . Electrical stimulation of which brain region is most likely to elicit a sensation of bad taste or disgust? Primary Motor Cortex Fusiform Gyrus Insular Cortex Broca s Area Wernicke s Area 2 The Insula processes Gustatory taste and visceral information including feelings of disgust . A patient recovering from a stroke combs only the right side of their hair and eats only food on the right side of their tray. Which artery was likely occluded? Left MCA Right MCA Left ACA Right ACA Basilar Artery 1 This is Hemispatial Neglect ignoring the Left side . The lesion is in the Right Parietal Lobe supplied by the Right MCA . Damage to Frontal Area 8 would most likely result in a deficit of Language comprehension Face recognition Voluntary saccadic eye movements Hearing Fine motor control of the hand 2 Frontal Area 8 corresponds to the Frontal Eye Fields which control voluntary rapid eye movements saccades . Role of Insula Taste Gustatory & Disgust / Interoception Cortex Lesion causing Hemispatial Neglect Right Posterior Parietal Lobe Right MCA Neurology Lesion causing Gerstmann Syndrome Left Angular Gyrus Dominant Parietal Neurology Symptoms of Gerstmann Syndrome Agraphia Acalculia Finger Agnosia L-R Disorientation Neurology Gene associated with Frontotemporal Dementia MAPT Tau protein Pathology Symptoms of Frontotemporal Dementia Disinhibition Apathy Personality Change Pathology Function of Fusiform Face Area Facial Recognition Lesion = Prosopagnosia Cortex Function of Frontal Eye Fields Area 8 Voluntary Saccades Physiology Layer of Neocortex with Betz Cells Layer 5 Output to Spinal Cord Histology Cingulotomy is a treatment for... Chronic Pain or OCD Psychiatry AMBOSS Neurology > Neuroanatomy > Cerebral Cortex FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Lobes BandB Neurology > Cerebrum Gerstmann Syndrome Tetrad Lesion of Left Angular Gyrus . n1. Agraphia n2. Acalculia n3. Finger Agnosia n4. L-R Disorientation Hemispatial Neglect Lesion of Right Parietal Lobe Non-dominant . nPatient neglects the Left side of the world. Frontotemporal Dementia FTD Behavioral variant. Disinhibition Apathy Personality change. n MAPT gene Tau . Insula Functions Taste Disgust Interoception Addiction Cravings Smoking . Prosopagnosia Face blindness. Lesion of Fusiform Face Area Temporal Lobe ."}, {"id": "l29", "title": "Lecture 29: Neocortical Development", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l29 Lecture 29 Neocortical Development n n Lecturer R. Ramos Ph.D. n Session Lecture 29 n n 1. The 3 P s of Brain Development n1. Production Proliferation Making neurons. n2. Placement Migration Moving neurons to correct layers. n3. Performance Synaptic function. n n 2. Production Proliferation n Location Ventricular Zone VZ . n Mechanism Radial Glial cells divide to produce neurons. n Pathology n Primary Microcephaly Small brain . Reduced proliferation. n Genes ASPM Abnormal Spindle-like Microcephaly Citron Kinase . n Hemi-megalencephaly One hemisphere is too large. excess proliferation. Risk of Epilepsy . n n 3. Placement Migration n Pattern Inside-Out . Oldest neurons are deep Layer 6 Youngest are superficial Layer 2 . n Guidance Radial Glia fibers guide neurons. Reelin secreted by Cajal-Retzius cells in Layer 1 tells them when to stop. n Pathology neuronal migration disorders n Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia PVH Neurons fail to leave VZ. Nodules lining ventricles. n Gene Filamin A . n Subcortical Band Heterotopia Double Cortex Neurons stuck in white matter Intermediate Zone . n Genes DCX LIS1 . n Lissencephaly Smooth Brain No gyri Agyria . n Gene Reelin mutations. n Molecular Layer Heterotopia Neurons migrate too far past Layer 2 into Layer 1 . Associated with Dyslexia and Epilepsy . n n 4. Gyrification n Timeline Large fissures Sylvian form first 14w . Frontal gyri form last. Lecture 29 Neocortical Development Neuroscience questions A newborn MRI shows a smooth brain surface with no gyri or sulci. What is the most likely diagnosis? Hemi-megalencephaly Lissencephaly Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia Double Cortex Syndrome Porencephaly 1 Lissencephaly literally means Smooth Brain . It is a severe migration disorder where gyri fail to form Agyria . Which cell type serves as the scaffold for neuronal migration in the developing neocortex? Astrocytes Oligodendrocytes Radial Glia Microglia Schwann Cells 2 Radial Glia extend processes from the ventricle to the pial surface acting as train tracks for migrating neurons. An MRI reveals nodules of gray matter lining the lateral ventricles. Which gene mutation is most strongly associated with this finding? Reelin Filamin A DCX ASPM MAPT 1 This describes Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia PVH . The classic association is Filamin A an actin-binding protein needed for migration initiation . In the Inside-Out pattern of cortical development which layer is formed LAST? Layer 6 Layer 5 Layer 4 Layer 2 Subplate 3 Deep layers 6 form first. Superficial layers 2 form last. Layer 1 is the marginal zone and exists early but the cortical plate neurons fill in 6->2 . Molecular Layer Heterotopia neurons migrating too far is often found in the brains of patients with Parkinson s Disease Dyslexia ALS Huntington s Disease Multiple Sclerosis 1 Small clusters of neurons in Layer 1 Molecular Layer Heterotopia are seen in Dyslexia and Epilepsy . Inside-Out Migration meaning Deep layers 6 formed first Superficial 2 formed last Embryology Radial Glia function Progenitor cells + Scaffold for migration Embryology Gene for Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia Filamin A Pathology Gene for Double Cortex Syndrome DCX / LIS1 Pathology Gene for Microcephaly ASPM / Citron Kinase Pathology Cells that secrete Reelin Cajal-Retzius Cells Layer 1 Embryology Lissencephaly means... Smooth Brain Agyria Pathology Molecular Layer Heterotopia association Dyslexia / Epilepsy Pathology Ventricular Zone function Site of neuronal proliferation Embryology 3 P s of Brain Dev Production Placement Performance Embryology AMBOSS Pediatrics > Neurology > Congenital anomalies FirstAid Neurology > Embryology Inside-Out Migration Neocortex forms Inside-Out . Layer 6 is born first. Layer 2 is born last. Double Cortex Syndrome Also called Subcortical Band Heterotopia . A band of gray matter is stuck in the white matter between the cortex and ventricle. Reelin Function Secreted by Cajal-Retzius cells in the Marginal Zone Layer 1 . Acts as a Stop signal for migrating neurons. Primary Microcephaly Genes ASPM and Citron Kinase . Defects in mitosis/cytokinesis. Filamin A Mutation Causes Periventricular Nodular Heterotopia Neurons stuck at the starting line ."}, {"id": "l3", "title": "Lecture #3: Neuroanatomy: Meninges, BBB, CSF, Ventricles", "module": "Neuroanatomy", "content": "l3 Lecture 3 Neuroanatomy Meninges BBB CSF Ventricles n n Lecturers Vladimir Grubisic M.D. Ph.D. & Vincy Mathew n Session Neuroanatomy n n 1. Central Nervous System Barriers Slides 6-9 nThe CNS is an immunologically privileged site protected from the fluctuating chemical environment of the blood by three major barriers n n Blood-Brain Barrier BBB n Location Capillaries within the brain parenchyma. n Structure n Endothelial Cells The physical barrier connected by Tight Junctions Zona Occludens . n Pericytes Contractile cells that wrap around endothelial cells. n Astrocyte End-feet Induce and maintain the barrier properties. n Function Highly selective. n Permeable Lipid-soluble substances O2 CO2 Ethanol Steroid hormones . n Impermeable Polar/Large molecules Glucose Amino acids Dopamine require specific transporters. n n correlate n Parkinson s Disease & Levodopa Slide 12 n Problem Parkinson s is due to dopamine depletion. Dopamine is polar and cannot cross the BBB . n Solution Levodopa L-DOPA is a precursor that can cross the BBB via amino acid transporters. Once inside neurons it is converted to dopamine by DOPA decarboxylase. n n n Other Barriers n Blood-CSF Barrier Formed by Tight Junctions between the Epithelial Cells of the Choroid Plexus . n Arachnoid Barrier Avascular arachnoid epithelium seals the CSF from the extracellular fluid of the dura. n n Circumventricular Organs CVOs Slide 14-16 nSpecific brain regions that lack a BBB . Their capillaries are fenestrated to allow communication between the blood and brain for sensory or secretory functions. n Sensory CVOs n Area Postrema Located in the caudal medulla floor of 4th ventricle . Functions as the Chemoreceptor Trigger Zone CTZ to detect toxins and induce vomiting . n Subfornical Organ SFO & OVLT Detect plasma osmolarity and Angiotensin II. n Secretory CVOs n Posterior Pituitary Neurohypophysis Releases Oxytocin and ADH. n Pineal Gland Secretes Melatonin. n Median Eminence Releases hypothalamic hormones. n n correlate n Levodopa-Induced Nausea Slide 16 nBecause the Area Postrema lacks a BBB circulating Levodopa can stimulate dopamine receptors there before entering the brain causing severe nausea/vomiting. n Prevention Co-administration of Carbidopa a peripheral DOPA decarboxylase inhibitor that does not cross the BBB. n n n--- n n 2. The Meninges Slides 20-25 nThe brain is encased in three layers PAD n1. Dura Mater Hard Mother Tough outer layer. n Periosteal Layer Attached to the skull. n Meningeal Layer Inner layer. Separates from periosteal layer to form Dural Venous Sinuses and Dural Folds Falx Cerebri Tentorium Cerebelli . n2. Arachnoid Mater Spider Mother Wispy intermediate layer. n Arachnoid Granulations One-way valves that drain CSF into venous sinuses. n3. Pia Mater Soft Mother Delicate inner layer adherent to the brain surface follows gyri/sulci . n n Meningeal Spaces & Hemorrhages n Epidural Space Potential space between Skull and Dura. n Vessel Middle Meningeal Artery MMA . n Pathology Epidural Hematoma Trauma to Pterion -> Lens-shaped bleed . n Subdural Space Potential space between Dura and Arachnoid. n Vessel Bridging Veins . n Pathology Subdural Hematoma Shearing forces/Shaken Baby -> Crescent-shaped bleed . n Subarachnoid Space Real space between Arachnoid and Pia. n Contents CSF and major cerebral arteries/veins. n Pathology Subarachnoid Hemorrhage Ruptured Aneurysm -> Thunderclap Headache . n n--- n n 3. Brain Herniations Slides 26-29 nIncreased Intracranial Pressure ICP can force brain tissue through compartments. n n correlate n A. Subfalcine Cingulate Herniation n Displacement Cingulate gyrus herniates under the Falx Cerebri . n Sequelae Can compress the Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA -> Contralateral leg weakness. n n n correlate n B. Transtentorial Uncal Herniation n Displacement Medial Temporal Lobe Uncus herniates past the Tentorium Cerebelli . n Sequelae n Compresses CN III Oculomotor -> Ipsilateral Blown Pupil Fixed & Dilated Down and Out eye. n Compresses Cerebral Peduncle -> Contralateral Hemiparesis. n Compresses PCA -> Cortical blindness. n n n correlate n C. Transforaminal Tonsillar Herniation n Displacement Cerebellar Tonsils herniate through the Foramen Magnum . n Sequelae Compresses the Medulla Cardiorespiratory centers -> Respiratory Arrest and death. n n n--- n n 4. Ventricular System & CSF Slides 35-43 n CSF Flow Pathway n1. Lateral Ventricles via Foramen of Monro --> n2. Third Ventricle via Cerebral Aqueduct of Sylvius --> n3. Fourth Ventricle via Foramina of Luschka Lateral and Magendie Medial --> n4. Subarachnoid Space --> n5. Arachnoid Granulations --> n6. Superior Sagittal Sinus Venous blood . n n Hydrocephalus nAccumulation of CSF resulting in ventricular enlargement. n Non-Communicating Obstructive Blockage within the ventricles. n Common Cause Aqueductal Stenosis Slide 44 . Lateral & 3rd ventricles dilate; 4th remains normal. n Communicating Blockage of absorption Arachnoid Granulations or flow in subarachnoid space. All ventricles dilate. n Causes Meningitis Subarachnoid Hemorrhage scarring . n Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus NPH Chronic communicating hydrocephalus in elderly. n Triad Wet Wobbly Wacky Urinary Incontinence Ataxia/Magnetic Gait Dementia . n Hydrocephalus Ex Vacuo Apparent enlargement of ventricles due to brain atrophy e.g. Alzheimer s Huntington s . ICP is normal . n n Cerebral Venous Thrombosis CVT Slide 26 n Thrombosis in dural sinuses e.g. Superior Sagittal Sinus . n Risk Factors Hypercoagulable states Pregnancy OCPs Infection Mastoiditis . n Signs Headache Papilledema Seizures due to venous congestion/infarction . Lecture 3 Neuroanatomy Meninges BBB CSF Ventricles lecturer Vladimir Grubisic M.D. Ph.D. & Vincy Mathew Neuroanatomy session Lecture 3 prev l2 next l4 pdf pdfs/Lecture 3. V. Grubisic M.D. Ph.D. PPT -1.pdf Neuroanatomy BBB & Meninges n Barriers n BBB n Tight Junctions Zona Occludens n Permeable Lipids O2 n Blocked Polar molecules Dopamine n CVOs No BBB n Area Postrema Vomiting n Posterior Pituitary n Meninges n Dura Outer n Epidural Space MMA n Subdural Space Bridging Veins n Arachnoid Middle n Subarachnoid Space CSF Arteries n Pia Inner n Herniations n Subfalcine Cingulate gyrus Leg weak n Uncal Temporal lobe Blown Pupil n Tonsillar Cerebellum Resp Arrest n Ventricles n Flow Lat -> 3rd -> 4th n Hydrocephalus NPH Wet Wobbly Wacky questions 1. A patient presents with neurological symptoms secondary to increased blood-brain barrier BBB permeability after a traumatic brain injury. Damage to which of the following proteins essential in tight junctions would most likely likely disrupt BBB integrity? Connexin-43 Laminin Zona Occludens ZO-1 Integrin Fibronectin 2 From Slide 11 Zona Occludens ZO proteins along with Claudins and Occludins form the tight junctions between endothelial cells. Damage to these compromises the barrier. 2. Levodopa is treated for Parkinson s disease because unlike Dopamine it can cross the Blood-Brain Barrier. However it frequently causes nausea. This is due to its interaction with which brainstem structure that lacks a BBB? Nucleus Tractus Solitarius Pineal Gland Area Postrema Subfornical Organ Median Eminence 2 From Slide 18 The Area Postrema is a Circumventricular Organ CVO in the medulla vomiting center that lacks a BBB. It detects emetic agents like circulating Levodopa/Dopamine in the blood before they enter the protected CNS. 3. A 26-year-old man presents with a dilated unresponsive right pupil blown pupil after a head injury. CT shows a subdural hematoma with displacement of the medial temporal lobe. What is the specific type of herniation? Subfalcine Cingulate Herniation Transforaminal Tonsillar Herniation Transtentorial Uncal Herniation Central Herniation Upward Cerebellar Herniation 2 From Slide 32 The Uncas medial temporal lobe herniating past the tentorium compresses CN III causing an ipsilateral blown pupil. This is a classic sign of Uncal Herniation. 4. Which layer of the meninges is highly vascular and contains the Middle Meningeal Artery rupture of which leads to an Epidural Hematoma? Pia Mater Arachnoid Mater Meningeal Dura Periosteal Dura / Epidural Space interface Subarachnoid Space 3 The Middle Meningeal Artery runs in the Epidural space between the skull and the periosteal layer of the dura . Trauma to the pterion often ruptures this artery. 5. In the ventricular system CSF flows from the Third Ventricle to the Fourth Ventricle via which structure? Foramen of Monro Foramen of Magendie Foramen of Luschka Cerebral Aqueduct of Sylvius Central Canal 3 CSF flows Lateral -> Monro -> 3rd -> Aqueduct of Sylvius -> 4th -> Luschka/Magendie. Which proteins form Tight Junctions in the BBB? Occludin Claudin Zona Occludens ZO Concept Which brain area lacks BBB and triggers vomiting? Area Postrema Anatomy Why is Levodopa used instead of Dopamine? Dopamine is polar blocked by BBB ; Levodopa crosses and converts. Pharm Structure separating cerebral hemispheres? Falx Cerebri Anatomy Structure separating cerebrum and cerebellum? Tentorium Cerebelli Anatomy Contents of Epidural Space? Middle Meningeal Artery MMA Clinical Contents of Subdural Space? Bridging Veins Clinical Sign of Uncal Herniation? Ipsilateral Blown Pupil CN III compression Pathology Sign of Subfalcine Herniation? Contralateral leg weakness ACA compression Pathology Triad for Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus NPH ? Wet Incontinence Wobbly Ataxia Wacky Dementia Clinical BandB Neurology > Meninges & CSF BBB Rule Lipid-soluble substances O2 Alcohol cross freely. Polar substances Dopamine Glucose need transport. Tight Junctions are key. Parkinson s Rx Give Levodopa not Dopamine. Levodopa crosses BBB via AA transporter and converts to Dopamine inside. Median Eminence vs Area Postrema Both lack BBB CVOs . Area Postrema = Vomiting CTZ . Median Eminence = Hormones. Epidural vs Subdural Epidural = Lens shaped MMA Trauma . n Subdural = Crescent shaped Bridging Veins Elderly . Uncal Herniation Medial temporal lobe pushes on CN III . First sign = Ipsilateral Blown Pupil . NPH Triad Normal Pressure Hydrocephalus n1. Wet Incontinence n2. Wobbly Ataxia n3. Wacky Dementia"}, {"id": "l30", "title": "Lecture 30: Supraspinal Motor Systems (Cortex & Brainstem)", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l30 Lecture 30 Supraspinal Motor Systems Cortex & Brainstem n n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D. n Session Lecture 30 n n 1. Pyramidal System Upper Motor Neurons n Definition UMNs originate in the Cortex M1 Premotor SMA and project to LMNs in the Brainstem or Spinal Cord. n Corticospinal Tract CST n Lateral CST 85% Decussates at Medullary Pyramids. Controls Distal Limbs Fine motor . n Ventral CST 15% Descends ipsilaterally decussates in spinal cord. Controls Axial/Proximal muscles Posture . n Corticobulbar Tract n Controls Cranial Nerves Head/Neck . n Innervation Most CN nuclei receive Bilateral input. n Exceptions n CN VII Face Lower Face receives only Contralateral input. Upper Face is Bilateral . n CN XII Tongue Tongue deviates AWAY from the UMN lesion towards the weak side . n n 2. UMN vs. LMN Lesions n| Feature | UMN Lesion Cortex/Stem | LMN Lesion Nerve/Root | n| --- | --- | --- | n| Tone | Spastic Increased | Flaccid Decreased | n| Reflexes | Hyperreflexia | Hyporeflexia | n| Babinski | Present + Toe up | Absent Toe down | n| Atrophy | Mild Disuse | Severe Denervation | n| Fasciculations | No | Yes | n n 3. Brainstem Tracts & Posturing n Rubrospinal Tract Originates in Red Nucleus Midbrain . Flexor tones of upper limbs. n Vestibulospinal Tract Extensor tone Antigravity . n Decorticate Posture Mummy n Lesion ABOVE Red Nucleus e.g. Cerebral Hemispheres . n Presentation Arms Flexed Red nucleus intact Legs Extended. n Decerebrate Posture n Lesion BELOW Red Nucleus e.g. Pons/Midbrain . n Presentation Arms Extended Legs Extended Rubrospinal lost Vestibulospinal unopposed . n n 4. Specific Clinical Syndromes n Locked-In Syndrome n Lesion Pons Basilar Artery . n Effect Bilateral CST/Corticobulbar damage. Quadriplegia + Anarthria can t speak . n Spared Vertical eye movements/Blinking Midbrain/CN III spared . Consciousness intact. n Bell s Palsy LMN CN VII Paralysis of Entire Hemi-face Forehead + Chin . n Stroke UMN CN VII Paralysis of Lower Face Only Forehead spared . Lecture 30 Supraspinal Motor Systems Cortex & Brainstem Neurology questions A patient presents with right-sided facial weakness involving the forehead and chin. What is the most likely location of the lesion? Left Motor Cortex Face area Left Internal Capsule Right Facial Nerve CN VII Left Facial Nucleus Right Trigeminal Nerve 2 Weakness of the entire hemiface forehead + chin indicates an LMN lesion Bell s Palsy or CN VII injury on the same side . A comatose patient exhibits extension of both the upper and lower extremities when stimulated. This posturing suggests a lesion located Above the Red Nucleus Below the Red Nucleus e.g. Pons In the Spinal Cord In the Thalamus In the Cerebellum 1 Extension of all limbs is Decerebrate posturing which occurs with lesions below the Red Nucleus rubrospinal tract lost . Which of the following is a classic sign of an Upper Motor Neuron UMN lesion? Fasciculations Severe Atrophy Hyporeflexia Babinski Sign Flaccidity 3 Babinski sign extensor plantar response is a hallmark of UMN dysfunction. The others are LMN signs. A patient suffers a stroke affecting the Left Primary Motor Cortex. The tongue deviates to which side upon protrusion? Left Right Does not deviate Upward Downward 1 With a UMN lesion Left Cortex the tongue deviates AWAY from the lesion towards the weak side . So it points to the Right . n Wait let me double check my pearl... LMN = Toward. UMN = Away. Correct. So Left Cortex lesion -> Right Genioglossus weak -> Tongue points Right. Correct Answer is Right. Options Left/Right . Which tract is primarily responsible for voluntary fine motor control of the distal upper extremities? Ventral Corticospinal Tract Lateral Corticospinal Tract Vestibulospinal Tract Reticulospinal Tract Tectospinal Tract 1 The Lateral Corticospinal Tract controls distal muscles hands/fingers . The Ventral tract controls axial muscles. UMN Lesion Signs Spasticity Hyperreflexia Babinski + Neurology LMN Lesion Signs Flaccidity Atrophy Fasciculations Neurology Decorticate Posture Arms Flexed Mummy . Lesion Above Red Nucleus. Neurology Decerebrate Posture Arms Extended. Lesion Below Red Nucleus. Neurology Bell s Palsy vs Stroke Face Bell s LMN = Whole Face. Stoke UMN = Lower Face. Neurology Lateral Corticospinal Tract Decussation Medullary Pyramids Neuroanatomy Rubrospinal Tract Origin Red Nucleus Midbrain Neuroanatomy Locked-In Syndrome Lesion Pons Basilar Artery Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Neuroanatomy > Pyramidal & Extrapyramidal FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Spinal Tracts UMN vs LMN Face UMN Stroke Spares forehead Look for wrinkles! . n LMN Bell s Affects whole face Forehead smooth . Decorticate vs Decerebrate De-COR-ticate Arms like C s Flexed to Cor e . Lesion Above Red Nucleus. n De-cere-brate Arms like E s Extended . Lesion Below Red Nucleus Worse prognosis . Tongue Deviation Lick your lesions. n LMN Tongue deviates TOWARD lesion. n UMN Tongue deviates AWAY from lesion. Locked-In Syndrome Pontine Stroke. Quadriplegia. Only eyes move Vertical . Consciousness preserved. Pyramidal Decussation Occurs at the Cervicomedullary Junction Lower Medulla ."}, {"id": "l31", "title": "Lecture 31: Basal Ganglia", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l31 Lecture 31 Basal Ganglia n n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D. n Session Lecture 31 n n 1. Basal Ganglia Anatomy n Striatum Input Caudate + Putamen. Releases GABA . n Pallidum Output GPi Internal & GPe External . Releases GABA . n Subthalamic Nucleus STN Releases Glutamate Excitatory . n Substantia Nigra pars Compacta SNc Releases Dopamine . n n 2. The Pathways Albin-DeLong Model n1. Direct Pathway GO n Promotes movement. n Striatum inhibits GPi $ to$ Disinhibition of Thalamus. n D1 Receptors Dopamine excites this pathway. n2. Indirect Pathway NO-GO n Inhibits movement. n Striatum inhibits GPe $ to$ STN active $ to$ GPi active $ to$ Thalamus inhibited. n D2 Receptors Dopamine inhibits this pathway Releases the brake . n3. Hyperdirect Pathway Fast suppression Freeze! . Cortex $ to$ STN $ to$ GPi. n n 3. Parkinson s Disease Hypokinetic n Pathology Loss of Dopaminergic neurons in SNc . Lewy Bodies $ alpha$-synuclein . n Symptoms TRAP n T remor Resting Pill-rolling . n R igidity Cogwheel . n A kinesia / Bradykinesia Slow movement Masked face . n P ostural Instability. n History MPTP toxin caused acute Parkinsonism in drug users Frozen Addicts . n n 4. Huntington s Disease Hyperkinetic n Pathology Atrophy of Caudate Nucleus Boxy ventricles on MRI . loss of GABA/ACh neurons. n Genetics CAG trinucleotide repeat >40 . Autosomal Dominant . Anticipation worse in next gen . n Symptoms Chorea Dance-like Dementia Depression. Signs appear age 30-50. n n 5. Other Disorders n Hemiballismus n Lesion Subthalamic Nucleus STN lacunar stroke . n Symptoms Wild ballistic flailing of contralateral arm/leg. n Tardive Dyskinesia n Cause Chronic use of Antipsychotics D2 blockers . n Symptoms Repetitive lip smacking tongue rolling. Late onset. Lecture 31 Basal Ganglia Neurology questions A patient presents with wild uncontrollable flailing movements of the right arm and leg. An MRI would most likely show a lacunar stroke in which structure? Left Substantia Nigra Left Subthalamic Nucleus Right Subthalamic Nucleus Left Caudate Right Putamen 1 Hemiballismus contralateral flailing is caused by a lesion in the Subthalamic Nucleus STN . Right movements = Left STN. Which neurotransmitter is primarily released by the neurons of the Subthalamic Nucleus? GABA Dopamine Glutamate Acetylcholine Serotonin 2 The STN is the only intrinsic BG nucleus that is excitatory releasing Glutamate . All others Striatum GPe GPi SNr release GABA. In Huntington s Disease which brain structure undergoes the most significant atrophy leading to ventriculomegaly? Substantia Nigra Caudate Nucleus Thalamus Hippocampus Cerebellum 1 Huntington s causes atrophy of the Striatum specifically the Caudate Nucleus leading to boxy dilated ventricles. A 65-year-old male presents with a resting tremor masked facies and a shuffling gait. Histology would likely reveal Neurofibrillary Tangles Lewy Bodies Amyloid Plaques Negri Bodies Pick Bodies 1 This is Parkinson s Disease. The hallmark histology is Lewy Bodies alpha-synuclein in the Substantia Nigra. Dopamine binding to D2 receptors in the striatum results in Excitation of the Indirect Pathway Inhibition of the Indirect Pathway Excitation of the Direct Pathway Inhibition of the Direct Pathway Inhibition of the Thalamus 1 D2 receptors are inhibitory . They are on the Indirect No-Go pathway. So Dopamine inhibits the Indirect pathway essentially turning off the stop signal which promotes movement. Hemiballismus Lesion Contralateral Subthalamic Nucleus STN Neurology Parkinson s Pathology Loss of Dopamine in SNc + Lewy Bodies Pathology Huntington s Genetic Defect CAG Repeat >40 on Ch 4 Genetics Direct Pathway Function Promotes Movement Disinhibits Thalamus Physiology Neurotransmitter of Striatum GABA Neuroscience Neurotransmitter of STN Glutamate Neuroscience Tardive Dyskinesia Cause Chronic D2 Blockers Antipsychotics Pharmacology MPTP causes... Parkinsonism Destroys SNc Pathology AMBOSS Neurology > Pathology > Degenerative diseases FirstAid Neurology > Pathology > Movement Disorders Parkinson s TRAP T remor Resting n R igidity n A kinesia n P ostural Instability Huntington s CAG Mnemonic C audate lost n A Ch lost n G ABA lost n Also CAG Repeats Hemiballismus Lesion Damage to the Subthalamic Nucleus STN causes contralateral flailing. Dopamine Receptors D1 = Direct Go . n D2 = Indirect No-Go . nDopamine stimulates D1 and inhibits D2 both resulting in Movement . MPTP Toxin Destroys SNc neurons causing rapid permanent Parkinsonism. Synthetic heroin case ."}, {"id": "l32", "title": "Lecture 32: Cerebellum", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l32 Lecture 32 Cerebellum n n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D. n Session Lecture 32 n n 1. Neuroanatomy n Organization Cortex 3 layers covering Deep Nuclei. n Deep Nuclei Lateral to Medial D on t E at G reasy F ood . n D entate. n E mboliform. n G lobose. n F astigial. n Circuits n Inputs n Mossy Fibers From Spinal cord/Brainstem. Excitatory Glutamate . n Climbing Fibers From Inferior Olive . Excitatory Glutamate . n Processing n Purkinje Cells The sole output of the cerebellar cortex. Releases GABA Inhibitory onto Deep Nuclei. n Output n Deep Nuclei send final signal to Thalamus/Brainstem. n n 2. Functional Divisions & Pathology n| Division | Anatomy | Function | Lesion Symptoms | n| --- | --- | --- | --- | n| Vestibulo-cerebellum | Flocculonodular Lobe | Balance Eye movements | Vertigo Nystagmus Wide-based gait Truncal Ataxia . | n| Spino-cerebellum | Vermis & Paravermis | Posture Trunk/Limb Gait | Ataxic Gait Drunk walk . Alcoholism damages Anterior Lobe Vermis . | n| Cerebro-cerebellum | Lateral Hemispheres | Fine motor planning | Intention Tremor Dysmetria Dysdiadochokinesia . | n n 3. Clinical Syndromes n Essential Tremor n Most common movement disorder. Familial Autosomal Dominant . n Postural/Action Tremor worse when holding a cup . n Note Improves with Alcohol . Often self-medicated . n Intention Tremor n Cerebellar lesion. Tremor worsens as you approach target . n Resting Tremor n Basal Ganglia Parkinson s . n n 4. DANISH Mnemonic Cerebellar Signs n D ysdiadochokinesia Can t do rapid alternating movements . n A taxia. n N ystagmus. n I ntention Tremor. n S peech Slurred/Scanning . n H ypotonia. Lecture 32 Cerebellum Neurology questions A chronic alcoholic presents with a wide-based staggering gait but normal arm coordination. Which region of the cerebellum is most likely degenerated? Flocculonodular Lobe Anterior Lobe Vermis Lateral Hemisphere Dentate Nucleus Inferior Olive 1 Chronic alcoholism typically causes atrophy of the Anterior Lobe Vermis leading to Truncal Ataxia and gait issues while sparing the limbs Lateral Hemispheres . Which of the following describes the output of Purkinje cells? Excitatory Glutamate to Deep Nuclei Inhibitory GABA to Deep Nuclei Excitatory Glutamate to Thalamus Inhibitory GABA to Thalamus Excitatory Acetylcholine to Spinal Cord 1 Purkinje Cells provide Inhibitory GABA input to the Deep Cerebellar Nuclei modulating the final output. A patient cannot rapidly supinate and pronate their hand Dysdiadochokinesia on the left side. Where is the lesion? Right Cerebellar Hemisphere Left Cerebellar Hemisphere Vermis Right Motor Cortex Right Basal Ganglia 1 Cerebellar symptoms are Ipsilateral . Dysdiadochokinesia limb coordination maps to the Lateral Hemisphere . So Left hand issue = Left Cerebellar Hemisphere . A child presents with frequent falls nystagmus and vomiting. An MRI reveals a tumor in the roof of the 4th ventricle compressing the flocculonodular lobe. What is the most likely diagnosis? Glioblastoma Multiforme Meningioma Medulloblastoma Schwannoma Pituitary Adenoma 2 Medulloblastoma is a common pediatric tumor that often arises in the Vermis/Flocculonodular lobe causing truncal ataxia and hydrocephalus vomiting . Climbing fibers which provide powerful excitatory input to Purkinje cells originate from which structure? Spinal Cord Pontine Nuclei Inferior Olive Red Nucleus Vestibular Nuclei 2 Climbing Fibers originate exclusively from the Inferior Olive Medulla . Deep Cerebellar Nuclei Lat to Med Dentate Emboliform Globose Fastigial Neuroanatomy Purkinje Cell Neurotransmitter GABA Inhibitory Neuroscience Climbing Fibers Origin Inferior Olive Neuroanatomy Lesion causing Dysdiadochokinesia Ipsilateral Lateral Cerebellar Hemisphere Neurology Classic sign of Cerebrocerebellar lesion Intention Tremor Neurology Sign of Vermis Lesion Truncal Ataxia Drunk Walk Neurology Essential Tremor characteristics Action/Postural. Improves w/ Alcohol. Neurology DANISH meaning Dysdiadochokinesia Ataxia Nystagmus Intention Tremor Slurred Speech Hypotonia Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Neuroanatomy > Cerebellum FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Cerebellum Deep Nuclei Mnemonic D on t E at G reasy F ood Lateral to Medial n D entate E mboliform G lobose F astigial. Cerebellar Hemisphere vs Vermis Lateral lesions affect Limbs fall Toward side of lesion . n Medial Vermis lesions affect Trunk Truncal Ataxia fall anywhere . Alcoholic Cerebellar Degeneration Chronic alcohol use specifically damages the Anterior Lobe Vermis causing a wide-based ataxic gait. Essential Tremor Action tremor eating/writing . Improves with Alcohol. Tx Beta-blockers. Output of Cerebellar Cortex Purkinje Cells are the ONLY output of the cortex. They are Inhibitory GABA ."}, {"id": "l33", "title": "Lecture 33: Sympathomimetics I - Direct Agonists", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l33 Lecture 33 Sympathomimetics I - Direct Agonists n n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. n Session Lecture 33 n n 1. Receptors & Physiology n $ alpha 1$ Gq Vasoconstriction Skin/Splanchnic Mydriasis Pupil Dilation Urinary Sphincter Contraction. n $ alpha 2$ Gi Presynaptic Inhibition Decreases NE release . n $ beta 1$ Gs Heart $ uparrow$ HR $ uparrow$ Contractility Kidney $ uparrow$ Renin . n $ beta 2$ Gs Bronchodilation Vasodilation Skeletal Muscle Uterine Relaxation Tocolysis . n $ beta 3$ Gs Bladder Detrusor Relaxation. n n 2. Direct-Acting Sympathomimetics n| Drug | Receptors | Action | Clinical Use | n| --- | --- | --- | --- | n| Epinephrine | $ alpha beta$ | Low Dose $ beta 2$ Hypotension . High Dose $ alpha 1$ Hyperstension . | Anaphylaxis DOC Cardiac Arrest. | n| Norepinephrine | $ alpha 1 = alpha 2 > beta 1$ | Potent Vasoconstriction. Reflex Bradycardia. | Septic Shock DOC . | n| Dopamine | D1 > $ beta 1$ > $ alpha 1$ | Low Renal Vasodilation. Med Inotropy. High Vasoconstriction. | Shock Renal sparing theoretically Heart Failure. | n| Dobutamine | $ beta 1 > beta 2$ | $ uparrow$ Inotropy > Chronotropy. | Heart Failure Decompensated Stress Testing. | n| Phenylephrine | $ alpha 1$ | Vasoconstriction. | Nasal Decongestant Hypotension Mydriasis. | n| Isoproterenol | $ beta 1 = beta 2$ | $ uparrow$ HR Vasodilation $ downarrow$ DBP . | Bradycardia Heart block Rarely used . | n n 3. Hemodynamics Key Concepts n Mean Arterial Pressure MAP $ text CO times text TPR $. n Pulse Pressure $ text Systolic - text Diastolic $. n Epinephrine Reversal Giving an $ alpha$-blocker before Epinephrine converts its pressor effect $ alpha 1$ to a depressor effect $ beta 2$ unmasked $ to$ Hypotension. n Reflex Bradycardia Pure $ alpha 1$ agonists Phenylephrine/Norepinephrine cause massive vasoconstriction $ to$ $ uparrow$ BP $ to$ Baroreceptors fire $ to$ Reflex $ downarrow$ HR. n n 4. Clinical Pearls n Anaphylaxis Epinephrine is the ONLY first-line drug. Prevents shock $ alpha 1$ opens airway $ beta 2$ stops mast cells. n Septic Shock Norepinephrine is first-line to restore MAP. n Tachyphylaxis Rapidly diminishing response to successive doses seen with Indirect agonists like Tyramine/Ephedrine less so with direct . Lecture 33 Sympathomimetics I - Direct Agonists Neurology questions A patient in septic shock requires a vasopressor to maintain Mean Arterial Pressure MAP . Which agent is the first-line choice due to its potent alpha-1 and beta-1 activity with minimal beta-2 effects? Epinephrine Dopamine Norepinephrine Phenylephrine Isoproterenol 2 Norepinephrine is the Drug of Choice DOC for Septic Shock. It provides strong vasoconstriction alpha-1 and some inotropy beta-1 without the hypotension associated with beta-2 stimulation. A patient is given a drug that increases Systolic BP decreases Diastolic BP and increases Heart Rate. Which drug fits this profile? Phenylephrine Norepinephrine Isoproterenol Clonidine Propranolol 2 Isoproterenol is a non-selective Beta agonist Beta-1 and Beta-2 . Beta-1 increases SBP and HR. Beta-2 causes vasodilation dropping DBP. Phenylephrine is administered intravenously. What represents the expected cardiovascular response? Increased BP Increased HR Increased BP Decreased HR Decreased BP Increased HR Decreased BP Decreased HR No change in BP Increased HR 1 Phenylephrine is a pure Alpha-1 agonist. It increases TPR and BP. The body responds with Reflex Bradycardia Decreased HR . Which receptor subtype is primarily responsible for the bronchodilatory effects of Epinephrine used in anaphylaxis? Alpha-1 Alpha-2 Beta-1 Beta-2 Dopamine-1 3 Beta-2 receptors mediate bronchodilation and skeletal muscle vasodilation . Alpha-1 mediates the vasoconstriction that supports BP. Dobutamine is primarily used in the setting of Anaphylactic Shock Septic Shock Decompensated Heart Failure Hypertension Asthma 2 Dobutamine Beta-1 > Beta-2 is an inotrope used to increase cardiac output in Decompensated Heart Failure or cardiogenic shock. Epinephrine Receptor Profile Alpha-1 Alpha-2 Beta-1 Beta-2 Pharmacology Norepinephrine Receptor Profile Alpha-1 > Alpha-2 > Beta-1 NO Beta-2 Pharmacology Phenylephrine Receptor Profile Pure Alpha-1 Agonist Pharmacology Isoproterenol Receptor Profile Beta-1 = Beta-2 Non-selective Beta Pharmacology Dobutamine Use Heart Failure Inotropy Pharmacology Drug of Choice for Anaphylaxis Epinephrine Pharmacology Drug of Choice for Septic Shock Norepinephrine Pharmacology Reflex response to Phenylephrine Bradycardia Decreased HR Pharmacology AMBOSS Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs > Sympathomimetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs Epinephrine Dosage Effects Low Dose = Beta-2 Dominant Vasodilation/Hypotension . n High Dose = Alpha-1 Dominant Vasoconstriction/Hypertension . Norepinephrine vs Epinephrine Norepinephrine has NO Beta-2 activity. It causes pure vasoconstriction and reflex bradycardia. Epinephrine has Beta-2 activity Bronchodilation . Dopamine Dosing Low Renal D1. n Medium Cardiac Beta-1. n High Pressor Alpha-1. Phenylephrine Reflex A pure Alpha-1 agonist Phenylephrine increases BP but causes Reflex Bradycardia lowers HR . Beta-1 Selectivity Dobutamine is a Beta-1 agonist used for Heart Failure to increase contractility without massively increasing Heart Rate Chronotropy ."}, {"id": "l34", "title": "Lecture 34: Sympathomimetics II - Indirect & Mixed Acting", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l34 Lecture 34 Sympathomimetics II - Indirect & Mixed Acting n n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. n Session Lecture 34 n n 1. Beta-2 Agonists Lungs & Uterus n SABA Short-Acting Albuterol Levalbuterol. n Use Acute Asthma/COPD exacerbation Rescue . n LABA Long-Acting Salmeterol Formoterol. n Use Chronic maintenance. Always combine with Steroids ICS in asthma to prevent mortality. n Tocolytics Terbutaline . n Use Relaxes uterine smooth muscle to delay preterm labor allows time for corticosteroids to mature fetal lungs . n n 2. Beta-3 Agonists Bladder n Mirabegron n Action Relaxes Detrusor muscle. n Use Overactive Bladder OAB . Alternative to Anticholinergics . n n 3. Alpha-2 Agonists Central Sympatholytics nReduces sympathetic outflow from the brainstem. n Clonidine n Uses Hypertension resistant Opioid Withdrawal ADHD. n Adverse Rebound Hypertension if stopped abruptly. dry mouth sedation. n Methyldopa n Use Hypertension in Pregnancy Safe for fetus . n Adverse Coombs+ Hemolytic Anemia Autoimmune Drug-induced Lupus. n n 4. Indirect & Mixed Acting n Amphetamines Adderall n Mechanism Displaces NE/DA from vesicles into synapse Reverse transport . n Use ADHD Narcolepsy. n Cocaine n Mechanism Blocks Reuptake NET/DAT . n Effect Vasoconstriction Local Anesthetic + Pressor . n Ephedrine n Mechanism Mixed Direct Agonist + Indirect Release . n Use Hypotension during Anesthesia. n Pseudoephedrine n Use Nasal Decongestant. Precursor for Meth hence restricted . n n 5. MAO Inhibitors The Cheese Effect n Mechanism Inhibits Monoamine Oxidase enzyme that breaks down NE/DA/Serotonin . n Tyramine Interaction Tyramine found in Aged Cheese Wine Cured Meats releases stored NE. Commonly degraded by GI MAO. n Crisis If on MAO-I Tyramine is NOT degraded $ to$ Massive NE release $ to$ Hypertensive Crisis . Lecture 34 Sympathomimetics II - Indirect & Mixed Acting Neurology questions A pregnant woman presents with chronic hypertension. Which centrally acting alpha-2 agonist is considered safe and appropriate for management? Clonidine Methyldopa Lisinopril Losartan Propranolol 1 Methyldopa is the classic safe antihypertensive for pregnancy. ACE inhibitors Lisinopril and ARBs are teratogenic. A patient on a Monoamine Oxidase Inhibitor MAOI for depression consumes a charcuterie board with aged cheese and wine. They present to the ER with a severe headache and BP of 220/120. mechanism is Direct Alpha-1 agonism by Tyramine Displacement of stored Norepinephrine by Tyramine Blockade of Norepinephrine reuptake Allergic reaction to wine Serotonin Syndrome 1 Tyramine acts as an indirect sympathomimetic by displacing stored NE from vesicles. Usually broken down by MAO but persists in MAO-I patients. Which drug works by relaxing the detrusor muscle via Beta-3 agonism to treat overactive bladder? Oxybutynin Mirabegron Bethanecol Tamsulosin Finasteride 1 Mirabegron is a Beta-3 agonist. Oxybutynin is an Anticholinergic which also works but via M3 blockade . Amphetamines exert their stimulant effect primarily by Directly activating Alpha-1 receptors Blocking the degradation of Acetylcholine Displacing Norepinephrine and Dopamine from vesicles into the synapse Blocking the reuptake of Serotonin only Directly activating Beta-2 receptors 2 Amphetamines enter the nerve terminal via uptake and then displace NE/DA from vesicles reversing the transporter to pump NE/DA OUT into the synapse. Salmeterol is classified as a Short-Acting Beta Agonist SABA Long-Acting Beta Agonist LABA Alpha-1 Antagonist Muscarinic Antagonist Corticosteroid 1 Salmeterol is a LABA used for asthma maintenance. Albuterol is a SABA. Methyldopa Indication Hypertension in Pregnancy Pharmacology Methyldopa Adverse Effect Coombs + Hemolytic Anemia Pharmacology Clonidine Mechanism Central Alpha-2 Agonist Decreases Sympathetic Outflow Pharmacology Mirabegron Target Beta-3 Receptor Bladder Relaxation Pharmacology Terbutaline Use Tocolysis Delay Labor Pharmacology Tyramine Mechanism Displaces Stored NE Indirect Agonist Pharmacology Cocaine Mechanism Blocks Reuptake NET/DAT Pharmacology Albuterol vs Salmeterol Albuterol = SABA Acute . Salmeterol = LABA Maintenance . Pharmacology AMBOSS Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs > Sympathomimetics FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs Methyldopa in Pregnancy Methyldopa is the classic choice for Hypertension in Pregnancy Preeclampsia prophylaxis . Watch for Hemolytic Anemia . Clonidine Rebound Never stop Clonidine abruptly. Causes massive Rebound Hypertension due to upregulated receptors. Cocaine vs Amphetamine Cocaine BLOCKS reuptake NET blocker . n Amphetamine REVERSES transporter Displaces NE . Both increase synaptic NE . Cheese Reaction MAO Inhibitors + Tyramine Wine/Cheese = Hypertensive Crisis. Tyramine displaces NE . Terbutaline Tocolysis Beta-2 agonist used to Stop Labor Relax Uterus . Turbulence delays the flight birth ."}, {"id": "l35", "title": "Lecture 35: Cranial Nerves I, II, III, IV, VI (Eye Movements)", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l35 Lecture 35 Cranial Nerves I II III IV VI Eye Movements n n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D. n Session Lecture 35 n n 1. CN I Olfactory & CN II Optic n CN I Visceral Sensory. Bypasses Thalamus. n Pathology Anosmia Early sign of Parkinson s - Lewy bodies in Olfactory Bulb . n CN II Special Sensory. n Reflex Pupillary Light Reflex Afferent II $ to$ Pretectal Area $ to$ Bilateral Edinger-Westphal $ to$ Efferent III . n n 2. CN III Oculomotor n Motor Medial Superior Inferior Recti; Inferior Oblique; Levator Palpebrae. n Parasympathetic Edinger-Westphal Nucleus $ to$ Pupil Constriction Miosis & Lens Accommodation. n Lesion CN III Palsy n Down and Out eye Unopposed SO4/LR6 . n Ptosis Droopy eyelid . n Mydriasis Blown pupil - loss of parasympathetics . n Note The Superior Rectus and Levator are innervated by the Contralateral nucleus but the nerve fibers cross inside the complex so a NERVE lesion affects the ipsilateral eye. n n 3. CN IV Trochlear n Motor Superior Oblique Depresses/Intorts eye . n Unique Features n 1. Only CN to exit Dorsally . n 2. Only CN to Decussate Completely Right Nucleus $ to$ Left Superior Oblique . n Lesion n Vertical Diplopia Worse when looking down e.g. stairs/reading . n Head Tilt Patient tilts head AWAY from the affected side to compensate/align eyes. n n 4. CN VI Abducens & Gaze Control n Motor Lateral Rectus Abducts eye . n Horizontal Gaze Circuit n To look RIGHT Right PPRF $ to$ Right Abducens Nucleus . n Right Abducens Nucleus does TWO things n 1. Contracts Ring Lateral Rectus Abducts Right Eye . n 2. Sends signal via MLF Medial Longitudinal Fasciculus to Left Oculomotor Nucleus $ to$ Contracts Left Medial Rectus Adducts Left Eye . n Pathology n CN VI Palsy Esotropia Eye turns in . n INO Internuclear Ophthalmoplegia n Lesion of MLF . n Ipsilateral eye cannot Adduct Medial Rectus palsy . n Contralateral eye has Nystagmus. n Convergence is SPARED because CN III is intact . n Classic Cause Multiple Sclerosis Bilateral INO . n n 5. Summary Table n| Nerve | Muscles | Lesion Sign | n| --- | --- | --- | n| CN III | All others + Levator + Pupil | Down & Out Ptosis Blown Pupil | n| CN IV | Superior Oblique | Head tilt AWAY Vertical Diplopia stairs | n| CN VI | Lateral Rectus | Esotropia Eye turns In | Lecture 35 Cranial Nerves I II III IV VI Eye Movements Neurology questions A patient presents with vertical diplopia that worsens when walking down stairs. They are observed tilting their head to the left. Which cranial nerve is likely injured? Left CN IV Right CN IV Left CN III Right CN VI Left CN VI 2 Vertical diplopia stairs problem suggests CN IV Trochlear palsy. The head tilts AWAY from the lesion. Left tilt = Right CN IV lesion. A patient cannot look to the right with the right eye. When attempting to look right the left eye also fails to adduct. Convergence is intact. Where is the lesion? Right Abducens Nerve Right Abducens Nucleus Right MLF Left MLF Right PPRF 2 This is a Right Abducens Nucleus lesion. It knocks out the Right LR Can t look right AND the signal to the Left MR via MLF causing a gaze palsy to that side. A simple nerve lesion would only affect the Right LR . In Internuclear Ophthalmoplegia INO the lesion is located in the Abducens Nerve Oculomotor Nerve Medial Longitudinal Fasciculus MLF Superior Colliculus Lateral Geniculate Nucleus 2 INO is caused by damage to the MLF disconnecting the Abducens nucleus from the contralateral Oculomotor nucleus. Which cranial nerve contains the parasympathetic fibers responsible for pupillary constriction? Optic II Oculomotor III Trochlear IV Trigeminal V Abducens VI 1 CN III Oculomotor carries parasympathetics from the Edinger-Westphal nucleus to the ciliary ganglion for miosis and accommodation. Anosmia is an early and often overlooked clinical sign of which neurodegenerative disorder? Alzheimer s Disease Parkinson s Disease Huntington s Disease ALS Multiple Sclerosis 1 Parkinson s Disease is strongly associated with early Anosmia due to Lewy Body deposition in the olfactory bulb. Eye Muscles Innervation Mnemonic SO4 LR6 All Others 3 Neuroanatomy CN IV Exit Point Dorsal Brainstem Midbrain Neuroanatomy CN III Palsy Signs Down & Out Ptosis Mydriasis Neurology INO Lesion Location Medial Longitudinal Fasciculus MLF Neurology Head Tilt in CN IV Palsy Away from Lesion Neurology Vertical Diplopia Cause CN IV Palsy Neurology Parasympathetic Nucleus for Eye Edinger-Westphal Nucleus Neuroanatomy Abducens Nucleus Function Abducts Ipsilateral Eye + Adducts Contralateral Eye via MLF Neurophysiology AMBOSS Neurology > Neuroanatomy > Cranial Nerves FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Cranial Nerves SO4 LR6 All The Rest 3 S uperior O blique = CN 4 . n L ateral R ectus = CN 6 . nAll other eye muscles = CN 3 . CN IV Trochlear Trivia The only cranial nerve to exit from the BACK Dorsal of the brainstem. Also the only one to cross completely! INO Internuclear Ophthalmoplegia Lesion of MLF . Eye lags when looking towards nose Adduction deficit . The other eye shakes Nystagmus . Seen in MS . Parkinson s Early Sign Anosmia Loss of smell is often the FIRST sign of Parkinson s appearing years before motor symptoms. Head Tilt in CN IV Palsy Head tilts AWAY from the lesion. Right CN IV palsy = Left Head Tilt ."}, {"id": "l36", "title": "Lecture 36: Cranial Nerves V, VII-XII (Brainstem Nuclei)", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l36 Lecture 36 Cranial Nerves V VII-XII Brainstem Nuclei n n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D. n Session Lecture 36 n n 1. CN V Trigeminal n Sensory Face n Mesencephalic Nucleus Proprioception Jaw jerk . n Principal Pontine Nucleus Discriminative Touch Face . n Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus Pain & Temperature Face . Extends down to C-spine . n Motor V3 Muscles of Mastication Temporalis Masseter Pterygoids . n Clinical n Trigeminal Neuralgia Suicide disease . Sharp shooting pain. n Corneal Reflex Afferent V1 Ophthalmic $ to$ Efferent VII Facial . n n 2. CN VII Facial n Motor Muscles of Facial Expression Stapedius dampens sound Stylohyoid Digastric Post . n Parasympathetic Superior Salivatory Nucleus $ to$ Lacrimal Tears Submandibular/Sublingual Saliva . n Sensory Taste Anterior 2/3 of Tongue. n Clinical n Bell s Palsy LMN Ipsilateral paralysis of ENTIRE face Forehead involved . Hyperacusis Loss of taste. n UMN Lesion Stroke Contralateral paralysis of LOWER face only Forehead Spared due to bilateral innervation . n n 3. CN IX Glossopharyngeal & CN X Vagus n CN IX n Sensory Post 1/3 Tongue Taste/Feel Carotid Body/Sinus. n Motor Stylopharyngeus. n Reflex Gag Reflex Afferent . n CN X n Motor Nucleus Ambiguus $ to$ Pharynx/Larynx/Palate. n Visceral Dorsal Motor Nucleus $ to$ GI/Heart Parasymp . n Reflex Gag Reflex Efferent . n Lesion Uvula deviates AWAY from lesion. Hoarseness. n n 4. CN XI Accessory & CN XII Hypoglossal n CN XI Trapezius Shrug SCM Turn head . Lesion Shoulder droop cannot turn head away from lesion. n CN XII Tongue muscles. Lesion Tongue deviates TOWARD lesion Lick your wounds . n n 5. Brainstem Syndromes n Lateral Medullary Wallenberg Syndrome n Artery PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery . n Nuclei n Ambiguus Dysphagia Hoarseness. n Vestibular Vertigo Nystagmus. n Spinal V Ipsilateral Facial Pain/Temp loss. n Spinothalamic Contralateral Body Pain/Temp loss. n Sympathetic Ipsilateral Horner s Syndrome. n Medial Medullary Syndrome n Artery ASA Anterior Spinal Artery . n Nuclei n Hypoglossal XII Tongue deviates to lesion. n Corticospinal Tract Contralateral Hemiparesis. n Medial Lemniscus Contralateral Proprioception/Vibration loss. Lecture 36 Cranial Nerves V VII-XII Brainstem Nuclei Neurology questions A patient presents with sudden onset of right-sided facial drooping involving the forehead and the lower face. They also complain that sounds seem abnormally loud in the right ear. The most likely location of the lesion is Left Motor Cortex Right Internal Capsule Right Facial Nerve Left Facial Nerve Right Trigeminal Nerve 2 Right Facial Nerve LMN lesion causes Bell s Palsy Whole hemiface paralysis and Hyperacusis loss of Stapedius . A patient sticks out their tongue and it deviates to the LEFT. There is atrophy of the left side of the tongue. Which nerve is injured? Right CN XII Left CN XII Right CN X Left CN X Left CN VII 1 The tongue deviates TOWARD the side of the lesion Lick your wounds . Left deviation + Atrophy = Left CN XII LMN lesion. In Lateral Medullary Syndrome Wallenberg which nucleus is responsible for the characteristic dysphagia and hoarseness? Solitary Nucleus Nucleus Ambiguus Dorsal Motor Nucleus of Vagus Hypoglossal Nucleus Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus 1 Nucleus Ambiguus provides motor innervation to the pharynx and larynx via IX and X . Damage causes dysphagia and hoarseness. Loss of the afferent limb of the corneal reflex would result from damage to which cranial nerve? CN II CN III CN V1 CN VII CN VIII 2 The afferent sensing limb of the corneal reflex is CN V1 Ophthalmic division of Trigeminal . The efferent blinking is CN VII. A patient has loss of pain and temperature sensation on the RIGHT side of the face and the LEFT side of the body. This crossed sensory deficit suggests a lesion in the Right Lateral Medulla Left Lateral Medulla Right Medial Medulla Left Medial Medulla Right Thalamus 0 Crossed findings Ipsi Face Contra Body are a hallmark of brainstem lesions. Right Lateral Medulla hits Right Spinal V Face and Right Spinothalamic carrying Left Body info . Corneal Reflex Limbs In V1. Out VII. Neurology Gag Reflex Limbs In IX. Out X. Neurology Tongue Deviation Rule Deviates TOWARD the lesion Lick your wounds Neurology Uvula Deviation Rule Deviates AWAY from the lesion Neurology Nucleus Ambiguus Function Motor to Pharynx/Larynx Swallowing/Speech - CN IX X XI Neuroanatomy Wallenberg Syndrome Artery PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Neurology Bell s Palsy vs Stroke Face Bell s = Whole Face. Stroke = Lower Face only Forehead spared . Neurology Hyperacusis Nerve CN VII Stapedius muscle paralysis Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Neuroanatomy > Cranial Nerves FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Cranial Nerves Bell s Palsy vs Stroke Bell s LMN Can NOT wrinkle forehead Whole face paralyzed . n Stroke UMN CAN wrinkle forehead Forehead spared . Uvula vs Tongue Uvula CN X Deviates AWAY from lesion. n Tongue CN XII Deviates TOWARD lesion. Corneal Reflex In CN V1 Feels the cotton . n Out CN VII Blinks the eye . Lateral Medullary Syndrome Wallenberg PICA stroke. Specific sign Dysphagia/Hoarseness Nucleus Ambiguus + Crossed Pain/Temp Face same side Body opposite side . Gag Reflex In CN IX Glossopharyngeal . n Out CN X Vagus ."}, {"id": "l37", "title": "Lecture 37: Neurologic Exam II - Motor & Reflexes", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l37 Lecture 37 Neurologic Exam II - Motor & Reflexes n n Lecturer R. Morgan D.O. n Session Lecture 37 n n 1. Motor System n Grading 0-5 Scale n 0 No contraction. n 1 Trace / Flicker. n 2 Active movement with gravity eliminated horizontal . n 3 Active movement against gravity only . n 4 Active movement against resistance but weak . n 5 Normal strength. n Key Roots n C5/6 Biceps Deltoid Brachioradialis. n C7 Triceps Wrist Extension. n C8/T1 Hand intrinsics Grip . n L2/3/4 Iliopsoas Hip Flex Quads Knee Ext . n L5 Tibialis Ant Dorsiflex/Heel walk . n S1 Gastroc Plantarflex/Toe walk . n n 2. Reflexes DTRs n Grading 0-4 Scale n 0 Absent. n 2 Normal. n 4 Hyperactive with Clonus UMN . n Roots n Biceps C5 C6. n Brachioradialis C6. n Triceps C7. n Patellar L4. n Achilles S1. n Reinforcement Jendrassik Maneuver hook fingers and pull distracts patient to elicit hard-to-find reflexes. n n 3. UMN vs LMN Findings n| Feature | UMN Upper Motor Neuron | LMN Lower Motor Neuron | n| --- | --- | --- | n| Tone | Spastic Increased | Flaccid Decreased | n| Reflexes | Hyperreflexia +Clonus | Hyporeflexia / Absent | n| Babinski | Positive Upgoing toe | Negative Downgoing | n| Atrophy | Mild Disuse | Severe Denervation | n| Fasciculations | Absent | Present | n n 4. Coordination & Gait Cerebellum n Dysdiadochokinesis Inability to perform Rapid Alternating Movements RAM . n Dysmetria Overshooting/Undershooting Finger-to-Nose . n Ataxia Unsteady gait. Tandem Walking Heel-to-toe brings out subtle ataxia. n Romberg Test n Patient stands feet together eyes CLOSED . n Positive Patient sways/falls. n Meaning Loss of Proprioception Dorsal Columns . Note Cerebellar ataxia occurs with eyes OPEN too. n n 5. Other Signs n Pronator Drift Eyes closed arms out palms up. If one arm pronates and drops $ to$ Contralateral UMN Corticospinal weakness. n Primitive Reflexes Grasp Rooting Snout. Seen in Frontal Lobe lesions or babies . Lecture 37 Neurologic Exam II - Motor & Reflexes Neurology questions A patient presents with weakness in the right arm. On examination they have increased tone spasticity and hyperactive reflexes 3+ in the right biceps and triceps. There is no atrophy. This pattern suggests a lesion in the Lower Motor Neuron e.g. Brachial Plexus Upper Motor Neuron e.g. Corticospinal Tract Neuromuscular Junction Cerebellum Muscle fiber directly 1 UMN signs include Spasticity Hyperreflexia and preserved muscle bulk no atrophy . LMN signs would be flaccidity and atrophy. Which of the following describes muscle strength Grade 3/5? Active movement against gravity and resistance Active movement against gravity only Active movement with gravity eliminated Flicker of contraction only Normal strength 1 Grade 3 is movement against gravity but NOT resistance. Grade 2 is gravity eliminated. Grade 4 is against resistance. A positive Romberg test swaying significantly only when eyes are closed primarily indicates dysfunction of The Cerebellum The Motor Cortex Proprioception Dorsal Columns The Basal Ganglia The Thalamus 2 Romberg tests Proprioception . If vision eyes open compensates but balance fails when eyes close it s a sensory ataxia Dorsal Columns . The Achilles tendon reflex is mediated primarily by which spinal nerve root? L3 L4 L5 S1 S2 3 Achilles reflex is S1 . Patellar is L4. Dysdiadochokinesis inability to perform rapid alternating movements is a hallmark sign of damage to the Cerebellum Frontal Lobe Spinal Cord Basal Ganglia Cranial Nerves 0 Incoordination of rapid movements RAM is a classic Cerebellar sign. S1 Reflex Achilles Ankle Jerk Neurology L4 Reflex Patellar Knee Jerk Neurology C7 Reflex Triceps Neurology C5/C6 Reflex Biceps / Brachioradialis Neurology Grade 3 Strength Against Gravity Only Neurology Grade 2 Strength Gravity Eliminated Neurology Jendrassik Maneuver Use Reinforce/Distract for Reflexes Neurology Positive Romberg Meaning Sensory Ataxia Dorsal Column Proprioception Loss Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Clinical Neurology > Neurological Examination FirstAid Neurology Reflex Roots Mnemonic S1 2 Buckle my shoe - Ankle . n L3 4 Kick the door - Knee . n C5 6 Pick up sticks - Biceps . n C7 8 Lay them straight - Triceps . Romberg is Sensory A positive Romberg falling only when eyes CLOSED indicates a Sensory Ataxia Dorsal Columns/Proprioception NOT Cerebellum. Pronator Drift Highly specific sign for subtle UMN Weakness . The supinator muscles are weaker than pronators in UMN lesions. Babinski Sign Stroke sole of foot. Normal = Toes curl DOWN. UMN Lesion = Big toe goes UP Dorsiflex + fanning . Jendrassik Maneuver Can t get a knee jerk? Have them hook hands and PULL. Distraction unmasks the reflex."}, {"id": "l38", "title": "Lecture 38: OMM Lab 18 - Sacral Diagnosis", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l38 Lecture 38 OMM Lab 18 - Sacral Diagnosis n n Lecturer P. Noto D.O. n Session Lecture 38 n n 1. Axes of Motion n Oblique Axes Named for the superior pole. n Right Oblique Axis Runs from Right Superior to Left Inferior pole. n Left Oblique Axis Runs from Left Superior to Right Inferior pole. n Usage Walking cycle Torsions . n Transverse Axes n Superior S2 Respiratory / Craniosacral motion. n Middle S2 Postural / Flexion-Extension. n Inferior S3 Innominate rotation Ilial shear . n n 2. Diagnosing Torsions vs Shears n L5 Rules n 1. L5 rotates OPPOSITE to the sacrum. n 2. L5 sidebends to the SAME side as the Oblique Axis. n Example If L5 is Rotated Right Sacrum is Rotated Left. n Seated Flexion Test SFT n Positive on the side of Dysfunction Blocked motion . n The axis is on the Opposite side of the positive SFT. n Physiologic Forward vs Non-Physiologic Backward n Physiologic Neutral mechanics Type I . Forward torsions L on L R on R . n Spring Test Negative It springs / Good motion . n Sphinx Test Negative Symmetry improves with extension . n Non-Physiologic Non-neutral Type II . Backward torsions L on R R on L . n Spring Test Positive Hard end feel / No spring . n Sphinx Test Positive Asymmetry gets worse / Pain . n n 3. The 4-Step Diagnosis n1. Seated Flexion Test Determines the side of dysfunction and thus the axis is on the opposite side . n2. Spring/Sphinx Determines if it is Forward Negative or Backward Positive . n3. L5 Confirms rotation. n4. Deep Sulcus / Post ILA n Forward Torsion Deep Sulcus and Posterior/Inf ILA are on Opposite sides. n Backward Torsion Deep Sulcus and Posterior/Inf ILA are on Opposite sides. n Wait simplify n Deep Sulcus = Side of Forward Rotation Sacral Base Anterior . n Posterior ILA = Side of Backward Rotation Sacral Base Posterior . n n 4. Specific Diagnoses n Left on Left Forward Torsion n Facing Left. n Axis Left. n L5 Rotated Right. n Spring Negative Good . n Right on Left Backward Torsion n Facing Right. n Axis Left. n L5 Rotated Left. n Spring Positive Bad . n Unilateral Shears n Not an axis dysfunction. Whole side is stuck forward or backward. n SFT + on same side as deep sulcus/low ILA if flexion or shallow sulcus/high ILA if extension . Lecture 38 OMM Lab 18 - Sacral Diagnosis Neurology questions A patient has a positive Seated Flexion Test on the RIGHT. The Spring Test is NEGATIVE. L5 is Rotated Right. What is the diagnosis? Right on Right Sacral Torsion Left on Left Sacral Torsion Right on Left Sacral Torsion Left on Right Sacral Torsion Right Unilateral Sacral Flexion 1 Step 1 SFT Right + $ to$ Axis is on the Left . So it s ? on Left . n Step 2 Spring Negative $ to$ Forward Torsion L on L . n Check Forward torsions have SAME letters. L on L fits. Which of the following findings is consistent with a Non-Physiologic Backward Sacral Torsion? Negative Sphinx Test Negative Spring Test Positive Spring Test L5 Rotated opposite to Sacrum Symmetrical Sacral Sulci 2 Positive Spring Test Hard/Stiff indicates the sacral base is stuck backward Posterior which is a non-physiologic backward torsion. In a Right on Right sacral torsion L5 will be Rotated Left Sidebent Left Rotated Left Sidebent Right Rotated Right Sidebent Right Rotated Right Sidebent Left Neutral 1 R on R Axis is Right $ to$ L5 Sidebends Right . Rotation is Right $ to$ L5 Rotates Left Opposite . Pattern Rot Left SB Right Type I neutral mechanics . The Superior Transverse Axis S2 is associated with which motion? Postural Flexion/Extension Innominate Rotation Respiratory / Craniosacral Motion Walking Cycle Disc Herniation 2 Superior Axis Respiratory/Craniosacral. n Middle Axis Postural. n Inferior Axis Innominate Ilial . If the Seated Flexion Test is positive on the LEFT which oblique axis is engaged? Left Oblique Axis Right Oblique Axis Middle Transverse Axis Inferior Transverse Axis Vertical Axis 1 SFT is positive on the side of dysfunction. The functioning axis is on the OPPOSITE side. Positive Left $ to$ Axis is Right . SFT Positive Side Meaning Side of Dysfunction Axis is Opposite OMM Spring Test Positive Meaning Stuck Backward Non-physiologic / Backward Torsion OMM L5 Rotation Rule Rotates OPPOSITE to Sacrum OMM L5 Sidebending Rule Sidebends SAME side as Axis OMM Same Letters e.g. L on L Forward Torsion Physiologic OMM Different Letters e.g. R on L Backward Torsion Non-Physiologic OMM AMBOSS OMM > Sacrum FirstAid OMM > Sacrum L5 Rule Mnemonic L5 acts like a Bully . If L5 rotates Right the Sacrum turns away Left . Sidebending side = Axis side. Spring Test Rules If it Springs it s a Functional Physiologic/Forward torsion. nIf it s Stiff Positive it s a Stuck Non-physiologic/Backward torsion. Naming Torsions Rotation on Axis . n R on R Forward Physiologic . n L on L Forward Physiologic . n R on L Backward Non-Physiologic . n L on R Backward Non-Physiologic . n Hint Same letters = Forward. Different letters = Backward. Seated Flexion Test Finds the side of ILIOSACRAL dysfunction. The axis is on the OPPOSITE side of the positive test. Sphinx Test Logic Extension Sphinx makes a forward sacrum happy more symmetrical . It makes a backward sacrum sad more asymmetrical ."}, {"id": "l39", "title": "Lecture 39: Pharmacology - Adrenergic Antagonists", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l39 Lecture 39 Pharmacology - Adrenergic Antagonists n n Lecturer L. Goldstein Pharm.D. n Session Lecture 39 n n 1. Alpha-Adrenergic Antagonists Alpha Blockers n Non-Selective Alpha-1 & Alpha-2 n Phenoxybenzamine Irreversible Covalent bond . Long duration. Used pre-op for Pheochromocytoma prevents hypertensive crisis . n Phentolamine Competitive Reversible . Used for Epinephrine Reversal local anesthetic overdose and Pheochromocytoma diagnosis/surgery. n Side Effects Severe Orthostatic Hypotension Reflex Tachycardia due to Alpha-2 block increasing NE release . n Selective Alpha-1 Blockers -zosins n Prazosin Terazosin Doxazosin Used for HTN add-on and BPH. n Tamsulosin Flomax Alpha-1A selective Prostate specific . Used for BPH less hypotension . n Adverse Effects First-Dose Effect Syncope/Severe Orthostatis $ to$ Take at bedtime! Floppy Iris Syndrome Cataract surgery risk . n Selective Alpha-2 Antagonist n Yohimbine Increases NE release blocks feedback . Aphrodisiac . Causes anxiety/tremor. n n 2. Beta-Adrenergic Antagonists Beta Blockers n Non-Selective Beta-1 & Beta-2 n Propranolol Lipophilic CNS effects nightmares/sedation . Used for Migraine prophylaxis Performance Anxiety Stage fright Thyroid Storm. n Contraindication Asthma/COPD Bronchoconstriction via B2 . n Beta-1 Selective Cardioselective A-M - Atenolol Metoprolol Esmolol . n Safer in lung disease. n Combined Alpha/Beta Blockers n Labetalol Carvedilol Block Alpha-1 Vasodilation + Beta-1/2. n Use CHF Carvedilol decreases mortality Hypertensive Emergencies Labetalol . n n 3. Key Pharmacology Concepts n Epinephrine Reversal n Normal Epi Increases BP Alpha-1 vasoconstriction > Beta-2 dilation . n Epi + Alpha Blocker Decreases BP Alpha blocked unmasks Beta-2 vasodilation . n Diabetes & Beta Blockers n Beta blockers MASK symptoms of hypoglycemia tachycardia tremors . n Non-selectives delay recovery from hypoglycemia block liver gluconeogenesis . n Withdrawal n NEVER stop abruptly. Chronic use UPREGULATES receptors. n Sudden stop $ to$ Rebound HTN Angina MI Sudden Death. n n 4. Therapeutic Uses Summary n| Indication | Preferred Agents | Mechanism | n| --- | --- | --- | n| Hypertension | Metoprolol Atenolol | Decrease CO Renin inhibition. | n| Angina/MI | Metoprolol Carvedilol | Decrease O2 demand HR/Contractility . | n| CHF | Carvedilol Metoprolol Succinate Bisoprolol | Block sympathetic remodeling. Increase survival. | n| BPH | Tamsulosin Doxazosin | Relax prostate smooth muscle Alpha-1 . | n| Pheochromocytoma | Phenoxybenzamine | Irreversible Alpha block before surgery. | n| Glaucoma | Timolol | Decrease aqueous production. | Lecture 39 Pharmacology - Adrenergic Antagonists Neurology questions A patient with Pheochromocytoma is scheduled for surgery. Which drug is most appropriate for preoperative management to prevent hypertensive crisis? Propranolol Phenoxybenzamine Atenolol Prazosin Yohimbine 1 Phenoxybenzamine is an Irreversible alpha blocker. It is the gold standard for pheochromocytoma pre-op to prevent catecholamine surges. A 65-year-old male is started on Terazosin for BPH. What important counseling point must be provided? Take on an empty stomach because food reduces absorption. Take with grapefruit juice to enhance effect. Take the first dose at bedtime to avoid syncope. Discontinue immediately if nasal congestion occurs. Expect heart rate to decrease significantly. 2 First-Dose Effect Alpha-1 blockers cause significant orthostatic hypotension/syncope initially. Taking it at bedtime mitigates the risk of falling. Which of the following beta blockers also possesses Alpha-1 blocking activity providing additional vasodilation? Metoprolol Atenolol Carvedilol Timolol Esmolol 2 Carvedilol and Labetalol trigger Combined blockade Beta-1 Beta-2 and Alpha-1 . This provides vasodilation + heart rate control. A diabetic patient taking insulin is prescribed a beta blocker. They should be warned that the drug might mask which symptom of hypoglycemia? Sweating Hunger Tachycardia Confusion Fatigue 2 Tachycardia and tremors are mediated by the sympathetic response to hypoglycemia. Beta blockers mask these. Sweating cholinergic is usually preserved. The phenomenon known as Epinephrine Reversal describes a drop in blood pressure when Epinephrine is administered in the presence of A Beta Blocker An Alpha Blocker A Muscarinic Agonist A Ganglionic Blocker A Calcium Channel Blocker 1 An Alpha Blocker like Phentolamine blocks the vasoconstriction Alpha-1 of Epi leaving the vasodilation Beta-2 unopposed $ to$ BP drop. Phenoxybenzamine Mechanism Irreversible Alpha Blocker Pharmacology First Dose Effect Drug Class Alpha-1 Blockers -zosins Pharmacology A-M Beta Blockers Atenolol Metoprolol Beta-1 Selective Cardioselective Pharmacology Carvedilol / Labetalol Mechanism Mixed Alpha-1 and Beta Blockers Pharmacology Epinephrine Reversal Cause Epi + Alpha Blocker Unmasks Beta-2 Vasodilation Pharmacology Hypoglycemia Signs Masked by Beta Blockers Tachycardia Tremors Anxiety Pharmacology Hypoglycemia Sign SPARED by Beta Blockers Sweating Cholinergic Pharmacology Pheochromocytoma Pre-op Drug Phenoxybenzamine Pharmacology SketchyPharm Autonomic Drugs > Adrenergic Antagonists FirstAid Pharmacology > Autonomic Drugs Epinephrine Reversal Giving an Alpha Blocker Phentolamine before Epinephrine turns Epi s Pressor effect into a Depressor effect BP drops due to unopposed Beta-2 vasodilation . First Dose Effect Alpha-1 blockers Prazosin/Terazosin cause severe orthostatic hypotension on the first dose. Counseling Take the first dose at Bedtime . Beta Blocker Withdrawal Chronic beta blockade causes receptor Upregulation . Abrupt withdrawal leads to supersensitivity = Rebound Hypertension / MI . Always TAPER. Diabetes & Beta Blockers Beta blockers mask the warning signs of hypoglycemia palpitations tremors anxiety . Sweating Cholinergic is the only sign that remains. Pheochromocytoma Rule Always give Alpha blockers BEFORE Beta blockers. A before B . Giving Beta first leaves Alpha unopposed $ to$ Hypertensive Crisis."}, {"id": "l4", "title": "Lecture #4: Blood Supply of the CNS", "module": "Neuroanatomy", "content": "l4 Lecture 4 Blood Supply of the CNS n n Lecturer J. Xie Ph.D. & Vincy Mathew n Session Neuroanatomy n n 1. Overview of Cerebral Circulation Slides 3-6 nThe brain represents only 2% of body weight but consumes 20% of the body s oxygen and glucose . nBlood enters the cranium via two major systems n1. Anterior Circulation Internal Carotid Arteries Supplies ~80% of the brain Telencephalon Diencephalon . n2. Posterior Circulation Vertebral Arteries Supplies ~20% of the brain Brainstem Cerebellum Occipital lobes . n n--- n n 2. Anterior Circulation ICA Slides 8-14 nThe Internal Carotid Artery ICA enters the skull via the Carotid Canal passes through the Cavernous Sinus and gives off 4 key Intradural branches OPAM n n 1. Ophthalmic Artery Slide 14 n First branch. Supplies the eye/retina. n Clinical Occlusion causes Amaurosis Fugax transient monocular blindness described as a curtain coming down . n n 2. Posterior Communicating Artery PCom Slide 15 n Connects the Anterior ICA and Posterior PCA circulations. n Clinical Common site for Berry Aneurysms . Aneurysm here can compress CN III causing an ipsilateral Down and Out eye with blown pupil. n n 3. Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA Slides 16-17 20 n Supplies the Medial surface of the Frontal and Parietal lobes. n Function Motor/Sensory to the Lower Limb Leg/Foot and trunk. n Stroke Symptoms Contralateral Leg weakness and sensory loss. n n 4. Middle Cerebral Artery MCA Slides 18-19 20 n Largest branch. Runs laterally through the Sylvian Fissure. n Supplies the Lateral surface of Frontal Temporal and Parietal lobes. n Function Motor/Sensory to the Face and Upper Limb Arm/Hand . Also supplies language centers in the dominant hemisphere. n Stroke Symptoms Contralateral Face/Arm weakness Face/Arm sensory loss. n Left MCA Dominant Aphasia Broca s or Wernicke s . n Right MCA Non-dominant Hemineglect. n n correlate n Lenticulostriate Arteries Slide 19 n Small deep perforating branches of the MCA supplying the Basal Ganglia and Internal Capsule. n Pathology High pressure Hypertension causes lipohyalinosis and microaneurysms Charcot-Bouchard Aneurysms . n Result Rupture leads to Intraparenchymal Hemorrhage . Occlusion leads to Lacunar Infarcts pure motor strokes . n n n--- n n 3. Posterior Circulation Vertebrobasilar Slides 23-26 n Vertebral Arteries VA n Ascend through transverse foramina of C6-C1. Enter skull via Foramen Magnum. n Branches n Anterior Spinal Artery ASA Supplies anterior 2/3 of spinal cord. n PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Supplies Lateral Medulla and Inferior Cerebellum. Occlusion -> Wallenberg Syndrome . n n Basilar Artery BA n Formed by fusion of VAs. Runs along the Pons. n Branches n AICA Anterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Supplies Lateral Pons. n Pontine Arteries Supply the Pons. Occlusion -> Locked-in Syndrome . n SCA Superior Cerebellar Artery Supplies Superior Cerebellum. n n Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA n Terminal bifurcation of the Basilar Artery. n Supplies Occipital Lobe Visual Cortex and Inferior Temporal lobe. n Stroke Symptoms Contralateral Homonymous Hemianopsia Visual field loss often with Macular Sparing . n n pearl n CN III Rule The Oculomotor Nerve CN III acts as a sandwich filling between the SCA Superior Cerebellar and PCA Posterior Cerebral arteries. Aneurysms here compress CN III. n n n--- n n 4. Collateral & Venous Circulation n n Circle of Willis Slides 29-30 n Anastomotic ring at the base of the brain providing collateral flow. n Formed by ACom ACA ICA PCom PCA. n Note The circle is incomplete in ~50% of people. n Watershed Zones Border areas between ACA/MCA/PCA territories. Susceptible to ischemia during Global Hypoperfusion e.g. Cardiac Arrest or severe hypotension -> Man in a Barrel syndrome proximal weakness . n n Venous Drainage Slides 32-42 nVeins drain into Valveless Dural Venous Sinuses n Superior Sagittal Sinus SSS Drains superficial veins + CSF via arachnoid granulations . n Cavernous Sinus A venous plexus surrounding the Sella Turcica. n Contents ICA CN VI freely inside and CN III IV V1 V2 in the wall . n Pathology Cavernous Sinus Thrombosis or Fistula can cause multiple cranial nerve palsies. n n Presentation Thunderclap Headache Worst headache of life meningeal signs. n Lecture 4 Blood Supply of the CNS lecturer J. Xie Ph.D. Neuroanatomy session Lecture 4 prev l3 next l5 pdf pdfs/Lecture 4. J. Xie Ph.D. PPT .pdf CNS Blood Supply n Anterior Circ ICA n Ophthalmic Eye Amaurosis Fugax n ACA Medial Brain Legs n MCA Lateral Brain Face/Arms + Speech n Lenticulostriate Lacunar stroke n PCom Connects to Posterior n Posterior Circ Vert-Basilar n Vertebral A. -> PICA n Basilar A. -> AICA SCA PCA n PCA Occipital Vision n Circle of Willis n ACom Connects ACAs n PCom Connects ICA-PCA n Venous n Sup. Sagittal Sinus n Cavernous Sinus CN III IV V VI n Transverse/Sigmoid -> IJV questions 1. A 65-year-old man presents with sudden difficulty understanding language. He speaks fluently but uses nonsensical words Word Salad . MRI shows an ischemic stroke. Which artery is most likely involved and which area is affected? ACA - Broca s Area MCA - Broca s Area MCA - Wernicke s Area PCA - Occipital Lobe PICA - Lateral Medulla 2 From Slide 46 Wernicke s Area comprehension is in the Superior Temporal Gyrus supplied by the MCA . Damage leads to Receptive Aphasia Fluent but meaningless . Broca s Expressive is also MCA but frontal. 2. A patient experiences a curtain coming down over their right eye which resolves after a few minutes Amaurosis Fugax . This symptom is a warning sign for a blockage in which artery? Ophthalmic Artery Branch of ICA Middle Cerebral Artery Posterior Cerebral Artery Basilar Artery External Carotid Artery 0 From Slide 14 The Ophthalmic Artery is the first branch of the Intradural ICA. Transient occlusion causes temporary monocular blindness Amaurosis Fugax a TIA warning sign. 3. A patient with chronic hypertension presents with a pure motor stroke. MRI reveals a small lacunar infarct in the internal capsule. Which small arteries are likely responsible? Callosomarginal Arteries Lenticulostriate Arteries Anterior Choroidal Arteries Posterior Communicating Arteries Pontine Arteries 1 From Slide 19 Lenticulostriate Arteries deep branches of MCA supply the Basal Ganglia and Internal Capsule. They are prone to lipohyalinosis and Charcot-Bouchard aneurysms in hypertension. 4. In the Circle of Willis which artery connects the Anterior Circulation ICA system to the Posterior Circulation Vertebrobasilar system ? Anterior Communicating Artery ACom Posterior Communicating Artery PCom Anterior Choroidal Artery Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Basilar Artery 1 The Posterior Communicating Artery PCom connects the ICA to the PCA linking the two major circulatory systems. 5. Contralateral leg weakness and sensory loss is most characteristic of a stroke in which vascular territory? MCA ACA PCA PICA AICA 1 From Slide 20 The Anterior Cerebral Artery ACA supplies the medial surface of the cortex where the Leg/Foot area of the motor/sensory homunculus is located. Branches of Intradural ICA? OPAM Ophthalmic PCom ACA MCA Anatomy Which artery supplies the visual cortex? Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA Anatomy Clinical sign of ACA stroke? Contralateral Leg Weakness Clinical Clinical sign of MCA stroke? Contralateral Face/Arm Weakness + Aphasia Clinical Artery passing through Cavernous Sinus? Internal Carotid Artery ICA Anatomy Transient monocular vision loss Curtain ? Amaurosis Fugax Ophthalmic Artery ischemia Clinical Common site for Berry Aneurysm causing CN III palsy? Posterior Communicating Artery PCom Pathology Vessels prone to rupture in hypertension Basal Ganglia ? Lenticulostriate Arteries Charcot-Bouchard Pathology What nerve passes between SCA and PCA? CN III Oculomotor Anatomy Risk factors for Cerebral Venous Thrombosis CVT ? Pregnancy OCPs Hypercoagulability Clinical BandB Neurology > Spinal Cord Anatomy CNS Blood Flow Rule The brain is 2% of body weight but consumes 20% of oxygen/glucose. It has zero reserve. Stroke Homunculus Rule ACA = Medial aspect = Legs/Feet . n MCA = Lateral aspect = Face/Arms/Hands + Speech. Amaurosis Fugax Transient monocular blindness Curtain coming down is a TIA of the Ophthalmic Artery warning of impending ICA stroke. CN III Compression Aneurysms of the PCom or between SCA and PCA often compress CN III causing a Down and Out eye with a blown pupil . Lenticulostriate Arteries These small vessels supply the Basal Ganglia. They are the primary site of hypertensive lipohyalinosis and Lacunar Infarcts . Aphasia Localization Aphasia is usually Left MCA. n Broca s Frontal = Broken speech. n Wernicke s Temporal = Word salad."}, {"id": "l40", "title": "Lecture 40: Spinal Cord Syndromes & Mononeuropathies", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l40 Lecture 40 Spinal Cord Syndromes & Mononeuropathies n n Lecturer A. Leder D.O. n Session Lecture 40 n n 1. Spinal Cord Anatomy Review n Dorsal Columns Vibration Proprioception Fine Touch. Cross in Brainstem . n Spinothalamic Tract Pain Temperature Crude Touch. Cross immediately/1-2 levels up . n Corticospinal Tract Motor. Cross in Medulla . n n 2. Spinal Cord Syndromes n Central Cord Syndrome Syringomyelia n Lesion Center of cord crossing spinothalamic fibers . n Presentation Cape-like loss of pain/temperature across shoulders/arms. Upper extremities > Lower. n Causes Syringomyelia Chiari I Trauma Hyperextension Tumor. n Posterior Column Syndrome Tabes Dorsalis n Lesion Dorsal Columns. n Presentation Loss of Proprioception/Vibration $ to$ Sensory Ataxia Stomping Gait +Romberg . Lhermitte Sign Shock down back . n Causes Neurosyphilis Tabes Dorsalis - Triad Lightning pains Ataxia Argyll Robertson Pupil B12 Deficiency. n Posterolateral Column Syndrome Subacute Combined Degeneration n Lesion Dorsal Columns + Corticospinal Tract. n Presentation Ataxia/Numbness + Spastic Paresis. n Causes Vitamin B12 Deficiency Pernicious anemia Gastric bypass Vegans Nitrous Oxide abuse Whippets . n Anterior Cord Syndrome n Lesion Anterior 2/3 of cord Spares Dorsal Columns . n Presentation Complete Motor Paralysis + Loss of Pain/Temp. Proprioception/Vibration SPARED . n Causes Anterior Spinal Artery ASA infarct Aortic aneurysm repair/surgery . n Brown-S u00e9quard Syndrome Hemicord n Lesion Half of the cord Hemisection . n Presentation n Ipsilateral Loss of Motor CST + Proprioception/Vibration DC . n Contralateral Loss of Pain/Temp Spinothalamic starting 1-2 levels below . n Causes Stab wound MS Tumor. n Cauda Equina Syndrome n Lesion Nerve roots L2-S5 Horse s tail . n Presentation Saddle Anesthesia Bowel/Bladder dysfunction Retention/Incontinence Radicular pain. n Management Surgical Emergency Decompression within 48hrs . n n 3. Common Mononeuropathies n Median Nerve Carpal Tunnel . Numbness 1st 3.5 digits. Thenar atrophy. n Ulnar Nerve Cubital Tunnel . Numbness 4th/5th digits. Claw Hand weak interossei . n Radial Nerve Saturday Night Palsy . Spiral groove compression. Wrist Drop . n Common Peroneal Fibular Nerve Unilateral Foot Drop Steppage gait . Compression at Fibular Head. n Lateral Femoral Cutaneous Nerve Meralgia Paresthetica . Numbness of lateral thigh. Caused by tight belts obesity pregnancy. Lecture 40 Spinal Cord Syndromes & Mononeuropathies Neurology questions A patient presents with loss of pain and temperature sensation in a cape-like distribution over the shoulders and arms. Vibration and proprioception are intact. This pattern is consistent with Anterior Cord Syndrome Central Cord Syndrome Brown-S u00e9quard Syndrome Posterior Column Syndrome Cauda Equina Syndrome 1 Central Cord Syndrome Syringomyelia expands from the central canal disrupting the crossing Spinothalamic fibers first causing the cape-like dissociation. Following a stab wound to the right side of the spinal cord Hemisection at T10 which finding would be expected? Loss of pain and temperature on the Right leg Loss of vibration and proprioception on the Left leg Paralysis of the Left leg Loss of pain and temperature on the Left leg Bilateral loss of all sensation 3 Brown-S u00e9quard Contralateral loss of Pain/Temp. Right lesion $ to$ Left leg Pain/Temp loss. Right leg would lose Motor/Vibe . A patient recovering from abdominal aortic aneurysm repair develops bilateral leg paralysis and loss of pain sensation but preserves vibration sense. The most likely diagnosis is Posterior Spinal Artery Infarct Anterior Spinal Artery Infarct Vitamin B12 Deficiency Tabes Dorsalis Guillain-Barr u00e9 Syndrome 1 Anterior Spinal Artery supplies the anterior 2/3 Motor + Spinothalamic . Infarct causes Anterior Cord Syndrome Paralysis + Pain loss with Dorsal Column sparing Vibe intact . Which of the following creates a Saturday Night Palsy causing wrist drop? Median Nerve compression at carpal tunnel Ulnar Nerve compression at cubital tunnel Radial Nerve compression at spiral groove Common Peroneal compression at fibular head Sciatic Nerve compression 2 Radial Nerve compression at the spiral groove humerus often from falling asleep with arm over a chair Saturday Night causes Wrist Drop . Subacute Combined Degeneration of the spinal cord involves demyelination of the Posterior Columns and Corticospinal Tracts due to deficiency of Vitamin B1 Thiamine Vitamin B3 Niacin Vitamin B12 Cobalamin Vitamin E Copper 2 Vitamin B12 deficiency or Nitrous Oxide toxicity causes Subacute Combined Degeneration. Central Cord Syndrome Pattern Cape-like Pain/Temp Loss Neurology Tabes Dorsalis Cause Neurosyphilis Tertiary Neurology Brown-Sequard Pain/Temp loss side Contralateral Opposite side Neurology Brown-Sequard Motor/Vibe loss side Ipsilateral Same side Neurology Wrist Drop Nerve Radial Nerve Neurology Foot Drop Nerve Common Peroneal Fibular Nerve Neurology Saddle Anesthesia + Incontinence Cauda Equina Syndrome Emergency Neurology Subacute Combined Degeneration Cause B12 Deficiency / Nitrous Oxide Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Spinal Cord Disorders FirstAid Neurology > Spinal Cord Lesions Brown-S u00e9quard Rules You lose Motor & Vibe on the SAME side Ipsi . nYou lose Pain & Temp on the OPPOSITE side Contra . Anterior Cord Spare Anterior Spinal Artery stroke kills everything EXCEPT the Dorsal Columns . So they can still feel Vibration/Proprioception but can t move or feel pain. Syringomyelia Sign Cape-like distribution of numbness Pain/Temp loss across the shoulders and back. Often associated with Chiari Malformation . Whippets & B12 Nitrous Oxide irreversibly oxidizes B12 leading to Subacute Combined Degeneration Posterolateral cord syndrome . Look for ataxia + numbness in a party-goer. Cauda Equina Flags New onset Incontinence or retention + Saddle Anesthesia . This is an MRI + Surgery EMERGENCY."}, {"id": "l41", "title": "Lecture 41: From Myelopathy to Myopathy", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l41 Lecture 41 From Myelopathy to Myopathy n n Lecturer A. Leder D.O. n Session Lecture 41 n n 1. Localization Approach n Myelopathy Spinal Cord UMN signs below Sensory level Bowel/Bladder. n Anterior Horn Cell LMN signs Weakness Atrophy Fasciculations . Sensory SPARED . n Radiculopathy Root Radiating pain Dermatomal sensory loss. n Plexopathy Multiple nerves/roots involved. Painful e.g. Parsonage-Turner . n Peripheral Neuropathy Stocking-glove sensory loss Distal weakness. n NMJ Fatigable weakness Diplopia/Ptosis. Sensory SPARED . n Myopathy Proximal muscle weakness. Sensory SPARED . n n 2. Anterior Horn Cell Disorders n ALS Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis n UMN + LMN Signs in same segment. n Asymmetric limb weakness Bulbar signs Dysarthria/Dysphagia . n Sensory Spared . n Polio & West Nile Virus n Acute Asymmetric flaccid paralysis LMN . n Fever/Meningeal signs. n Spinal Muscular Atrophy SMA n Genetic SMN1 . Symmetric LMN weakness. n n 3. Peripheral Nerve Disorders n Guillain-Barr u00e9 Syndrome AIDP n Acute Ascending Paralysis. Post-infectious Campylobacter Zika . n LP Albuminocytologic Dissociation High Protein Normal Cells . n Tx IVIG or Plasma Exchange. NO Steroids . n CIDP Chronic Inflammatory Demyelinating Polyneuropathy n Chronic >8 weeks . Chronic GBS . n Tx Steroids IVIG. n Leprosy Hansen s Disease. Mutilating neuropathy Armadillos . n n 4. Neuromuscular Junction NMJ n Myasthenia Gravis MG n Post-synaptic ACh Receptor Antibodies. n Clinical Ptosis Diplopia Bulbar weakness. Fatigue Worse at end of day . n Association Thymoma . n Lambert-Eaton Myasthenic Syndrome LEMS n Pre-synaptic Voltage-Gated Calcium Channel Antibodies. n Clinical Proximal leg weakness. Facilitation Strength IMPROVES with use . Dry mouth. n Association Small Cell Lung Cancer . n Botulism n Toxin blocks ACh release SNARE proteins . n Descending Paralysis. Pupils dilated/fixed. Honey infants . n n 5. Myopathies n Polymyositis/Dermatomyositis n Proximal symmetric weakness difficulty rising from chair . n Pain is uncommon . n Elevated CK. Lecture 41 From Myelopathy to Myopathy Neurology questions A 35-year-old male presents with ascending weakness starting in his legs and moving up. Reflexes are absent. Two weeks ago he had a diarrheal illness. CSF shows protein of 150 High and WBC of 2 Normal . What is the diagnosis? Multiple Sclerosis Guillain-Barr u00e9 Syndrome Myasthenia Gravis ALS Polio 1 GBS presents with acute ascending paralysis and areflexia after an infection Campylobacter . CSF shows Albuminocytologic Dissociation . Which of the following findings is classic for Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis ALS ? Pure sensory loss in a stocking-glove distribution Symmetric proximal muscle weakness with pain Combination of UMN and LMN signs with sensory sparing Diplopia and ptosis worsening at night Ascending paralysis with bladder dysfunction 2 ALS affects Anterior Horn Cells LMN and Corticospinal Tracts UMN . The hallmark is mixed UMN/LMN signs. Sensation is typically preserved. A patient with suspected Myasthenia Gravis is tested. Which antibody is most specific for this condition? Anti-Voltage Gated Calcium Channel Anti-Acetylcholine Receptor AChR Anti-Jo-1 Anti-GM1 Anti-Hu 1 AChR antibodies are pathogenic in Myasthenia Gravis Post-synaptic . Calcium channel antibodies are for Lambert-Eaton. Lambert-Eaton Myasthenic Syndrome is most commonly associated with which underlying malignancy? Thymoma Small Cell Lung Cancer Renal Cell Carcinoma Lymphoma Melanoma 1 Small Cell Lung Cancer is found in ~50% of patients with LEMS. Thymoma is associated with Myasthenia Gravis . Which feature distinguishes Polio from ALS? Polio causes LMN signs only; ALS causes UMN & LMN signs. Polio is progressive; ALS resolves spontaneously. Polio affects sensation; ALS spares sensation. Polio causes spasticity; ALS causes flaccidity. Polio is autoimmune; ALS is viral. 0 Polio destroys Anterior Horn Cells LMN only $ to$ Flaccid paralysis. ALS destroys AHCs LMN + CSTs UMN $ to$ Mixed Spastic/Flaccid. ALS Finding Mixed UMN + LMN Signs Neurology GBS CSF Finding Albuminocytologic Dissociation High Protein Normal Cells Neurology Myasthenia Gravis Mechanism Antibodies to Post-synaptic ACh Receptors Neurology Lambert-Eaton Mechanism Antibodies to Pre-synaptic Voltage-Gated Calcium Channels Neurology Lambert-Eaton Association Small Cell Lung Cancer Neurology Myasthenia Gravis Association Thymoma Neurology Botulism Presentation Descending Paralysis + Dilated Pupils Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Neuromuscular Junction Disorders FirstAid Neurology > Neuromuscular UMN vs LMN Signs UMN Spasticity Hyperreflexia Babinski + No atrophy. n LMN Flaccidity Hyporeflexia Fasciculations Atrophy. ALS Hallmark Presence of UMN and LMN signs in the same limb/segment. e.g. Atrophied muscle has a brisk reflex . Sensory is Intact . GBS vs CIDP Treatment Both get IVIG/PLEX. n GBS Steroids do NOT work. n CIDP Steroids DO work. MG vs LEMS MG Eyes Post-synaptic. Worse with use. Thymoma. n LEMS Legs Pre-synaptic Ca channels . BETTER with use. Small Cell Ca. Albuminocytologic Dissociation High Protein but Normal WBCs in CSF. Classic finding in Guillain-Barr u00e9 Syndrome ."}, {"id": "l42", "title": "Lecture 42: Diseases of Myelin", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l42 Lecture 42 Diseases of Myelin n n Lecturer M. Plummer M.D. n Session Lecture 42 n n 1. Demyelinating Diseases Myelin Destruction n Multiple Sclerosis MS n Autoimmune destruction of CNS myelin Oligodendrocytes . n Pathology Periventricular Plaques Dawson s Fingers . Reactive Gliosis. Preservation of Axons early . n CSF Oligoclonal IgG Bands Intrathecal synthesis . n Clinical Relapsing-Remitting course. Optic Neuritis Painful vision loss INO MLF lesion Lhermitte Sign Electric shock with neck flexion Uhthoff Phenomenon Heat worsens symptoms . n Epidemiology Women > Men. Far from equator Vitamin D . n Guillain-Barr u00e9 Syndrome GBS n Autoimmune destruction of PNS myelin Schwann Cells . n Pathology Segmental demyelination + Lymphocytic infiltration. n Clinical Acute Ascending Paralysis + Areflexia. n Trigger Campylobacter jejuni CMV EBV Molecular Mimicry . n CSF Albuminocytologic Dissociation High Protein Normal Cells . n Central Pontine Myelinolysis Osmotic Demyelination n Cause Rapid correction of Hyponatremia . From Low to High your Pons will Die . n Clinical Locked-In Syndrome Quadriplegia + Preserved Consciousness/Eye movements . n n 2. Dysmyelinating Diseases Leukodystrophies n Definition Genetic defects in myelin formation or turnover . n Metachromatic Leukodystrophy MLD n Deficiency Arylsulfatase A . n Accumulation Sulfatides. n Path Metachromasia change in dye color . n Krabbe Disease Globoid Cell Leukodystrophy n Deficiency Galactocerebrosidase . n Accumulation Galactosylsphingosine Psychosine - Toxic. n Path Globoid Cells Engorged Macrophages . n Adrenoleukodystrophy ALD n Genetics X-Linked Recessive ABCD1 gene . n Defect Peroxisomal transporter. n Accumulation Very Long Chain Fatty Acids VLCFA . n Clinical Adrenal Insufficiency + CNS deterioration in young boys. n n 3. Infectious Demyelination n Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy PML n Pathogen JC Virus Reactivation . n Host Immunosuppressed AIDS CD4 < 200 . n Path Lytic infection of Oligodendrocytes. Enlarged nuclei with Ground Glass viral inclusions. n Clinical Rapidly progressive multifocal deficits. Death. Lecture 42 Diseases of Myelin Neurology questions A 28-year-old female presents with sudden loss of vision in her right eye associated with pain on eye movement. MRI shows periventricular white matter plaques. Analysis of her CSF would most likely reveal Albuminocytologic Dissociation Oligoclonal IgG Bands Neutrophilic Pleocytosis Decreased Glucose Positive India Ink Stain 1 Oligoclonal Bands found in the CSF but not serum indicate intrathecal IgG production a hallmark of Multiple Sclerosis . A 45-year-old alcoholic is brought to the ER with severe hyponatremia Na = 102 . The residents rapidly correct his sodium to 135 over 6 hours. Two days later he is quadriplegic but can blink and move his eyes vertically. What happened? Stroke in the MCA territory Subarachnoid Hemorrhage Central Pontine Myelinolysis Wernicke s Encephalopathy Herniation 2 Central Pontine Myelinolysis Osmotic Demyelination Syndrome is caused by rapid correction of hyponatremia . It destroys the Pons causing Locked-In Syndrome . Which pattern of weakness is characteristic of Guillain-Barr u00e9 Syndrome? Descending paralysis with pupillary dilation Hemiparesis sparing the face Ascending flaccid paralysis with areflexia Proximal muscle weakness with preserved sensation Waxing and waning weakness improving with rest 2 GBS causes an acute Ascending paralysis toes to nose with loss of reflexes Areflexia . Adrenoleukodystrophy involves the accumulation of which substance due to a peroxisomal defect? Sphingomyelin Sulfatides Very Long Chain Fatty Acids VLCFA Galactocerebroside Copper 2 ALD is a defect in the ABCD1 transporter preventing the breakdown of Very Long Chain Fatty Acids in peroxisomes. Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy PML is caused by the reactivation of which virus in immunocompromised patients? Herpes Simplex Virus 1 HSV-1 Cytomegalovirus CMV JC Virus Epstein-Barr Virus EBV Varicella Zoster Virus VZV 2 JC Virus causes PML. Mnemonic J ohn C unningham virus destroys oligodendoctyes in AIDS . Multiple Sclerosis CSF Finding Oligoclonal IgG Bands Neurology Lhermitte Sign Electric shock down back with neck flexion MS/Spinal Cord Neurology Uhthoff Phenomenon Heat worsens MS symptoms Neurology Central Pontine Myelinolysis Cause Rapid correction of Hyponatremia Neurology Metachromatic Leukodystrophy Enzyme Arylsulfatase A Neurology Krabbe Disease Hallmark Globoid Cells Neurology Adrenoleukodystrophy Accumulation Very Long Chain Fatty Acids VLCFA Neurology PML Virus JC Virus Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Demyelinating Disorders FirstAid Neurology > Demyelinating MS Triad Charcot 1. Scanning Speech Dysarthria n2. Intention Tremor Cerebellar n3. Nystagmus INO/MLF Uhthoff s Phenomenon MS symptoms get WORSE with HEAT Hot bath exercise fever . Heat slows conduction in demyelinated axons. CPM Rule From Low to High your Pons will Die Hyponatremia -> Rapid Correction -> CPM/Locked-in . n From High to Low your Brain will Blow Hypernatremia -> Rapid Correction -> Cerebral Edema . Krabbe Findings Look for Globoid Cells Multinucleated Giant Cells and optic atrophy. Adrenoleukodystrophy Think Adrenal glands + Leuko White matter + Dystrophy Bad growth . Young boy with bronzing skin Addison s and behavioral changes."}, {"id": "l43", "title": "Lecture 43: Pharmacology of Multiple Sclerosis", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l43 Lecture 43 Pharmacology of Multiple Sclerosis n n Lecturer M. Pino Ph.D. n Session Lecture 43 n n 1. Acute Exacerbations Flare-ups n Glucocorticoids High Dose IV Methylprednisolone n Mechanism Induces Lipocortin $ to$ Inhibits Phospholipase A2 $ to$ Decreased Prostaglandins/Leukotrienes. n Use Shortens duration of acute attacks e.g. Optic Neuritis . Does NOT change long-term prognosis. n Adverse Effects HPA axis suppression Taper needed Osteoporosis Hyperglycemia Insomnia. n n 2. Disease Modifying Therapies DMTs n Interferon Beta 1a & 1b n Mechanism Cytokine JAK/STAT ; Inhibits T-cell activation/migration across BBB. n Adverse Effects Flu-like symptoms Fever chills aches Depression Liver toxicity. n Glatiramer Acetate n Mechanism Synthetic polypeptide Myelin Basic Protein decoy . Shifts immune response from Th1 Pro-inflammatory to Th2 Anti-inflammatory . n Adverse Effects Injection site reactions Post-injection systemic reaction Flushing Chest chest tightness - benign but scary . n Note Safest in Pregnancy . n Natalizumab n Mechanism Monoclonal antibody against $ alpha$4-integrin . Blocks leukocytes from binding VCAM-1 and entering the CNS. n Adverse Effects PML Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy due to JC Virus reactivation. n Fingolimod n Mechanism S1P Receptor Modulator . Sequesters lymphocytes in lymph nodes can t exit . n Adverse Effects Bradycardia Monitor EKG at first dose Macular Edema. n Dimethyl Fumarate n Mechanism Activates Nrf2 Antioxidant pathway . n Adverse Effects Flushing GI upset. n Teriflunomide n Mechanism Inhibits Pyrimidine synthesis Dihydroorotate dehydrogenase . n Adverse Effects Hepatotoxicity Teratogenicity Contraindicated in pregnancy . n Mitoxantrone n Mechanism Anthracycline analog. Intercalates DNA / Inhibits Topoisomerase II. n Use Secondary Progressive MS. n Adverse Effects Cardiotoxicity Dilated Cardiomyopathy - monitor EF Acute Myeloid Leukemia AML . Blue discoloration of urine/sclera. n Anti-CD20 Ocrelizumab Rituximab n Mechanism B-cell depletion. n Adverse Effects Infusion reactions Hepatitis B reactivation. n n 3. Symptomatic Treatment n Spasticity n Baclofen GABA-B agonist . n Tizanidine $ alpha$2-adrenergic agonist . n Walking Speed n Dalfampridine K+ Channel Blocker - Enhances conduction. n Urinary Incontinence n Oxybutynin Muscarinic Antagonist . n Botulinum Toxin Cleaves SNARE proteins . Lecture 43 Pharmacology of Multiple Sclerosis Neurology questions A 32-year-old female with Multiple Sclerosis is started on a new medication. Since starting she complains of flushed skin and profound gastrointestinal distress. Which medication mechanism is most likely responsible? Inhibition of Dihydroorotate Dehydrogenase Activation of Nrf2 pathway Sequestration of lymphocytes in lymph nodes Blockade of alpha-4 integrin Depletion of CD20 B cells 1 Dimethyl Fumarate activates the Nrf2 antioxidant pathway. Its most common side effects are Flushing and GI upset. A patient being treated for MS develops Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy PML . Which drug mechanism is associated with this complication? Interferon beta-1a Glatiramer Acetate Natalizumab Baclofen Methylprednisolone 2 Natalizumab blocks alpha-4 integrin preventing immune surveillance in the CNS allowing JC Virus reactivation PML . Which medication helps improve walking speed in MS patients by blocking potassium channels? Tizanidine Dalfampridine Oxybutynin Modafinil Amantadine 1 Dalfampridine blocks voltage-gated Potassium channels increasing action potential duration and improving conduction. Before starting a patient on Fingolimod which baseline test is most critical? Liver Function Tests Echocardiogram Electrocardiogram EKG Pulmonary Function Tests Thyroid Stimulating Hormone 2 Fingolimod can cause significant Bradycardia and heart block after the first dose. Observation and EKG monitoring are required. Which agent treats spasticity in MS by acting as an agonist at GABA-B receptors? Diazepam Dantrolene Baclofen Tizanidine Cyclobenzaprine 2 Baclofen is a GABA-B agonist used centrally for spasticity. Tizanidine is an alpha-2 agonist; Dantrolene works on muscle Ryanodine receptors . Natalizumab Mechanism Blocks Alpha-4 Integrin prevents CNS entry Pharmacology Natalizumab Serious AE PML JC Virus Pharmacology Fingolimod Mechanism S1P Receptor Modulator Sequesters lymphocytes Pharmacology Fingolimod AE Bradycardia First dose Pharmacology Teriflunomide Contraindication Pregnancy Teratogenic Pharmacology Glatiramer Acetate Mechanism Myelin Basic Protein Decoy Th1 -> Th2 Pharmacology Interferon Beta AE Flu-like symptoms Depression Pharmacology Dalfampridine Mechanism Potassium Channel Blocker Improves walking Pharmacology AMBOSS Neurology > Multiple Sclerosis > Management FirstAid Pharmacology > Neurologic PML Risk Natalizumab is highly effective but carries a risk of PML Progressive Multifocal Leukoencephalopathy if the patient is JC Virus positive. Natalizumab says NO to leukocytes entering the brain . Fingolimod & The Heart Fingolimod causes Bradycardia . You must monitor the patient s heart rate/EKG for 6 hours after the First Dose . Mitoxantrone Toxicity Like Doxorubicin Mitoxantrone is Cardiotoxic Dilated Cardiomyopathy . Must monitor Ejection Fraction. Pregnancy & MS Teriflunomide is Teratogenic . Glatiramer Acetate is generally considered the safest during pregnancy. Walking Drug Dalfampridine is the Walking Drug . It blocks Potassium Channels to prolong the action potential and improve conduction in demyelinated nerves."}, {"id": "l44", "title": "Lecture 44: Introduction to Ophthalmology", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l44 Lecture 44 Introduction to Ophthalmology n n Lecturer A. Nattis D.O. n Session Lecture 44 n n 1. The Red Eye n Conjunctivitis n Viral Pink Eye Watery discharge. Adenovirus. Highly contagious. n Bacterial Purulent pus discharge. Glued shut in AM. n Allergic Itching Pruritus . Bilateral. n Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma n Presentation Severe eye pain Headache Nausea/Vomiting Haloes around lights . n Exam Mid-dilated fixed pupil . Cloudy cornea. High IOP. Emergency . n Uveitis Iritis n Presentation Deep pain Photophobia Light sensitivity Redness. n Exam Cells and Flare in anterior chamber. Irregular pupil Synechiae . n Association Autoimmune HLA-B27 Sarcoidosis . n Scleritis n Presentation Deep boring pain. Wakes patient at night. n Association Rheumatoid Arthritis. n n 2. Neuro-Ophthalmology Pupils n Horner s Syndrome n Triad Ptosis Droopy lid Miosis Small pupil Anhidrosis No sweat . n Cause Sympathetic pathway lesion Pancoast tumor Carotid dissection . n CN III Palsy n Presentation Eye is Down and Out . Ptosis. n Pupil If Dilated Blown it s a PCom Aneurysm Surgical Emergency . If pupil spared it s Ischemic Diabetes . n Relative Afferent Pupillary Defect RAPD / Marcus Gunn Pupil n Test Swinging Flashlight Test. n Result Pupil appears to Dilate when light is swung to the bad eye because it senses less light than the good eye . n Cause Optic Nerve lesion Optic Neuritis . n Argyll Robertson Pupil n Sign Accommodates but does not react to light Prostitute s Pupil . n Cause Neurosyphilis. n n 3. Retina & Optic Nerve n Retinal Detachment n Symptoms Flashes and Floaters . Curtain coming down over vision. n Tx Surgical Emergency. n Central Retinal Artery Occlusion CRAO n Sign Cherry Red Spot on macula. Pale retina. Sudden painless vision loss. n Cause Embolus Stroke of the eye . n Central Retinal Vein Occlusion CRVO n Sign Blood and Thunder Diffuse hemorrhages . n Optic Neuritis n Sign Painful vision loss. RAPD + . Associated with MS . n Giant Cell Arteritis Temporal Arteritis n Sign Ischemic Optic Neuropathy AION . Jaw claudication. Scalp tenderness. high ESR. n Tx High Dose Steroids immediately to save the other eye. Lecture 44 Introduction to Ophthalmology Neurology questions A 65-year-old male presents with sudden painless loss of vision in his right eye. Fundoscopy reveals a pale retina with a cherry-red spot at the fovea. What is the most likely diagnosis? Central Retinal Vein Occlusion Retinal Detachment Central Retinal Artery Occlusion Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma Optic Neuritis 2 Central Retinal Artery Occlusion CRAO presents with a pale retina edema and a Cherry Red Spot thin fovea showing choroid . It is an embolic event. A patient presents with a painful red eye nausea and halos around lights. The pupil is mid-dilated and non-reactive. What is the immediate concern? Viral Conjunctivitis Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma Uveitis Scleritis Corneal Abrasion 1 Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma is an ocular emergency characterized by high pressure pain halos and a fixed mid-dilated pupil. Which finding is part of the classic triad of Horner s Syndrome? Mydriasis Dilated Pupil Exophthalmos Bulging Eye Anhidrosis Lack of sweating Diplopia Double vision Hyperhidrosis Excess sweating 2 Horner s Triad Ptosis Miosis Anhidrosis on the ipsilateral face . Does the pupil in a CN III palsy due to a PCom aneurysm typically present as dilated or constricted? Constricted Miotic Dilated Mydriatic Normal Irregular Pear-shaped 1 Parasympathetic fibers run on the outside of CN III. Compressive lesions like an PCom Aneurysm compress these fibers first causing a Dilated pupil. A patient with MS presents with pain on eye movement and decreased color vision. Swinging flashlight test shows a Relative Afferent Pupillary Defect RAPD . Diagnosis? Papilledema Optic Neuritis Glaucoma Cataract Macular Degeneration 1 Optic Neuritis is inflammation of the optic nerve common in MS. It causes painful vision loss and an RAPD . Horner s Syndrome Triad Ptosis Miosis Anhidrosis Ophthalmology Acute Angle Closure Glaucoma Symptoms Pain Haloes Nausea Fixed Mid-dilated Pupil Ophthalmology Cherry Red Spot Diagnosis Central Retinal Artery Occlusion CRAO Ophthalmology Blood and Thunder Fundus Central Retinal Vein Occlusion CRVO Ophthalmology Retinal Detachment Symptoms Flashes Floaters Curtain falling Ophthalmology CN3 Palsy Appearance Down and Out Ophthalmology Painful Vision Loss + MS Optic Neuritis Ophthalmology Painless Vision Loss + Jaw Claudication Giant Cell Arteritis AION Ophthalmology AMBOSS Ophthalmology > Red Eye FirstAid Ophthalmology Horner s vs CN3 Palsy Horner s Pupil is Small Miosis . Ptosis is mild. n CN3 Palsy Pupil is Large Dilated . Ptosis is severe Eye shut . The Painful Red Eye Sudden pain + Haloes + Hard eye = Acute Glaucoma . nPhotophobia + Autoimmune Hx = Uveitis . nContact Lens + Sleeping in them = Corneal Ulcer Pseudomonas . Retinal Detachment Warning New onset Flashes and Floaters is a Retinal Detachment until proven otherwise. Papilledema Bilateral Optic Disc Swelling due to increased Intracranial Pressure. In an obese young female think Pseudotumor Cerebri IIH . Hutchinson s Sign Shingles vesicle on the tip of the nose predicts ocular involvement Herpes Zoster Ophthalmicus ."}, {"id": "l45", "title": "Lecture 45: Structure-Function Integration to Identify Lesions", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l45 Lecture 45 Structure-Function Integration to Identify Lesions n n Lecturers J. Xie Ph.D. & K. Poole Ph.D. n Session Lecture 45 n n 1. Brainstem Vascular Syndromes n Lateral Medullary Syndrome Wallenberg n Artery PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery or Vertebral Artery. n Key Structures & Symptoms n Nucleus Ambiguus CN 9 10 Dysphagia Hoarseness Decreased Gang Reflex. Specific to Lateral . n Vestibular Nuclei Vertigo Nystagmus Vomiting. n Sympathetic Tract Horner s Syndrome Ipsilateral . n Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus Loss of Pain/Temp on Ipsilateral Face . n Spinothalamic Tract Loss of Pain/Temp on Contralateral Body . n Inferior Cerebellar Peduncle Ataxia Fall toward lesion . n Mnemonic Don t PICA horse hoarseness that can t eat dysphagia . n Medial Medullary Syndrome n Artery Anterior Spinal Artery ASA . n Key Structures & Symptoms n Corticospinal Tract Contralateral Hemiparesis Upper & Lower limbs . n Medial Lemniscus Contralateral loss of Proprioception/Vibration. n CN XII Hypoglossal Tongue deviates Toward the lesion Lick your wounds . n Medial Pontine Syndrome n Artery Paramedian branches of Basilar Artery. n Key Contralateral Hemiparesis + CN VI Abducens palsy Eye can t look out . n n 2. Cranial Nerve & Vessel Associations n CN III Oculomotor n Runs between Posterior Cerebral Artery PCA and Superior Cerebellar Artery SCA . n Susceptible to PCom Aneurysms $ to$ Dilated Pupil + Down & Out Eye. n n 3. Cerebellar Lesions n Lateral Cerebellum Hemispheres n Affects Limbs . n Signs Dysdiadochokinesia Intention Tremor Dysmetria. n Side Ipsilateral fall/ataxia. n Medial Cerebellum Vermis/Flocculonodular n Affects Trunk . n Signs Truncal Ataxia Wide-based gait Nystagmus. n Alcohol Specific damage to Vermis . n n 4. Spinal Cord Tracts Review n Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus DCML n Function Touch Vibration Proprioception. n Crossing Medulla Internal Arcuate Fibers . n Spinothalamic Tract STT n Function Pain Temperature. n Crossing Spinal Cord Anterior White Commissure 1-2 levels up . n Corticospinal Tract CST n Function Voluntary Motor. n Crossing Medulla Pyramids . Lecture 45 Structure-Function Integration to Identify Lesions Neurology questions A 65-year-old male presents with sudden onset vertigo vomiting and difficulty swallowing dysphagia . Exam shows a droopy left eyelid small left pupil loss of pain sensation on the left face and loss of pain sensation on the right body. Where is the lesion? Right Lateral Medulla Left Lateral Medulla Left Medial Medulla Right Medial Pons Left Lateral Pons 1 Left Lateral Medullary Syndrome Wallenberg Ipsilateral Horner s Ipsilateral Face sensory loss Contralateral Body sensory loss Dysphagia Nucleus Ambiguus . Lesion is on the Left . A patient presents with right-sided hemiparesis arm and leg and the tongue deviates to the left upon protrusion. Sensation of pain and temperature is intact. Which artery is likely occluded? Left PICA Right PICA Left Anterior Spinal Artery Right Anterior Spinal Artery Basilar Artery 2 Medial Medullary Syndrome Tongue deviates Toward lesion Left CN XII . Hemiparesis is Contralateral Right side . Artery is the Anterior Spinal Artery . Alcoholic cerebellar degeneration primarily affects which structure resulting in a wide-based ataxic gait? Flocculus Lateral Hemispheres Vermis Dentate Nucleus Inferior Olive 2 Chronic alcohol use causes atrophy of the Anterior Vermis leading to truncal ataxia and gait instability legs/trunk often sparing the arms. A cavernous sinus thrombosis would most likely affect which cranial nerve traveling through the sinus? Optic Nerve CN II Abducens Nerve CN VI Facial Nerve CN VII Vestibulocochlear Nerve CN VIII Hypoglossal Nerve CN XII 1 CN VI Abducens travels inside the cavernous sinus along with the Carotid Artery . CN III IV V1 V2 are in the lateral wall. CN VI is often the first affected. In Brown-S u00e9quard syndrome spinal cord hemisection pain and temperature sensation are lost Ipsilateral to the lesion at the level of the lesion Contralateral to the lesion 1-2 segments below Ipsilateral to the lesion 1-2 segments below Bilaterally at the level of the lesion only Completely preserved 1 Spinothalamic tract crosses almost immediately. A hemisection causes Contralateral loss of Pain/Temp starting 1-2 segments below the lesion. Wallenberg Syndrome Artery PICA Posterior Inferior Cerebellar Artery Neurology Wallenberg Syndrome Key Symptom Dysphagia / Hoarseness Nucleus Ambiguus Neurology Medial Medullary Artery Anterior Spinal Artery ASA Neurology Medial Medullary Key Sign Tongue deviation to lesion side + Contralateral Hemiparesis Neurology Cerebellar Vermis Lesion Truncal Ataxia Drunk walk Neurology Cerebellar Hemisphere Lesion Limb Ataxia Finger-to-Nose Neurology CN 3 Palsy + Dilated Pupil PCom Aneurysm Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Brainstem Syndromes FirstAid Neurology > Brainstem Rule of 4s Medial vs Lateral Medial structures Motor pathway CST Medial Lemniscus MLF Motor CNs 3 4 6 12 . n Lateral structures Spinothalamic Spinocerebellar Spinal Trigeminal Sympathetic Sensory CNs 8 . Wallenberg Hallmark The ONLY brainstem stroke with Dysphagia/Hoarseness Nucleus Ambiguus is Lateral Medullary PICA . Tongue Deviation CN XII lesion Tongue deviates TOWARD the side of the lesion Lick your wounds . This is the hallmark of Medial Medullary Syndrome . PCom Aneurysm A Blown Pupil Dilated with a CN III palsy is a PCom Aneurysm until proven otherwise. Ischemic CN III palsy Diabetes spares the pupil. Cerebellar Hemisphere vs Vermis Lateral = L imbs Arm/Leg ataxia . n Medial Ver M is = Midline Trunk/Gait ataxia ."}, {"id": "l46", "title": "Lecture 46: Sensory System & Special Techniques", "module": "Neurology", "content": "l46 Lecture 46 Sensory System & Special Techniques n n Lecturer R. Morgan D.O. n Session Lecture 46 n n 1. Sensory Pathways Review n Spinothalamic Tract n Modality Pain Temperature Crude Touch. n Fibers Small Unmyelinated/Thinly myelinated. n Posterior Columns DCML n Modality Vibration Proprioception Fine Touch. n Fibers Large Heavily myelinated. n Testing Always test Distal first Toes/Fingers . If normal distally proximal is likely normal. n n 2. Cortical Sensation Discriminative n Requires Intact primary sensation DCML/STT + Intact Parietal Lobe Sensory Cortex . n Stereognosis Identify common object key coin in hand with eyes closed. n Graphesthesia Identify number drawn on palm. n Two-Point Discrimination Distinguish two points vs one. n Extinction Touch both sides simultaneously. If patient only feels one side suspects contralateral parietal lesion Neglect . n n 3. Meningeal Signs Meningitis/SAH n Brudzinski Sign n Maneuver Flex patient s neck chin to chest . n Positive Involuntary flexion of Hips and Knees . n Kernig Sign n Maneuver Flex hip/knee to 90 degrees then slowly Extend the knee. n Positive Pain/Resistance in hamstring. n n 4. Special Musculoskeletal Tests n Cervical Spine Radiculopathy n Spurling Test Extend and Rotate head to affected side + Axial Compression. Positive = Radiation of pain down arm. n Distraction Test Lift head Traction . Positive = Relief of pain. n Lumbar Spine Sciatica/Disc Herniation n Straight Leg Raise SLR Passive leg raise. Positive = Radicular pain between 30-70 degrees. n Bragard s Test SLR + Dorsiflexion of foot. Increases nerve stretch. n Crossed Well SLR Raise the Unaffected leg. Positive = Pain in the Affected leg. Highly specific for Disc Herniation . n Malingering / Functional n Hoover Test Cup both heels. Ask patient to lift the paralyzed leg. n Positive for Malingering You do NOT feel downward pressure from the normal heel. Normal physiology requires pushing down with the opposite leg to lift one leg . Lecture 46 Sensory System & Special Techniques Neurology questions You are performing a neurological exam on a patient claiming to have a paralyzed right leg. You cup your hands under both heels and ask him to lift his right leg. He strains but his leg doesn t move. You feel no pressure in your left hand under his left heel. What is the interpretation? True Right Leg Paralysis Left Leg Paralysis Positive Hoover Sign Lack of Effort Sciatic Nerve Impingement Cauda Equina Syndrome 2 Positive Hoover Sign Lack of downward pressure in the contralateral normal heel indicates the patient is not making a genuine effort to lift the affected leg. Which physical exam finding is most specific for a lumbar disc herniation? Positive Straight Leg Raise Ipsilateral Positive Crossed Well Straight Leg Raise Paraspinal muscle tenderness Decreased range of motion Negative Brudzinski sign 1 While the Ipsilateral SLR is sensitive the Crossed Well Straight Leg Raise pain in the affected leg when lifting the healthy leg is highly Specific for disc herniation. A patient presents with fever and severe headache. When you flex the patient s neck their hips and knees spontaneously flex. This is a positive Kernig Sign Brudzinski Sign Babinski Sign Lhermitte Sign Hoffmann Sign 1 Brudzinski Sign Neck flexion causes involuntary hip/knee flexion. It suggests meningeal irritation Meningitis . A patient complains of shooting pain down their right arm. You extend and rotate their head to the right and apply downward pressure. This maneuver reproduces the pain. What is the test? Distraction Test Spurling Test Tinel Sign Phalen Test Lhermitte Sign 1 Spurling Test Cervical compression with extension/rotation narrows the neural foramen. Reproduction of radicular pain indicates Cervical Radiculopathy. Which sensory modality is carried by the Dorsal Columns Posterior Columns ? Pain Temperature Crude Touch Vibration and Proprioception Itch 3 Dorsal Columns Vibration Proprioception Fine Touch. Spinothalamic = Pain Temp Crude Touch . Brudzinski Sign Neck flexion -> Hip/Knee flexion Meningitis Neurology Kernig Sign Knee extension -> Pain Meningitis Neurology Spurling Test Cervical Compression -> Radicular Pain Radiculopathy Neurology Hoover Test Test for Malingering Lack of contralateral heel pressure Neurology Crossed Straight Leg Raise Lift Good Leg -> Pain in Bad Leg Specific for Disc Herniation Neurology Stereognosis Identify object in hand Parietal Cortex Neurology Graphesthesia Identify number drawn on palm Parietal Cortex Neurology AMBOSS Neurology > Neurological Examination FirstAid Neurology > Exam Hoover Test Logic In a True effort to lift one leg the other leg MUST push down. If you don t feel downward pressure from the good leg when they try to lift the bad leg they aren t really trying. BrudZinski vs Kernig B rudzinski = B rain Neck flexion causes knees to bend . n K ernig = K nee Knee extension causes pain . Spurling Test think Spurling crushes the Spine . Compression reproduces radicular pain. Distraction relieves it. Crossed SLR The Crossed Straight Leg Raise lifting the good leg causes pain in the bad leg is very Specific for a Herniated Disc. Stocking-Glove The classic pattern of Diabetic Polyneuropathy. Starts in toes/feet longest nerves and ascends. Sensory loss is symmetrical."}, {"id": "l5", "title": "Lecture #5: Clinical Applications of Neuroimaging", "module": "Neuroanatomy", "content": "l5 Lecture 5 Clinical Applications of Neuroimaging n n Lecturer S. Zakhary D.O. n Session Neuroanatomy n n 1. Computed Tomography CT Slides 5-18 n Mechanism n Uses Helical X-ray beams to measure the attenuation density of tissues. n Hounsfield Units HU The scale of density. n Hyperdense White Bone +1000 HU Acute Hemorrhage Calcifications. n Isodense Gray Brain parenchyma. n Hypodense Black Air -1000 HU Fat CSF Water. n Windows Different software settings to highlight specific tissues e.g. Bone Window for fractures Brain Window for strokes . n n Clinical Indications n Neurologic Emergencies The workhorse of the ER. n Acute Hemorrhage Stroke/Trauma First-line test. Fast <30s . n Trauma Skull fractures. n Initial Stroke To rule out hemorrhage before giving tPA. n n CT Contrast Iodinated n Uses Infection Abscess Tumors Vascular pathology CTA . n Contraindications/Risks n 1. Contrast-Induced Nephropathy CIN Check Creatinine/GFR. Hydrate. n 2. Allergy Mild hives to Anaphylaxis. Pre-medicate with Steroids/Benadryl. n 3. Metformin Risk of Lactic Acidosis . Discontinue for 48 hours. n n--- n n 2. Magnetic Resonance Imaging MRI Slides 19-27 n Mechanism n Uses strong magnetic fields to align protons. No Ionizing Radiation. n Sequences n T1 Fat is bright Anatomy . Good for structure. n T2 Water/CSF is bright Pathology . Good for Edema. n Diffusion Weighted Imaging DWI Most sensitive for Acute Ischemic Stroke shows cytotoxic edema within minutes . n n Clinical Indications n Soft Tissue Pathology Superior resolution for brain parenchyma. n Tumors Multiple Sclerosis Plaques Infections. n Posterior Fossa/Brainstem CT has bone artifact here; MRI does not. n n MRI Contrast Gadolinium n Risk Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis NSF . n Rare but serious fibrosis of skin/organs. n Contraindicated if GFR < 30 Severe Renal Failure . n n Summary CT vs. MRI n n| Feature | CT | MRI | n| --- | --- | --- | n| Speed | Fast < 1 min | Slow 30-45 mins | n| Cost | Cheaper | Expensive | n| Radiation | Yes X-ray | No | n| Bone | Excellent | Poor | n| Soft Tissue | Fair | Excellent | n| Contraindications | Pregnancy relative | Metal Implants Pacemakers | n n--- n n 3. Vascular Imaging Slides 28-38 n Carotid Duplex Ultrasound n Indication Screening for Carotid Artery Stenosis bruit TIA . n Pros Non-invasive No radiation Portable. n Cons Operator dependent. n n Conventional Angiography Digital Subtraction Angiography - DSA n Gold Standard for vascular pathology. n Procedure Catheter inserted femoral/radial -> dye injected directly into vessels. n Usage n Diagnostic Aneurysms AVMs Vasculitis. n Therapeutic Coiling aneurysms Thrombectomy clot removal . n Risks Invasive stroke risk 1% access site bleeding. n n--- n n 4. Clinical Correlates Intracranial Hemorrhages Slides 47-53 n n correlate n Epidural Hematoma n Vessel Rupture of Middle Meningeal Artery MMA branch of Maxillary . n Cause Trauma to Pterion Temple . Rarely crosses sutures. n CT Appearance Lens-shaped Biconvex hyperdensity. n Clinical Lucid Interval Knocked out -> Fine -> Rapid Decline/Death . n n n correlate n Subdural Hematoma n Vessel Rupture of Bridging Veins . n Cause Shearing forces falls in elderly alcoholics shaken baby . n CT Appearance Crescent-shaped hyperdensity. Crosses sutures. n Clinical Gradual onset Chronic or Acute. n n n correlate n Subarachnoid Hemorrhage SAH n Vessel Ruptured Berry Aneurysm Circle of Willis or Trauma. n CT Appearance Hyperdensity in Sulci and Cisterns Starburst . n Clinical Thunderclap Headache Worst headache of my life Meningismus. n n n--- n n 5. Enhancement Patterns Slide 15 n Ring-Enhancing Lesion Central necrosis with peripheral enhancement. n Differential M.A.G.I.C. D.R. n R adiation necrosis Lecture 5 Clinical Applications of Neuroimaging lecturer S. Zakhary D.O. Neuroanatomy session Lecture 5 prev l4 next l6 pdf pdfs/Lecture 5. S. Zakhary D.O. PPT .pdf Neuroimaging n CT Scan n Physics X-ray Attenuation n Uses Acute Hemorrhage Bone Trauma n Contrast Iodine Risk Allergy Kidney n MRI n Physics Magnets T1/T2 n Uses Ischemia DWI Tumors MS n Contrast Gadolinium Risk NSF n Contra Metal Pacemaker n Angiography n DSA Gold Standard Vascular n Therapeutic Coiling n Ultrasound Carotid Stenosis screening n Pathology Signs n Ring Enhancement Abscess GBM n Hyperdense CT Acute Blood questions 1. A 65-year-old male presents to the ER with sudden onset right-sided weakness and aphasia starting 30 minutes ago. What is the most appropriate INITIAL imaging modality? Non-contrast CT Head MRI Brain with Gadolinium Carotid Ultrasound Conventional Angiography Lumbar Puncture 0 From Slide 48 Non-contrast CT is the first-line study for suspected stroke Code Stroke . It is fast <30s and rules out Hemorrhage . If no blood is seen the patient may be a candidate for tPA. MRI takes too long for the initial decision. 2. A diabetic patient taking Metformin is scheduled for a CT scan with IV contrast. What is the recommended management regarding their medication? Take Metformin as usual Discontinue Metformin for 48 hours BEFORE the scan Discontinue Metformin for 48 hours AFTER the scan Double the dose of Metformin Replace Metformin with Insulin permanently 2 From Slide 46/Slide 134 Metformin must be discontinued for 48 hours after contrast administration or before in elective cases to prevent the risk of Lactic Acidosis if contrast-induced nephropathy occurs. 3. Which of the following is an absolute contraindication for an MRI of the brain? Pregnancy 1st trimester Shellfish Allergy Cardiac Pacemaker Non-compatible Renal Failure GFR < 60 Claustrophobia 2 From Slide 23/Slide 57 Metallic Implants like older Pacemakers are an absolute contraindication due to the strong magnetic field. Allergy is an issue for Contrast not the MRI itself. Renal failure is an issue for Gadolinium not the MRI itself. 4. A patient with severe renal failure GFR < 30 requires neuroimaging. You specifically avoid Gadolinium-based contrast due to the risk of which condition? Anaphylaxis Lactic Acidosis Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis NSF Acute Tubular Necrosis Stevens-Johnson Syndrome 2 From Slide 27 Gadolinium in patients with GFR < 30 is associated with Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis NSF a rare but serious scleroderma-like fibrosing disease. 5. Ring- Enhancing Lesion on a contrast CT or MRI is classically associated with which of the following pathologies? Multiple Sclerosis Brain Abscess Ischemic Stroke Acute Meningioma Subdural Hematoma 1 From Slide 15 The classic differential for a Ring-Enhancing Lesion is Abscess or Glioblastoma/Metastasis . Meningiomas typically show diffuse homogeneous enhancement dural tail . First-line imaging for acute stroke? Non-contrast CT Head Radiology What does hyperdense white material on CT usually represent? Bone or Acute Blood Radiology Risk of Gadolinium in renal failure? Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis NSF Pathology Classic appearance of Abscess on contrast MRI? Ring-Enhancing Lesion Radiology Contraindication for MRI? Metal Implants Pacemaker Safety Best modality for Acute Ischemic Stroke most sensitive ? Diffusion Weighted MRI DWI Radiology Metformin management with Contrast? Hold for 48 hours Risk of Lactic Acidosis Pharm Appearance of Meningioma with contrast? Diffuse enhancement + Dural Tail Radiology Gold standard for Aneurysm/AVM? Conventional Angiography DSA Radiology Mechanism of CT? X-ray attenuation Hounsfield Units Physics FirstAid Neurology > Radiology Code Stroke Imaging Non-contrast CT is the first-line radiologic intervention for suspected stroke to rule out hemorrhage. CT Density Rule Hyperdense White = Bone Acute Blood Calcification. n Hypodense Black = Air Fat Water CSF. Metformin & Contrast Metformin must be withheld for 48 hours after iodinated contrast administration to prevent Lactic Acidosis . MRI Safety Strong magnetic fields can dislodge ferromagnetic objects. Pacemakers and metallic implants are absolute contraindications. Gadolinium & Renal Failure Avoid Gadolinium in patients with GFR < 30 due to the risk of Nephrogenic Systemic Fibrosis NSF . Ring-Enhancing Lesion Classic Differential MAGIC DR Metastasis Abscess Glioblastoma Infarct Contusion Demyelination Radiation."}, {"id": "l6", "title": "Lecture #6: Anatomy of the Spinal Cord", "module": "Neuroanatomy", "content": "l6 Lecture 6 Anatomy of the Spinal Cord n n Lecturer J. Xie Ph.D. n Session Neuroanatomy n n 1. Gross Anatomy of the Spinal Cord Slides 5-8 nThe spinal cord is a cylindrical structure that serves as the main information superhighway between the brain and the body. It is flattened dorsoventrally and resides within the protective vertebral column. nCrucially the spinal cord is shorter than the vertebral column. In adults it ends at the L1-L2 vertebral level forming a tapered tip called the Conus Medullaris . Below this level the spinal canal contains a collection of nerve roots called the Cauda Equina horse s tail floating in the Lumbar Cistern of CSF. n n 2. Meninges and Vascular Supply Slides 10-11 40-41 nThe spinal cord is wrapped in three meningeal layers n1. Dura Mater The tough outer layer. Unlike the cranial dura which is double-layered the spinal dura is single-layered . Between the dura and the vertebrae lies the Epidural Space a real space containing fat and veins Venous Plexus of Batson . n2. Arachnoid Mater The middle spider-web-like layer. n3. Pia Mater The delicate inner layer attached directly to the cord. It forms lateral projections called Denticulate Ligaments that anchor the cord to the dura. n n Blood Supply nThe cord is supplied by one Anterior Spinal Artery ASA and two Posterior Spinal Arteries PSA . n ASA Supplies the anterior 2/3 of the cord including the Ventral Horns/Motor and STT/Pain . n PSA Supplies the posterior 1/3 Dorsal Columns/Sensory . n n correlate n Anterior Spinal Artery ASA Syndrome n- Pathophysiology Infarction of the ASA often from aortic surgery or occlusion . n- Presentation Sudden onset of complete motor paralysis Corticospinal tract and loss of pain/temperature sensation Spinothalamic tract below the lesion. n- Key Distinction Proprioception and Vibration are SPARED because the Dorsal Columns are supplied by the Posterior Spinal Arteries. n n n 3. Internal Topography and Tracts Slides 29-39 nThe internal structure consists of a central H-shaped butterfly of Gray Matter cell bodies surrounded by White Matter axons . n n The Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus DCML Pathway nThis ascending pathway conveys fine touch vibration and proprioception . nPrimary afferents enter the cord and ascend ipsilaterally in the Dorsal Columns Fasciculus Gracilis for legs Cuneatus for arms . They do not synapse until they reach the Medulla Nucleus Gracilis/Cuneatus . There they decussate Internal Arcuate Fibers and ascend to the Thalamus. n n The Spinothalamic Tract STT - Anterolateral System nThis pathway conveys pain temperature and crude touch . nPrimary afferents enter via Lissauer s Tract and synapse immediately in the Dorsal Horn Substantia Gelatinosa . The second-order neurons decussate immediately via the Anterior White Commissure and ascend contralaterally. n n The Corticospinal Tract CST nThis is the primary descending motor pathway for voluntary movement. nFibers originate in the Motor Cortex descend through the brainstem and decussate in the Medulla Pyramids . They descend in the spinal cord as the Lateral Corticospinal Tract and synapse on Anterior Horn Cells Lower Motor Neurons . n n 4. Spinal Reflexes Slides 23-27 nReflexes are involuntary motor responses to stimuli mediated by local spinal circuits. n n Stretch Reflex Myotatic nThis is the simplest reflex e.g. Knee Jerk . It is Monosynaptic . n- Mechanism Muscle stretch stimulates specific sensory receptors Muscle Spindles -> Ia afferent fires -> Direct synapse on Alpha Motor Neuron -> Muscle contracts. n- Function Maintains muscle tone and posture. n n Flexor Withdrawal & Crossed-Extensor Reflexes nThese are Polysynaptic . n- Flexor Withdrawal A painful stimulus invokes pulling the limb away Flexion . n- Crossed-Extensor Simultaneously the opposite limb extends to support body weight preventing falling . n n correlate n Tetanus n- Pathophysiology Clostridium tetani toxin Tetanospasmin travels retrogradely from a wound to the spinal cord. It cleaves SNARE proteins in Renshaw Cells inhibitory interneurons . n- Mechanism Prevents release of inhibitory neurotransmitters GABA and Glycine . n- Presentation Unchecked excitation of motor neurons leads to spastic paralysis lockjaw trismus and opisthotonos arching back . n n n 5. Clinical Correlates Additional n n correlate n Shingles Herpes Zoster n- Pathophysiology Reactivation of Varicella Zoster Virus VZV which lies dormant in the Dorsal Root Ganglia DRG . n- Presentation Painful vesicular rash in a specific dermatomal distribution does not cross midline . n n n correlate n Disc Herniation Slides 14-15 n- Pathophysiology The soft Nucleus Pulposus herniates through a tear in the tough Annulus Fibrosus . n- Location Posterolateral herniation is most common and typically compresses the spinal nerve root below the level of the disc e.g. L4/L5 disc compresses L5 root . n- Presentation Radiculopathy shooting pain sensory loss and weakness in the affected nerve distribution. n Lecture 6 Anatomy of the Spinal Cord lecturer J. Xie Ph.D. Neuroanatomy session Lecture 6 prev l5 next l7 pdf pdfs/Lecture 6. J. Xie Ph.D. PPT .pdf Spinal Cord n Tracts n Ascending Sensory n DCML Touch/Prop. Ipsilateral in cord -> Decussates Medulla n STT Pain/Temp. Contralateral in cord -> Decussates Cord n Descending Motor n CST Motor Ipsilateral in cord -> Decussates Medulla n Reflexes n Stretch Monosynaptic DTRs n Withdrawal Polysynaptic Pain n Pathology n ASA Syndrome Spares Dorsal Columns n Brown-Sequard Hemi-section n Ipsi Motor Proprioception n Contra Pain/Temp n Tetanus Blocks Inhibition questions 1. A patient presents with loss of pain and temperature sensation in the right leg and spastic paralysis and loss of vibration sense in the left leg. This Dissociated Sensory Loss pattern Brown-S u00e9quard Syndrome is caused by a hemisection lesion on which side of the spinal cord? Right side Ipsilateral to pain loss Left side Ipsilateral to motor/vibration loss Bilateral central lesion Dorsal Columns only Anterior Horn only 1 Brown-S u00e9quard Hemisection <br>- Ipsilateral signs Motor CST decussated above + Vibration DCML decussates above .<br>- Contralateral signs Pain/Temp STT decussates immediately . <br>Since motor/vibration loss is on the Left the lesion is on the Left . 2. Tetanus toxin causes spastic paralysis by blocking the release of inhibitory neurotransmitters GABA and Glycine from which specific spinal cord cells? Alpha Motor Neurons Gamma Motor Neurons Renshaw Cells Dorsal Root Ganglion Cells Clarke s Nucleus Cells 2 From Slide 22 Tetanospasmin blocks the release of GABA/Glycine from Renshaw Cells inhibitory interneurons leading to unchecked excitation tetany . 3. An infarction of the Anterior Spinal Artery ASA would result in all of the following deficits below the lesion level EXCEPT Loss of pain sensation STT Loss of temperature sensation STT Spastic Paralysis CST Loss of vibration and proprioception DCML Loss of crude touch 3 From Slide 41 The ASA supplies the anterior 2/3 of the cord STT CST . The Dorsal Columns DCML are supplied by the Posterior Spinal Arteries and are SPARED in ASA syndrome. 4. Which spinal tract carries fine touch and proprioception and ascends ipsilaterally within the spinal cord? Lateral Corticospinal Tract Spinothalamic Tract Dorsal Column Medial Lemniscus DCML Rubrospinal Tract Vestibulospinal Tract 2 The DCML enters the cord and ascends in the posterior columns on the SAME side ipsilateral until it reaches the medulla where it decussates. 5. In the stretch reflex e.g. Knee Jerk the primary afferent neuron synapses directly onto the alpha-motor neuron. This type of reflex is classified as Polysynaptic Disynaptic Monosynaptic Intersegmental Contralateral 2 From Slide 24 The stretch reflex is the simplest reflex and is Monosynaptic one synapse between sensory and motor neuron . Function of Dorsal Column Medial Lemniscus DCML ? Fine Touch Vibration Proprioception Neuro Function of Spinothalamic Tract STT ? Pain and Temperature Neuro Function of Corticospinal Tract CST ? Voluntary Motor Control Neuro Where does DCML decussate? Medulla Internal Arcuate Fibers Neuro Where does STT decussate? Spinal Cord Anterior White Commissure Neuro Where does CST decussate? Medulla Pyramids Neuro What is spard in Anterior Spinal Artery Syndrome? Dorsal Columns Proprioception/Vibration Pathology Virus dormant in DRG causing Shingles? Varicella Zoster Virus VZV Micro Toxin blocking Renshaw Cells GABA/Glycine ? Tetanus Toxin Micro Reflex level for Patellar Knee Jerk? L3-L4 Clinical BandB Neurology > Somatosensory Systems Lumbar Puncture LP Because the spinal cord ends at L1-L2 a Lumbar Puncture is safely performed at the L3-L4 or L4-L5 interspace to avoid damaging the cord. Vascular Watershed The mid-thoracic region T4-T8 is a vascular watershed area and is most susceptible to infarction during hypotension. DCML Rule Lesions in the spinal cord affect the DCML Ipsilaterally because it doesn t cross until the medulla . STT Rule Lesions in the spinal cord affect the STT Contralaterally typically 1-2 segments below the lesion due to Lissauer s tract ascent . CST Rule Lesions in the spinal cord cause Ipsilateral Spastic Paralysis UMN signs below the lesion because it already crossed in the medulla . Renshaw Cells Renshaw cells are the brakes of the motor system. Without them e.g. Tetanus toxin blockade motor neurons fire uncontrollably."}, {"id": "l7", "title": "Lecture #7: Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem I", "module": "Neuroanatomy", "content": "l7 Lecture 7 Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem I n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D. n Session Neuroanatomy n n 1. Overview of Brainstem Divisions Slides 4-5 nThe brainstem is divided into three major regions Rostral to Caudal n1. Midbrain Mesencephalon The most superior portion. n2. Pons Metencephalon The middle bulbous portion. n3. Medulla Myelencephalon The most inferior portion continuous with the spinal cord. n n 2. External Anatomy Ventral View Slide 6 n Midbrain Characterized by the Cerebral Peduncles Crus Cerebri massive stalks containing descending motor fibers from the cortex. n Pons Characterized by the Basilar Pons a bulbous structure with transverse fibers. n Medulla n Pyramids Two medial columns containing the Corticospinal Tract . n Pyramidal Decussation The crossing point of motor fibers at the caudal medulla border with spinal cord . n Olive A lateral bump produced by the Inferior Olivary Nucleus motor coordination . n n 3. External Anatomy Dorsal View Slide 7-8 n Midbrain Tectum n Superior Colliculi Visual reflexes/saccades. n Inferior Colliculi Auditory pathway. n Pons n Cerebellar Peduncles n Superior SCP Main output from cerebellum. n Middle MCP Connects Pons to Cerebellum massive input . n Inferior ICP Connects Medulla to Cerebellum. n Floor of 4th Ventricle The open posterior aspect. n Medulla n Gracile Tubercle Medial bump Leg sensory . n Cuneate Tubercle Lateral bump Arm sensory . n Obex The caudal point where the 4th ventricle closes. n n 4. Internal Anatomy Cross-Sectional Zones Slide 10 nIn cross-section the brainstem is divided anterior-to-posterior n1. Basilar Region Ventral Contains descending motor tracts Corticospinal Corticobulbar . n2. Tegmentum Middle The core containing Cranial Nerve Nuclei Reticular Formation and sensory tracts. n3. Tectum Dorsal Found only in the Midbrain Colliculi . n n 5. Key Nuclei by Level Slides 15-26 n n Medulla n Gracile & Cuneate Nuclei Dorsal column nuclei Touch/Proprioception . n Inferior Olivary Nucleus Large crumpled sac appearance. Motor learning Climbing fibers to cerebellum . n Spinal Trigeminal Nucleus Pain/Temp for the face extends down to C2 . n n Pons n Pontine Nuclei Scattered in the basilar pons. Relay cortex information to the cerebellum via the MCP. n Facial Nucleus CN VII Motor to face. n Abducens Nucleus CN VI Lateral eye movement. n n Midbrain n Substantia Nigra Dark pigmented line Dopamine . Degenerates in Parkinson s. n Red Nucleus Motor coordination Rubrospinal tract . n Periaqueductal Gray PAG Pain modulation Opioid receptors . n Superior & Inferior Colliculi. n n correlate n Cranial Nerve Exit Points Rule of 4s n Midbrain CN 3 4 Oculomotor interpeduncular fossa Trochlear dorsal exit! . n Pons CN 5 6 7 8 Trigeminal lateral pons Abducens/Facial/Vestibulocochlear pontomedullary junction . n Medulla CN 9 10 11 12 Glossopharyngeal/Vagus/Accessory retro-olivary Hypoglossal pre-olivary sulcus . n Lecture 7 Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem I lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D. Neuroanatomy session Lecture 7 prev l6 next l8 pdf pdfs/Lecture 7. I. Kurtzer Ph.D. PPT .pdf Brainstem Anatomy I n Midbrain n Ventral Cerebral Peduncles Motor n Dorsal Tectum Sup/Inf Colliculi n Internal Aqueduct Substantia Nigra Red Nucleus n CN III IV n Pons n Ventral Basilar Pons Transverse fibers n Dorsal 4th Ventricle Floor n Internal Pontine Nuclei MCP n CN V VI VII VIII n Medulla n Ventral Pyramids Olive n Dorsal Gracile/Cuneate Tubercles n Internal Inf. Olive DC Nuclei n CN IX X XI XII questions 1. Which structure is a hallmark feature of the dorsal midbrain? Pyramids Inferior Olive Superior Colliculus Fourth Ventricle Gracile Tubercle 2 The Superior Colliculus is part of the Tectum found on the dorsal aspect of the midbrain. The Pyramids and Olive are ventral medulla structures. 2. The pyramidal decussation marks the caudal boundary of which brainstem division? Midbrain Pons Medulla Diencephalon Spinal Cord 2 The Pyramidal Decussation is found at the junction of the Medulla and the spinal cord. 3. Which cranial nerve is the only one to exit from the dorsal aspect of the brainstem? CN III Oculomotor CN IV Trochlear CN V Trigeminal CN VI Abducens CN VII Facial 1 CN IV Trochlear exits dorsally from the midbrain tectum. All other cranial nerves exit ventrally or laterally. 4. Damage to the Substantia Nigra in the midbrain is most commonly associated with which disorder? Huntington s Disease Alzheimer s Disease Parkinson s Disease Multiple Sclerosis Myasthenia Gravis 2 The Substantia Nigra contains dopaminergic neurons. Their degeneration leads to the motor symptoms of Parkinson s Disease . 5. Which ventricular space is located between the Pons/Medulla and the Cerebellum? Lateral Ventricle Third Ventricle Cerebral Aqueduct Fourth Ventricle Central Canal 3 The Fourth Ventricle is the open space bounded anteriorly by the Pons/Medulla and posteriorly by the Cerebellum. Three divisions of Brainstem Rostral->Caudal ? Midbrain -> Pons -> Medulla Anatomy Hallmark dorsal structure of Midbrain? Tectum Corpora Quadrigemina / Colliculi Anatomy Ventral columns of Medulla contain? Corticospinal Tract Pyramids Anatomy Only CN to exit dorsally? CN IV Trochlear Anatomy What marks the Medulla-Spinal Cord border? Pyramidal Decussation Anatomy Function of Inferior Colliculus? Auditory Processing Physiology Function of Superior Colliculus? Visual/Saccadic Eye Movements Physiology Function of Substantia Nigra? Dopamine production Motor Control Physiology Nucleus responsible for Olive bump? Inferior Olivary Nucleus Anatomy Ventricular space of the Midbrain? Cerebral Aqueduct Anatomy Tectum Location The Tectum Roof exists ONLY in the Midbrain . It consists of the Superior and Inferior Colliculi. Cerebral Aqueduct The Cerebral Aqueduct is a hallmark of the Midbrain . The 4th Ventricle is associated with the Pons and Medulla. Decussation Rule The Pyramidal Decussation crossing of motor fibers marks the transition from Medulla to Spinal Cord. CN IV Uniqueness The Trochlear Nerve CN IV is the ONLY cranial nerve to exit from the Dorsal aspect of the brainstem. Inferior Olive Function The Inferior Olive Medulla is a MAJOR source of input to the cerebellum Climbing Fibers for motor learning. Substantia Nigra Located in the Midbrain cerebral peduncles. Contains Dopaminergic neurons. Loss = Parkinson s Disease."}, {"id": "l8", "title": "Lecture 8: Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem II - Major Tracts and Cranial Nerve Nuclei", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l8 Lecture 8 Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem II - Major Tracts and Cranial Nerve Nuclei n n Lecturer I. Kurtzer Ph.D. n Session Lecture 8 n n 1. Major Tracts of the Brainstem Slide 3-17 n nThe brainstem serves as a major conduit for ascending and descending pathways connecting the spinal cord cerebellum and cerebrum. Understanding the relative positions of these tracts at different levels Midbrain Pons Medulla is crucial for localizing lesions. n n Descending Tracts n Corticospinal Tract n Function Voluntary motor control. n Pathway Descends from the cerebral cortex. n Trajectory It is always the most ventral structure throughout the brainstem. n Decussation Most fibers decussate at the caudal medulla pyramidal decussation to form the lateral corticospinal tract in the spinal cord. n n Ascending Tracts n Dorsal Column-Medial Lemniscus DCML n Function Fine touch vibration and proprioception for the body. n Pathway n Spinal Cord Ascends centrally as the Dorsal Columns Gracile and Cuneate fasciculi . n Medulla Synapses in the Dorsal Column Nuclei Gracile and Cuneate nuclei . n Decussation Axons form Internal Arcuate Fibers which decussate in the caudal medulla Slide 16 . n Brainstem Ascent Ascends as the Medial Lemniscus . n Trajectory It is always medial to the spinothalamic tract. Its orientation changes from vertical medulla to horizontal midbrain as it ascends. n Spinothalamic Tract Anterolateral System n Function Pain and temperature sensation. n Pathway Decussates immediately in the spinal cord. n Trajectory Ascends through the brainstem in a lateral position. It remains lateral to the DCML at all levels Slide 17 . n n 2. Cerebellar Peduncles Slide 18-20 n nThe cerebellum communicates with the brainstem via three paired peduncles located at specific levels n n Inferior Cerebellar Peduncle ICP n Level Medulla and lower Pons. n Function Primarily afferent input to the cerebellum from the spinal cord e.g. dorsal spinocerebellar tract and medulla. n Middle Cerebellar Peduncle MCP n Level Pons. n Function Entirely afferent input from the pontine nuclei corticopontine pathway . It is the largest peduncle. n Superior Cerebellar Peduncle SCP n Level Upper Pons and lower Midbrain. n Function Primarily efferent output from the deep cerebellar nuclei to the red nucleus and thalamus. n n 3. Cranial Nerve Nuclei Organization Slide 22-26 n nThe organization of cranial nerve nuclei follows a rational developmental logic based on the neural tube plates alar = sensory basal = motor . n n Functional Columns nNuclei are arranged in longitudinal columns. A general rule for the brainstem is Motor is Medial Sensory is Lateral . n n Motor Columns Medial to Lateral n 1. Somatic Motor GSE Most medial near the midline. Innervates myotome-derived muscles tongue eye . n Nuclei Oculomotor III Trochlear IV Abducens VI Hypoglossal XII . n 2. Branchial Motor SVE Ventrolateral. Innervates pharyngeal arch muscles. n Nuclei Trigeminal Motor V Facial Motor VII Nucleus Ambiguus IX X Spinal Accessory XI . n 3. Visceral Motor GVE Parasympathetic. n Nuclei Edinger-Westphal III Superior Salivatory VII Inferior Salivatory IX Dorsal Motor Nucleus of Vagus X . n n Sensory Columns Medial to Lateral n 1. Visceral Sensory GVA/SVA n Nuclei Nucleus Solitarius VII IX X - taste and cardiorespiratory . n 2. Somatic Sensory GSA General sensation from face. n Nuclei Trigeminal Nuclei Mesencephalic Pontine Spinal . n 3. Special Sensory SSA Most lateral. n Nuclei Vestibular and Cochlear Nuclei VIII . n n mnemonic n Cranial Nerves Sensory/Motor/Both n Some Say Marry Money But My Brother Says Big Brains Matter More n n S ome I - Sensory n S ay II - Sensory n M arry III - Motor n M oney IV - Motor n B ut V - Both n M y VI - Motor n B rother VII - Both n S ays VIII - Sensory n B ig IX - Both n B rains X - Both n M atter XI - Motor n M ore XII - Motor n Lecture 8 Internal Anatomy of Brain Stem II - Major Tracts and Cranial Nerve Nuclei Neuroscience questions A patient presents with loss of vibration and proprioception on the left side of the body. A lesion is located in the medulla. Which structure is most likely affected? Left Nucleus Gracilis/Cuneate Right Medial Lemniscus Left Medial Lemniscus Right Spinothalamic Tract Left Lateral Corticospinal Tract 1 The DCML decussates in the caudal medulla as internal arcuate fibers. After decussation the fibers ascend as the Medial Lemniscus on the contralateral side. Therefore a lesion in the medulla affecting the Right Medial Lemniscus would cause loss of vibration/proprioception on the Left side. Which cranial nerve nucleus column is located most laterally in the brainstem? Somatic Motor Branchial Motor Visceral Motor Somatic Sensory Special Sensory 4 Following the Motor Medial Sensory Lateral rule the Special Sensory column hearing and balance CN VIII is the most lateral column. The Middle Cerebellar Peduncle conveys fibers primarily from which location? Spinal Cord Medulla Pons Midbrain Deep Cerebellar Nuclei 2 The Middle Cerebellar Peduncle MCP connects the Pons to the Cerebellum and conveys afferent fibers from pontine nuclei. Where does the Corticospinal tract decussate? Midbrain Upper Pons Lower Pons Upper Medulla Caudal Medulla 4 The Corticospinal tract descends ventrally and decussates at the Pyramids in the Caudal lower Medulla. Which nucleus provides parasympathetic innervation to the pupil constriction ? Oculomotor Nucleus Edinger-Westphal Nucleus Trochlear Nucleus Abducens Nucleus Superior Salivatory Nucleus 1 The Edinger-Westphal nucleus is the Visceral Motor parasympathetic component of CN III Oculomotor responsible for pupil constriction. Decussation level of Corticospinal Tract Caudal Medulla pyramids Neuroanatomy Decussation level of DCML Caudal Medulla internal arcuate fibers Neuroanatomy Decussation level of Spinothalamic Tract Spinal Cord anterior white commissure Neuroanatomy Relative position of DCML vs Spinothalamic in brainstem DCML is always MEDIAL to Spinothalamic Neuroanatomy Brainstem level of Inferior Cerebellar Peduncle Medulla and Lower Pons Neuroanatomy Brainstem level of Middle Cerebellar Peduncle Pons Neuroanatomy Brainstem level of Superior Cerebellar Peduncle Upper Pons and Lower Midbrain Neuroanatomy General rule for CN Nuclei location Medial-Lateral Motor is Medial Sensory is Lateral Neuroanatomy Nucleus responsible for taste CN VII IX X Nucleus Solitarius Gustatory nucleus Neuroanatomy Nucleus supplying pharyngeal muscles swallowing Nucleus Ambiguus CN IX X XI Neuroanatomy BandB Neurology > Brainstem > Brainstem Anatomy FirstAid Neurology > Anatomy > Brainstem Pathoma CNS Tract Trajectories Rule The Corticospinal tract is always the most ventral structure. The DCML is always medial to the Spinothalamic tract. The Spinothalamic tract is always lateral . Medial vs. Lateral Rule In the brainstem cranial nerve nuclei organization Motor is Medial Sensory is Lateral . Decussation Levels Corticospinal Caudal Medulla Pyramids . n DCML Caudal Medulla Internal Arcuate Fibers . n Spinothalamic Spinal Cord immediately on entry . Peduncle Levels Inferior Medulla/Lower Pons Afferent . n Middle Pons Afferent . n Superior Upper Pons/Midbrain Efferent . Nucleus Ambiguus The Nucleus Ambiguus supplies branchial motor innervation to the pharynx and larynx swallowing gag reflex via CN IX and X."}, {"id": "l9", "title": "Lecture 9: Reticular Formation", "module": "Neuroscience", "content": "l9 Lecture 9 Reticular Formation n n Lecturer J. Xie Ph.D. n Session Lecture 9 n n 1. Reticular Formation Overview Slide 6-7 n nThe Reticular Formation RF is a phylogenetically old set of nuclei located throughout the brainstem core extending from the caudal medulla to the lateral hypothalamus. It is not a single anatomical structure but a diffuse network responsible for life-sustaining functions. n n Structure Composed of short interneurons reflexes and long projection neurons neuromodulation . n Key Concept A single neuron can synapse with 25 000+ others allowing diffuse modulation of the entire CNS. n n 2. Neurochemistry of the Reticular Formation Slide 8-13 n nThe RF houses the nuclei for major neuromodulatory systems. You MUST know the location and neurotransmitter for each n n Serotonin 5-HT n Nuclei Raphe Nuclei midline of entire brainstem . n Functions Mood Arousal Pain modulation Raphe Magnus Sleep. n Norepinephrine NE n Nuclei Locus Coeruleus pons/midbrain junction and Lateral Tegmental Area. n Functions Arousal RAS Attention Fight or Flight Pain modulation. n Dopamine DA n Nuclei Ventral Tegmental Area VTA and Substantia Nigra Midbrain . n Pathways n Nigrostriatal Coordination of movement degenerate in Parkinson s . n Mesolimbic Reward/Addiction Schizophrenia positive symptoms . n Mesocortical Cognition Schizophrenia negative symptoms . n Acetylcholine ACh n Nuclei Pedunculopontine Tegmental Nucleus PPT Brainstem and Nucleus Basalis of Meynert Forebrain . n Functions Arousal REM sleep generation Memory degenerate in Alzheimer s . n Histamine n Nuclei Tuberomammillary Nucleus Posterior Hypothalamus . n Function Arousal/Wakefulness Antihistamines cause drowsiness . n n 3. Reticular Activating System ARAS & Consciousness Slide 16-24 n nThe Ascending Reticular Activating System ARAS projects to the cortex to maintain Wakefulness and Alertness . n n Disorders of Consciousness n Coma Low arousal no awareness. Dysfunction of ARAS or bilateral cortex. n Persistent Vegetative State Unresponsive Wakefulness . Arousal is present eyes open sleep-wake cycles but NO awareness of environment. n Minimally Conscious State Intermittent inconsistent awareness. n n correlate n Locked-in Syndrome n Pathophysiology Ischemic stroke of the Ventral Pons Basilar Artery . n Presentation Quadriplegia corticospinal disruption and anarthria corticobulbar disruption . n Key Feature The ARAS is spared tegmentum so the patient is Fully Awake and Aware but cannot move. n Communication Vertical eye movements controlled by midbrain and blinking are usually preserved. n n n 4. Sleep Physiology Slide 25-33 n n Stages n N1-N2 Light sleep Sleep Spindles K-Complexes . n N3 Slow Wave Sleep Delta waves - Deep restorative sleep. n REM Rapid Eye Movement dreaming atonia. EEG looks like Awake Beta/Sawtooth . n Neurochemistry n Wakefulness Promoters NE 5-HT Histamine ACh Hypocretin Orexin . n Sleep Promoters GABA VLPO nucleus and Melatonin. n n correlate n Narcolepsy n Pathophysiology Loss of Hypocretin Orexin neurons in the lateral hypothalamus. n Presentation n Excessive Daytime Sleepiness. n Cataplexy Sudden loss of muscle tone triggered by emotion. n Sleep Paralysis and Hypnagogic Hallucinations . n Treatment Stimulants Amphetamines to increase NE/DA. n n n 5. Descending Motor & Pain Control Slide 37-39 n n Motor Rigidity Posturing n Decorticate Posturing Flexion of upper limbs. Lesion Above Red Nucleus midbrain . Rubrospinal tract intact. n Decerebrate Posturing Extension of all limbs. Lesion Below Red Nucleus . Vestibulospinal tracts dominate. Worse prognosis. n n Descending Pain Modulation nThe brainstem can inhibit incoming pain signals Pain Inhibits Pain . n Pathway Periaqueductal Gray PAG $ to$ Rostral Ventromedial Medulla RVM Raphe Magnus $ to$ Dorsal Horn block pain transmission via Enkephalins/Serotonin/NE . n Clinical Antidepressants like SnRIs Duloxetine or TCAs Amitriptyline treat chronic pain by boosting NE/5-HT in this inhibitory pathway. Lecture 9 Reticular Formation Neuroscience questions A patient presents with locked-in syndrome following a stroke. They are fully aware but cannot move their limbs or speak. Which structure is most likely spared preserving their consciousness? Basis Pontis Corticospinal Tracts Reticular Activating System Tegmentum Medullary Pyramids Internal Capsule 2 Locked-in syndrome is caused by a lesion to the ventral pons basis pontis destroying corticospinal and corticobulbar tracts. Consciousness is preserved because the Reticular Activating System ARAS located in the dorsal tegmentum is spared. Which neurotransmitter is synthesized primarily in the Locus Coeruleus? Serotonin Dopamine Acetylcholine Norepinephrine Histamine 3 The Locus Coeruleus is the primary site of Norepinephrine NE synthesis in the brainstem involved in arousal and attention. A comatose patient exhibits abnormal posturing with extension of both upper and lower extremities to painful stimuli. This Decerebrate rigidity suggests a lesion at which level? Cortex Above the Red Nucleus At or Below the Red Nucleus Spinal Cord Thalamus 2 Decerebrate rigidity extension of all limbs implies a lesion at or below the Red Nucleus midbrain/upper pons removing rubrospinal influence and leaving vestibulospinal extension unchecked. A 25-year-old male complains of excessive daytime sleepiness and episodes of sudden muscle weakness when laughing cataplexy . A deficiency in which neuropeptide is likely? Serotonin GABA Hypocretin Orexin Dopamine Melatonin 2 Narcolepsy with cataplexy is classically caused by a loss of Hypocretin Orexin neurons in the lateral hypothalamus. Which drug class treats chronic neuropathic pain by enhancing the descending pain inhibitory pathway from the brainstem? SSRIs Fluoxetine Benzodiazepines SNRIs Duloxetine Dopamine Agonists Antihistamines 2 SNRIs like Duloxetine and TCAs are effective for neuropathic pain because they inhibit reuptake of Norepinephrine and Serotonin enhancing the descending inhibitory signals from the RVM/PAG to the spinal cord. Location of Serotonin 5-HT cell bodies Raphe Nuclei Neurochemistry Location of Norepinephrine NE cell bodies Locus Coeruleus Neurochemistry Location of Dopamine DA cell bodies Midbrain Substantia Nigra & Ventral Tegmental Area VTA Neurochemistry Location of Histamine cell bodies Tuberomammillary Nucleus Hypothalamus Neurochemistry Lesion location for Decorticate Rigidity Above the Red Nucleus Midbrain Neurology Lesion location for Decerebrate Rigidity At or Below the Red Nucleus Neurology Which is worse Decorticate or Decerebrate? Decerebrate indicates deeper brainstem damage Neurology Neurotransmitter deficiency in Narcolepsy Hypocretin Orexin Neurology Area spared in Locked-in Syndrome Reticular Activating System Tegmentum & Vertical Eye Movements Neurology Structure responsible for Descending Pain Modulation Periaqueductal Gray PAG Neurology BandB Neurology > Brainstem > Brainstem Anatomy FirstAid Neurology > Pathology > Coma & Sleep Pathoma CNS Decorticate vs. Decerebrate Decorticate Flexor posturing Hands to C ore . Lesion Above Red Nucleus. n Decerebrate Extensor posturing worse . Lesion Below Red Nucleus. Locating Neurotransmitters Raphe Nuclei = Serotonin 5-HT . n Locus Coeruleus = Norepinephrine NE . n Substantia Nigra = Dopamine DA . n Tuberomammillary Nucleus = Histamine. Locked-in Syndrome Caused by a Ventral Pontine stroke Basilar artery . The ARAS is spared Tegmentum so the patient is fully awake/aware but quadriplegic. Can only blink or move eyes vertically. Narcolepsy Mechanism Caused by a deficiency of Hypocretin Orexin producing neurons in the Lateral Hypothalamus . Pain Modulation Pathway PAG Midbrain $ to$ RVM Medulla $ to$ Spinal Cord Dorsal Horn. Mediated by Opioids Serotonin and NE."}];